var tafsirData_4 = {
  "1": "“O humanity!” opens a new chapter by speaking to every human being, not only to the believers who were addressed at the end of the last one. The Family of Imran closed with patience, watchfulness, and mindfulness. This chapter, often called Women, begins by tying mindfulness to the origin of the human family. Before inheritance, marriage, and the rights of orphans, Allah reminds people who they are and where they came from.\n\n“Be mindful of your Lord Who created you from a single soul, and from it He created its mate, and through both He spread countless men and women.” The “single soul” is the first human, Adam. From that one soul Allah made its mate, the first woman, so that the human story would not begin as two unrelated kinds. From the pair He spread countless men and women. Another verse says He created people from a male and a female and made them into peoples and tribes so that they may know one another. The most noble is the most righteous. This opening agrees with that. All the later rules about women, men, orphans, and relatives sit on one blood and one creation. No tribe may act as if another tribe were made of cheaper clay. No man may act as if woman were a different species.\n\n“And be mindful of Allah—in Whose Name you appeal to one another—and ˹honour˺ family ties.” Mindfulness is named a second time. People take oaths and ask favours by Allah’s name. The verse says: if you use His name to appeal to each other, then fear Him in the rights that exist between you. “Family ties” are the womb-relations that bind parents, children, siblings, and kin. Cutting those ties while speaking Allah’s name is a contradiction the verse will not allow.\n\n“Surely Allah is ever Watchful over you.” The Watcher sees the hidden deal around an orphan’s property, the quiet harshness in a home, and the polite speech that covers a stolen right. The whole chapter that follows is lived under that watch. What begins as a wide address to humanity will soon become very practical: give orphans their wealth, marry with justice, pay the dowry, and do not eat a weak person’s share.",
  "2": "“Give orphans their wealth ˹when they reach maturity˺” moves from the wide human family to the weakest members of it. An orphan in this setting is a child whose father has died, often leaving property in an adult’s hand. The command is to give them their wealth when they reach maturity, not to adopt it as a permanent extra income. The previous verse said Allah is Watchful. This verse names one of the first things He is watching.\n\n“and do not exchange your worthless possessions for their valuables.” A known abuse was to take a good animal or a fine piece of the orphan’s goods and put a poor substitute in its place, then say the pile was still the same size. The verse forbids that swap. The orphan’s quality is not a hunting ground for the guardian’s benefit.\n\n“nor cheat them by mixing their wealth with your own.” Mixing can be a trick. If the properties are stirred together without a clear account, the orphan’s share disappears into the adult’s spending, and later no one can prove what was whose. The Quran does not forbid every honest partnership. Another verse says that improving the orphans’ condition is best, and that if you partner with them they are bonded with you in faith. The difference is intention and clarity. Mixing in order to hide is “a great sin.”\n\n“For this would indeed be a great sin.” The wording is heavy on purpose. Some people treat a child’s property as a small matter because the child cannot sue. Allah calls the theft great. Later this chapter will say that those who unjustly consume orphans’ wealth consume nothing but fire into their bellies. The two verses should be read together. What looks like food now is fire later.\n\nSo the chapter’s first social law is not about the strong man’s honour. It is about the child’s pile. The next verse will treat another abuse of orphan girls in marriage, and will open the ruling about marrying two, three, or four only under the fear of failing justice.",
  "3": "“If you fear you might fail to give orphan women their ˹due˺ rights ˹if you were to marry them˺, then marry other women of your choice—two, three, or four.” The verse begins with fear of injustice, not with appetite. In that society a guardian sometimes married an orphan girl under his care because her property was near his hand, then failed to give her a fair dowry or a fair standing. This verse closes that door. If you fear you will not give such orphan women their rights in marriage, do not marry them for their goods. Marry other women, of your choice, up to four.\n\nThe number “two, three, or four” is a limit, not a target and not an open field. Pre-Islamic practice often had no such ceiling. The Quran both restricts the number and ties it to justice. Later this same chapter will say, “You will never be able to maintain ˹emotional˺ justice between your wives—no matter how keen you are,” and will forbid total inclination toward one while leaving another in suspense. So even the permission is fenced.\n\n“But if you are afraid you will fail to maintain justice, then ˹content yourselves with˺ one or those ˹bondwomen˺ in your possession.” Justice is the condition. If a man fears he cannot keep fairness among more than one wife in spending, time, and rights, he is told to remain with one. The verse also mentions those already lawfully in a man’s possession under the system of that time. The aim named is not collection of women. The aim is to avoid wrong.\n\n“This way you are less likely to commit injustice.” The closing reason must control the reading. The verse is treating marriage as a place where the weak can be eaten, and it is reducing that danger. A common misunderstanding is to treat this line as a command that every man should marry four, or as a denial of women’s dignity. The wording is the opposite. It starts from orphan women’s rights, limits number, and sends the fearful man back to one so that injustice will be less likely.\n\nThe next verse will continue the protection of women at the point of marriage itself: give them their due dowries graciously.",
  "4": "“Give women ˹you wed˺ their due dowries graciously.” After warning men not to marry orphan girls in order to swallow their rights, the Quran states the right of every wife to her dowry. The dowry is her due, not a tip and not a purchase price that makes her a product. “Graciously” means the giving should have a good spirit: promptly, openly, without sourness, and without a plan to snatch it back. A man who pays while looking angry has not yet obeyed the word “graciously.”\n\nThis command also corrects an old habit of handing the dowry to the woman’s male relatives as if she were not the owner. The verse says give women their due dowries. The property is theirs. Later verses will forbid taking it back through mistreatment, and will ask how a man could take back even a stack of gold after having lived with his wife. The present verse plants the first stake: it must be given, and given well.\n\n“But if they waive some of it willingly, then you may enjoy it freely with a clear conscience.” The woman is not forbidden to be generous. If she freely lets go of some of her dowry, without fear, pressure, or a cold house around her, the husband may take that waived part without a guilty conscience. “Willingly” is the wall around this permission. A waiver forced by harm, threats, or the hope of being treated less cruelly is not the willingness meant here. The same chapter will soon say it is not permissible to mistreat women in order to make them return some of the dowry.\n\nSo the verse balances two honours. Her right is protected against the man’s greed. Her free gift is protected against other people’s interference. A clear conscience is possible only when the waiver is real.\n\nThe next verse will turn to another trust: wealth that supports a household must not be placed in the hands of dependants who cannot manage it, though they must still be fed, clothed, and spoken to kindly.",
  "5": "“Do not entrust the incapable ˹among your dependants˺ with your wealth which Allah has made a means of support for you” protects the household’s living without humiliating the weak. “The incapable” are those who cannot handle property soundly: a child, a person of weak judgment, or anyone whose spending would destroy the money that feeds the family. “Your wealth” here includes the family’s supporting property, and also orphan wealth under a guardian’s care when the orphan is not yet ready. Allah made this wealth “a means of support.” It is not a toy and not a prize for anyone who asks for the keys.\n\nThe command is about trust and timing, not about contempt. A person may be loved and still not be the right holder of the purse today. Giving such a person full control is not kindness if the result is hunger, debt, and ruin.\n\n“but feed and clothe them from it, and speak to them kindly.” The same verse that withholds full control commands care and good speech. They are not to be left hungry while the money is “protected.” They are not to be dressed in shame. And they are not to be spoken to as if they were a burden or a fool. Kind speech is part of the ruling. A guardian can keep the account safe and still sin with his tongue.\n\nThis balances the previous commands. Orphans must receive their wealth when they are ready. Wives must receive their dowries. Yet the supporting wealth of a home is not to be thrown into incapable hands in the name of softness. Justice is neither swallowing the weak nor handing them a fire they cannot hold.\n\nThe next verse will explain how to know when an orphan is ready: test their competence until they reach a marriageable age, then, if they show sound judgment, return their wealth, with witnesses, and without wasteful haste before they grow up.",
  "6": "“Test ˹the competence of˺ the orphans until they reach a marriageable age.” The return of an orphan’s wealth is not tied to a birthday alone. It is tied to growth and to tested ability. “Test” means watch how they deal with small trusts, spending, and judgment as they approach the age of marriage. The aim is to see whether they can protect what is theirs. “Marriageable age” marks adulthood in the verse’s own language: a stage of maturity, not a race to marry them off.\n\n“Then if you feel they are capable of sound judgment, return their wealth to them.” Two conditions meet: age and sound judgment. If both are present, the guardian must hand the property back. He is not allowed to say, “I manage it better,” once the orphan can manage it lawfully. “And do not consume it wastefully and hastily before they grow up ˹to demand it˺.” A known trick was to spend quickly while the child was still weak, so that nothing remained when the child could ask. The verse names that haste as a sin.\n\n“If the guardian is well-off, they should not take compensation; but if the guardian is poor, let them take a reasonable provision.” The rich guardian serves without feeding on the orphan. The poor guardian may take a fair wage for real work, not an open hand. “Reasonable” blocks both greed and a false holiness that leaves the poor guardian to starve while he keeps another person’s field.\n\n“When you give orphans back their property, call in witnesses. And sufficient is Allah as a ˹vigilant˺ Reckoner.” Witnesses protect both sides from later accusations. Allah’s reckoning protects the orphan from a quiet cheat that no human witness saw. The Watcher named in the first verse is still here as Reckoner.\n\nThe next verse will announce a wider right that many old customs denied: men and women both have a share in what parents and close relatives leave, whether the estate is little or much.",
  "7": "“For men there is a share in what their parents and close relatives leave, and for women there is a share in what their parents and close relatives leave.” After protecting the living orphan, the chapter protects the inheriting woman and the inheriting man. In much of the surrounding custom, women and children could be pushed aside so that a strong male kinsman took the estate. This verse breaks that habit with a doubled sentence. Men have a share. Women have a share. The repetition is deliberate. Her share is not a favour granted by the men. It is stated in the same breath as his.\n\n“whether it is little or much.” The right does not appear only when the estate is large enough to make people polite. A small house, a few animals, a modest sum: she still has her share, and he still has his. People sometimes say, “It is too little to divide,” and then the weakest lose the little that would have helped them. The verse closes that excuse.\n\n“˹These are˺ obligatory shares.” Obligatory means the shares are a ruling from Allah, not a suggestion for kind families. The coming verses will name the portions in detail: daughters, parents, spouses, and maternal siblings. This verse gives the principle first so that the later numbers are heard as law, not as social taste.\n\nThe first verse of the chapter said humanity came from a single soul and its mate, and it commanded honour for family ties. This verse is one form of that honour. You do not honour the womb by burying a woman’s right when the father dies. You do not honour the family by letting a powerful relative swallow a child’s portion.\n\nThe next verse will soften the edges of formal inheritance without breaking it. If relatives who do not inherit, or orphans, or the needy, are present at the distribution, offer them a small provision and speak to them kindly. The obligatory shares stand. Mercy still has a place in the room.",
  "8": "“If ˹non-inheriting˺ relatives, orphans, or the needy are present at the time of distribution, offer them a ˹small˺ provision from it and speak to them kindly.” The last verse made inheritance shares obligatory. This verse stops those shares from becoming cold. Sometimes people stand in the room who have no legal portion: a poor cousin, an orphan from another branch, a needy neighbour who served the dead. The law does not transfer the estate to them. The Quran still tells the heirs to offer a small provision from it.\n\n“Small” matters. The gift should not swallow an heir’s right, especially a child’s or a woman’s share that this chapter came to protect. Mercy here is an extra kindness, not a new division that cancels the obligatory shares. The dead person’s debts and will, named in the coming verses, also come first. What remains may carry a little generosity in the gathering itself.\n\n“and speak to them kindly.” If nothing can be given, or if the estate is already tight, the tongue must still be good. A person who receives no coin should not receive humiliation. Kind speech is part of honouring the family ties named in the first verse. A harsh, “This is not yours, leave,” can be legally true and still be a sin of the mouth.\n\nThe verse trains a community that has just been told women and men both inherit. Some relatives who used to take everything by custom will now stand as non-inheriting guests. They must not be mocked. They may be given something. They must be spoken to well. Justice and courtesy are being taught in one room.\n\nThe next verse will turn from the distribution gathering back to the inner life of guardians. Let them fear for orphans as they would fear for their own helpless children if they themselves were about to die.",
  "9": "“Let the guardians be as concerned ˹for the orphans˺ as they would if they were to ˹die and˺ leave ˹their own˺ helpless children behind.” The Quran now enters the guardian’s imagination. Do not measure the orphan by the fact that he is someone else’s child. Measure him by the fear you would feel if death were coming for you tonight and your own little ones would be left weak. That imagined fear is meant to become present care. A person who truly sees his own children in the orphan’s face will not swap good property for bad, will not mix accounts in order to hide, and will not spend in haste before the child can ask.\n\nThis is one of the most practical moral tests in the chapter. Many people are brave with other people’s weakness and tender only with their own blood. The verse demands a transfer of tenderness. The first verse said humanity came from a single soul. This verse applies that origin to the orphan file.\n\n“So let them be mindful of Allah and speak equitably.” Mindfulness is named again because the orphan often has no watcher except Allah. Equitable speech means just words: honest accounts, no false comfort, no cruel reminders of dependence, no promises the guardian does not intend to keep. Speech here includes the way a guardian talks about the child’s future and the way he talks to judges and relatives.\n\nThe coming verse will show why this inner concern is not optional. Those who unjustly consume orphans’ wealth consume nothing but fire into their bellies. The imagined fear for one’s own children is meant to save a person from that fire. If the heart will not move for Allah’s watchfulness alone, let it move for the picture of its own children left helpless. Both motives are being used to protect the same weak life.",
  "10": "“Indeed, those who unjustly consume orphans’ wealth ˹in fact˺ consume nothing but fire into their bellies.” After commands, tests, and appeals to a parent’s fear, the Quran gives the unseen reality of the crime. What the thief of an orphan thinks he is eating is food, clothing, or a better house. What he is in fact putting into his belly is fire. The word “unjustly” leaves room for the poor guardian’s reasonable provision named earlier. The fire is for the unjust consumer, not for a fair wage.\n\nA similar picture appears in the previous chapter about people who hide revelation for a fleeting gain: they consume nothing but fire into their bellies. The two verses explain each other. Unlawful gain does not wait until the Hereafter to become punishment. It is already punishment entering the person, even while his outside looks well fed.\n\n“And they will be burned in a blazing Hell!” The inner fire becomes an outer home. The chapter does not treat this as a small social fault. It treats it as a road to blazing punishment. That severity matches the first verses: Allah is Watchful; this is a great sin; return the wealth with witnesses. A child cannot defend a field. So the threat is made large enough to defend the child.\n\nAn authentic report says the Prophet will be close in Paradise to the one who cares for an orphan. This verse is the opposite scene. One person is near the Prophet for protecting a child. Another is burned for eating the child’s wealth. The community is being taught that the orphan file is a file of the Hereafter, not only of local custom.\n\nThe next verses will turn from stolen property to the lawful division of what a dead person leaves. Allah Himself will command the shares of children and parents, so that the strong male kinsman cannot rewrite the family after a death.",
  "11": "“Allah commands you regarding your children: the share of the male will be twice that of the female.” The chapter now gives the first detailed map of inheritance. “Allah commands you” takes the matter out of tribal taste. The 2:1 share between a son and a daughter is stated as a command, not as a hint that a girl has no worth. The same chapter has already said women have a share, whether the estate is little or much, and that male and female are equal in the reward of deeds. This verse is about a family estate after death, in a system where sons were generally given the heavier financial duties of support. The Quran does not here ask the listener to invent a new religion. It asks him to hear the numbers as Allah’s command.\n\n“If you leave only two ˹or more˺ females, their share is two-thirds of the estate. But if there is only one female, her share will be one-half.” Daughters are not leftovers. Two or more daughters together take two-thirds when there is no son. One daughter takes half. These are large portions. They overturn any custom that treated a daughter as a guest at her father’s wealth.\n\n“Each parent is entitled to one-sixth if you leave offspring. But if you are childless and your parents are the only heirs, then your mother will receive one-third. But if you leave siblings, then your mother will receive one-sixth.” Parents are not erased by children, and a mother is not erased by a father. Her portion changes with the rest of the family: a sixth beside offspring, a third when she and the father are the main heirs, a sixth when siblings also inherit. The verse is teaching that shares move with the real household, not with whoever shouts loudest.\n\n“after the fulfilment of bequests and debts.” Before any of these portions is paid, debts are cleared and a lawful will is fulfilled. Heirs do not divide a pile that still belongs to creditors.\n\n“Be fair to your parents and children, as you do not fully know who is more beneficial to you.” People sometimes prefer a son for pride or a parent for sentiment. Allah says you do not fully know where the benefit lies. “This is an obligation from Allah. Surely Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” The next verse will complete the map with spouses and with brothers and sisters from the mother’s side.",
  "12": "“You will inherit half of what your wives leave if they are childless. But if they have children, then ˹your share is˺ one-fourth of the estate—after the fulfilment of bequests and debts.” The map now enters marriage. A husband takes half of his wife’s estate if she leaves no children, and a fourth if she does. Children reduce his share because their right is being honoured. Debts and bequests again come first. A man may not treat his wife’s property as already his while she lives, then take all of it when she dies.\n\n“And your wives will inherit one-fourth of what you leave if you are childless. But if you have children, then your wives will receive one-eighth of your estate—after the fulfilment of bequests and debts.” The wife’s share is stated with the same care. She is an heir, not a piece of the estate. One-fourth or one-eighth may look smaller than the husband’s fractions, but she is guaranteed a portion even when powerful relatives gather. In a world that often sent the widow away empty, this is protection.\n\n“And if a man or a woman leaves neither parents nor children but only a brother or a sister ˹from their mother’s side˺, they will each inherit one-sixth, but if they are more than one, they ˹all˺ will share one-third of the estate.” This is the special case of a person who leaves no parents and no children. Maternal siblings are given a defined kindness: a sixth each, or a shared third if they are more. The later verse at the end of this chapter will treat other sibling cases. Here the mother’s children are not forgotten.\n\n“after the fulfilment of bequests and debts without harm ˹to the heirs˺.” A person may leave a will, but not as a weapon to injure the heirs—by fake debts, by a will that swallows the estate, or by gifts meant only to punish a daughter. “This is a commandment from Allah. And Allah is All-Knowing, Most Forbearing.” He knows the hidden harm in a will, and He is forbearing with families while they learn to obey. The next two verses will call these entitlements the limits of Allah, with Paradise for obedience and Hell for exceeding them.",
  "13": "“These ˹entitlements˺ are the limits set by Allah.” The shares just named are not a first draft for later custom to edit. They are limits. A limit is a line that must not be crossed: not by a father who wants to erase a daughter, not by a brother who wants to erase a widow, not by a widow who wants to erase a mother, and not by a will designed to harm. The chapter began with Allah’s watchfulness. It now says the inheritance table itself is one of His borders.\n\n“Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger will be admitted into Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever. That is the ultimate triumph!” Obedience in this place includes paying the shares honestly. The reward, however, is larger than a clean estate. It is the same triumph defined at the end of the previous chapter: being admitted into Paradise. Rivers and a lasting stay are promised to people who accept Allah’s limits in the most tempting family hour—the hour when money is on the floor and the dead can no longer speak.\n\nNaming the Messenger beside Allah matters. The Prophet explains and applies these limits. Obedience is not a private redesign of the portions in the name of modern taste or old tribal taste. It is following what Allah sent and what His Messenger taught.\n\n“The ultimate triumph” also rebukes the person who thinks he has triumphed by seizing a house. Triumph is not the extra field. Triumph is the garden that lasts. The next verse will state the opposite limit-crossing: whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger and exceeds their limits will be cast into Hell forever, with a humiliating punishment. Family theft is being raised to the level of a path to the Fire, not left as a quiet village habit.",
  "14": "“But whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger and exceeds their limits will be cast into Hell, to stay there forever.” The previous verse promised Gardens to those who obey. This verse names the other road. Disobedience here is not a small slip in counting. It is pushing past the limits just set: devouring an orphan, cancelling a woman’s share, harming heirs through a will, or treating Allah’s portions as optional. “Exceeds their limits” pictures a person who will not stay inside the line. He wants more than his share, or he wants a rule of his own.\n\n“And they will suffer a humiliating punishment.” The punishment is not only pain. It is humiliation. The one who used power over a widow, a daughter, or a child is paid with disgrace. This matches the last chapter’s language of complete disgrace for those committed to the Fire, and of a humiliating punishment for people who used extra time only to increase in sin. The proud divider of estates is being told how his story ends if he dies on that excess.\n\nForever should be understood in the Quran’s usual way. The most severe lasting punishment is tied to dying upon rejection and upon treating Allah’s law as something to be thrown behind the back. A believer who wrongs an heir and then repents, returns the right, and mends is not being told that the door named later in this chapter is closed. The verse is warning the person who exceeds and persists.\n\nTogether, verses 13 and 14 make inheritance a matter of faith. A community may call itself religious and still fail this test when a parent dies. The Quran will not let that failure be called a private family choice. The next verses will move from property to another area where the weak can be crushed: illegal intercourse, witnesses, and the door of repentance.",
  "15": "“those of your women who commit illegal intercourse—call four witnesses from among yourselves.” The chapter turns from estates to a public indecency. “Illegal intercourse” is sexual relations outside a lawful marriage. The first protection in the verse is not a rumour. It is four witnesses from the community. Four is a high number. It blocks the common cruelty of destroying a woman’s name with one claim, one jealousy, or one whispered story. If the crime is not proven in this demanding way, the accusation itself becomes the danger the law must restrain. Later verses in this chapter will punish those who accuse chaste women without four witnesses.\n\n“If they testify, confine the offenders to their homes until they die or Allah ordains a ˹different˺ way for them.” The first ruling named here is confinement, not a hasty crowd punishment. They are kept in their homes until death, or until Allah opens another way. Many early scholars understood “a different way” as a promise that a later ruling would come. In the chapter of Light, a specified punishment for proven illegal intercourse was later revealed. The present verse should be read as an early regulation that already took the matter out of private vengeance, already required four witnesses, and already left room for Allah to complete the law.\n\nThe verse is addressed in the setting of women whose offence has been established, and it must not be used as a license to imprison women on suspicion. Without four witnesses, the confinement is not authorised by this wording. The next verse will speak of “the two among you who commit this sin,” command discipline, and then open relief for those who repent and mend their ways. Even in a passage about indecency, repentance is near, because Allah is ever Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful.",
  "16": "“And the two among you who commit this sin—discipline them.” After the case of women proven guilty, the verse speaks of the two who commit the same kind of sin. The wording is dual: two people. Many commentators understood a man and a woman. Some also discussed other readings. What is clear in the chapter’s own English is that the sin is shared, and the first response named is discipline, not a mob, and not a secret deal that protects the strong and ruins the weak.\n\n“Discipline them” is a command to correct, restrain, and make the wrong bitter enough that it is not treated as sport. It is not a command to torture, and it is not a cancellation of the four-witness standard just given. Public indecency is not left without a response. It is also not handed to anyone’s anger.\n\n“If they repent and mend their ways, relieve them.” The door is as clear as the discipline. Repentance is turning back. Mending their ways is the change that proves the turning is real. Then they are to be relieved: the pressure is lifted, the community does not keep them under a lifelong boot, and the sin is not turned into a brand that outlives their return. This matches the last chapter’s praise of people who, upon committing an evil deed, remember Allah, seek forgiveness, and do not knowingly persist.\n\n“Surely Allah is ever Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful.” The community is being told to copy, in its small way, the way Allah receives people. If He is Accepting of Repentance, the believers should not act as if they are stricter than their Lord. The next two verses will define which repentance is accepted: the repentance of those who commit evil ignorantly or recklessly and then repent soon after—not the cry of a person who persists until death arrives and then says, “Now I repent.”",
  "17": "“Allah only accepts the repentance of those who commit evil ignorantly ˹or recklessly˺ then repent soon after.” After opening the door for the two who sinned, the Quran explains what kind of turning Allah has promised to accept. “Ignorantly or recklessly” does not mean the person had never heard that the act was wrong. In the Quran’s language it often means he did it out of weakness, heat, foolishness, or a lapse of the self, not as a proud war against Allah. Human beings sin in that way often. The accepted path is to repent soon after: to turn while the door is still the door of the living, not to store the sin like a treasure.\n\n“Soon after” is a mercy and a warning. Mercy, because it does not demand that a person have been perfect from birth. Warning, because delay itself becomes a second sin. A man who says, “I will repent when I am old,” is already refusing the wording of this verse. Death does not send an appointment.\n\n“Allah will pardon them. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” Pardon is promised to that quick, honest turning. All-Knowing means He sees whether the repentance is real or only a sentence. All-Wise means He does not treat a reckless stumble that is followed by repair as if it were the same as dying upon disbelief. This continues the last chapter’s teaching that those who repent afterwards and mend their ways find Allah All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nThe next verse will draw the hard line. Repentance is not accepted from those who knowingly persist until they start dying and then cry, “Now I repent,” nor from those who die as disbelievers. The two verses must be kept together. One keeps sinners from despair. The other keeps them from making a joke of the door.",
  "18": "“However, repentance is not accepted from those who knowingly persist in sin until they start dying, and then cry, ‘Now I repent!’” The door described in the previous verse has a time. A person who knows the wrong, remains in it, and treats repentance as a last-minute spell is not doing the turning Allah praised. “Start dying” means the moment when death has arrived and the test is over: the soul is leaving, and the unseen is becoming sight. At that point the cry “Now I repent” is like a student asking to begin the exam after the papers have been collected.\n\nThis is not the same as a person who has been trying, falling, and returning throughout life, then dies while still seeking mercy. The verse is about knowing persistence, then a panic at the end. Pharaoh’s last confession when drowning is the famous picture of a too-late recognition. The wording here is general.\n\n“nor those who die as disbelievers.” The second group never turns in this life at all. Their death upon disbelief seals the refusal. The last chapter said that if such people offered a world of gold it would not be accepted. This verse says their repentance after that death-line is not the repentance Allah has promised to accept.\n\n“For them We have prepared a painful punishment.” The preparation means the result is not improvised in anger. It is the known end of a chosen delay. Together with the previous verse, the teaching is simple enough for any adult. If you can still choose, repent soon. If you plan to persist and then whisper a sentence at the last breath, you are planning a repentance Allah has said He does not accept.\n\nThe next verses will return to women’s rights in marriage and widowhood. It is not permissible to inherit women against their will, or to mistreat them so that they return the dowry. Fair treatment is commanded even when the heart dislikes a wife.",
  "19": "“It is not permissible for you to inherit women against their will” names a cruelty that some men treated as a right. In parts of pre-Islamic custom, a widow could be inherited like a house. A relative of the dead husband might claim her, block her from marrying another, or keep her for her property. The verse abolishes that. A woman is not part of the estate. The first verses of the chapter said she has a share in what relatives leave. This verse says she herself is not a share to be left.\n\n“or mistreat them to make them return some of the dowry ˹as a ransom for divorce˺—unless they are found guilty of adultery.” The second cruelty is pressuring a wife with harm until she buys her freedom by giving back the dowry. That is forbidden. The exception named is when she is found guilty of adultery, a case already fenced by witnesses. Even then the verse is not a license for torture. It is a limit on taking back wealth. The ordinary path remains: treat them fairly.\n\n“Treat them fairly.” Fair treatment is the standing command, not a decoration. It covers living, speaking, spending, and releasing. “If you happen to dislike them, you may hate something which Allah turns into a great blessing.” Marriage is not being built on a passing mood. A man may dislike a trait, a season, or a disappointment, and still be commanded to stay just. The disliked thing may hide a blessing: a righteous child, a later love, a protection from a worse path, or a training of the soul. The verse does not order him to lie about his feelings. It orders him not to make those feelings into injustice.\n\nThe next verse will make the financial side even stricter. If he wants to replace a wife and has given her even a stack of gold, he may not take any of it back. Would he still take it unjustly and very sinfully?",
  "20": "“If you desire to replace a wife with another and you have given the former ˹even˺ a stack of gold ˹as a dowry˺, do not take any of it back.” Desire to replace a wife is being regulated, not blessed as a sport. If a man wants a new marriage, the old wife’s dowry is not a fund he can reclaim to finance the new one. “Even a stack of gold” is an extreme example used to close every smaller excuse. If such a huge dowry cannot be snatched back, then a modest dowry certainly cannot. The earlier command to give dowries graciously is now given its companion: once given, it stays hers when the marriage is being ended by his wish to replace her.\n\n“Would you ˹still˺ take it unjustly and very sinfully?” The question is meant to shame the hand that reaches back. Taking it is named unjust and very sinful, not merely impolite. The man would be using his power at the moment of her loss—loss of the marriage, loss of face, loss of the home—to take her wealth as well. The Watcher named in the first verse is still watching this room.\n\nThis also protects women from a market marriage in which a large dowry is advertised and then reclaimed through pressure. The next verse will add the moral reason that numbers cannot carry alone. How could you take it back after having enjoyed each other intimately, and after she has taken from you a firm commitment? The gold is not the only thing that has been given. A life has been shared, and a covenant has been taken.\n\nSo replacement, if it happens, must not become a second injury. He may move toward another marriage only without stealing from the first. The chapter is slowly building a law of dignity around women who, in the old custom, could be inherited, squeezed, or stripped.",
  "21": "“And how could you take it back after having enjoyed each other intimately and she has taken from you a firm commitment?” The previous verse forbade taking back the dowry when replacing a wife. This verse asks how a man could even think of it. “After having enjoyed each other intimately” reminds him that the marriage was not a failed business deal. It was a shared closeness. To enjoy that closeness and then reclaim the dowry is to treat the woman as a used object whose price can be refunded. The Quran refuses that accounting.\n\n“and she has taken from you a firm commitment.” The dowry and the marriage contract are a firm commitment. He spoke words before Allah and before witnesses. She received those words as security. Taking the wealth back is therefore not only greed. It is a breaking of a pledge. This chapter will later command people not to devour wealth illegally and to honour trusts. The marriage pledge is one of the heaviest trusts in a person’s life.\n\nThe question form is important. Allah does not only legislate. He appeals to honour. A man who still has a living conscience should be unable to do this after the reminder. A man who can still do it is being told that his hardness is itself the problem.\n\nRead with the verses before it, the teaching is a chain. Do not inherit women. Do not harm them to recover the dowry. If you replace a wife, do not take anything back, even a stack of gold. How could you, after intimacy and after a firm commitment? The next verse will add another abolished custom: do not marry former wives of your fathers. That too was a way of treating a woman as property that moves with a man’s estate.",
  "22": "“Do not marry former wives of your fathers—except what was done previously.” Another pre-Islamic practice is cut off. A son would sometimes take his father’s widow or former wife as if she were part of the father’s leftover goods. The verse forbids that marriage. “Except what was done previously” means the new rule is not being used to unpick every old union from the days of ignorance in a way that creates chaos. From now on the practice is closed.\n\n“It was indeed a shameful, despicable, and evil practice.” Three ugly names are stacked so that no one can defend the custom as honouring the father or keeping the woman inside the family. It is shameful in itself, despicable in what it does to a woman’s dignity, and evil as a way of life. A father’s wife is in the standing of a mother in respect. To take her as a wife is to confuse the household and to treat her as inherited property. The first verse of the chapter commanded honour for family ties. This verse applies that honour.\n\nThe wording also protects the woman from a second captivity after her husband’s death. She is free to live, to inherit her share, and to marry someone lawful. She is not a chair that passes to the son.\n\nThe next verse will widen the circle of women forbidden in marriage: mothers, daughters, sisters, aunts, nieces, foster-mothers, foster-sisters, mothers-in-law, certain stepdaughters, sons’ wives, and two sisters at the same time. The family is being given a map of respect, not only a map of desire. Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful toward what was done in the old ignorance, and firm about what must not be done now.",
  "23": "“forbidden to you for marriage are your mothers, your daughters, your sisters, your paternal and maternal aunts, your brother’s daughters, your sister’s daughters.” The verse now lists the near women who cannot be wives. The list begins with blood. A mother, daughter, sister, aunt, or niece is a person of honour and kinship, not a possible bride. This is part of the chapter’s care for family ties. A home that blurs these lines destroys itself.\n\n“your foster-mothers, your foster-sisters.” Milk-relationship is given the force of blood in marriage law. A woman who nursed a child as a mother, and the sisters formed through that nursing, are forbidden just as birth-mothers and birth-sisters are. The Prophet taught that what is forbidden through birth is forbidden through fostering. The Quran here states the core of that teaching.\n\n“your mothers-in-law, your stepdaughters under your guardianship if you have consummated marriage with their mothers—but if you have not, then you can marry them—nor the wives of your own sons.” Marriage creates new forbidden ties. A mother-in-law is forbidden. A stepdaughter is forbidden if the marriage with her mother has been consummated, especially as she is under the man’s guardianship. If the marriage with the mother was not consummated, the stepdaughter is not forbidden by this clause. A son’s wife is forbidden. The household cannot be turned into a chain of replacements that humiliate the young and the old.\n\n“nor two sisters together at the same time—except what was done previously.” Combining two sisters as wives is forbidden because it forces two women bound by sisterhood into rivalry. “Except what was done previously” again refuses to wreck every old case with chaos, while closing the door going forward.\n\n“Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” He forgives the old ignorance of those who turn back, and He is merciful in setting limits that protect the family. The next verse will add married women to the forbidden list, except in the special case of female captives, and will then open what is lawful: seeking women with wealth in a legal marriage, not in fornication.",
  "24": "“Also ˹forbidden are˺ married women—except ˹female˺ captives in your possession.” A woman already married is not lawful for another man. Her existing marriage is a barrier. That is the main rule, and it protects homes. The exception named is female captives in lawful possession after war, a reality of that time’s law of battle. Classical teachers explained that captivity ended the old marriage bond in that specific legal setting, and that a waiting period was still required before any new relation. The verse must not be twisted into a permission to take any married woman. The default is prohibition. The exception is a regulated case in a world that already took captives, not a command to go hunting homes.\n\n“This is Allah’s commandment to you. ˹But˺ lawful to you are all beyond these—as long as you seek them with your wealth in a legal marriage, not in fornication.” After the long forbidden list, the lawful path is stated in one line. Seek with your wealth: pay the dowry. In a legal marriage: a public, contracted bond. Not in fornication: not in secret use of women, not in paid indecency dressed as romance. Wealth is for marriage, not for buying sin.\n\n“Give those you have consummated marriage with their due dowries. It is permissible to be mutually gracious regarding the set dowry.” The dowry is due when the marriage has been entered. Then, as before, the two may be gracious: she may willingly reduce it, he may willingly increase it. Force is not grace. “Surely Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” He knows the secret affair that people rename as love, and the quiet theft of a dowry that people rename as custom.\n\nThe next verse will give a concession to a man who cannot afford to marry a free believing woman: he may marry a believing bondwoman, with permission, with a fair dowry, and only if she is chaste. Patience, the verse will say, is still better.",
  "25": "“But if any of you cannot afford to marry a free believing woman, then ˹let him marry˺ a believing bondwoman possessed by one of you.” The verse is a concession for a man who fears sin and cannot afford marriage to a free believer. It is not a ranking of human worth. The next sentences will fight that ranking. “Allah knows best the state of your faith and theirs. You are from one another.” Faith is known to Allah, not to proud families. “You are from one another” returns to the single soul of the first verse. The free and the bondswoman are not two species.\n\n“So marry them with the permission of their owners, giving them their dowry in fairness, if they are chaste, neither promiscuous nor having secret affairs.” Even this concession has conditions. Permission, because she is not a thing to be snatched. A fair dowry, because she is a wife, not a pastime. Chastity, because the aim is a lawful home, not the same indecency the chapter has been closing.\n\n“If they commit indecency after marriage, they receive half the punishment of free women.” The legal burden is lighter, matching their weaker social power in that system. The verse is regulating punishment, not inviting suspicion.\n\n“This is for those of you who fear falling into sin. But if you are patient, it is better for you. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The concession is for fear of falling. Patience—waiting until one can marry a free woman, or restraining desire—is called better. Forgiveness and mercy surround people who struggle with weakness. The next verses will say it is Allah’s will to make things clear, to guide to the noble ways of those before, and to turn to you in mercy, while those who follow desire want you to deviate entirely.",
  "26": "“It is Allah’s Will to make things clear to you” gathers the last rulings and tells the listener why so many details have arrived: orphans, shares, witnesses, dowries, forbidden women, lawful marriage. Allah wants clarity, not a fog in which the strong always win. Making things clear is itself a mercy. A community left to custom will call its cruelties inheritance and its desires love. Revelation names them.\n\n“guide you to the ˹noble˺ ways of those before you” ties these laws to the older prophetic path. The noble ways are the ways of prophets who protected lineage, marriage, and the weak. This is not a new hobby of the Arabs. It is guidance into a known goodness. The last chapter insisted that Abraham’s way was submission and that the messengers were one line. This verse applies that unity to family law.\n\n“and turn to you in mercy.” The laws are not only fences. They are a turning of Allah toward the community. He turns to them by teaching, by limiting harm, by allowing a concession to the weak, and by promising pardon to those who repent soon after a reckless sin. “For Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” He knows the abuses that needed these verses, and He is wise in the mix of firmness and lightness.\n\nThe next verse will set Allah’s will to turn in grace against another will: those who follow their desires wish to see you deviate entirely from Allah’s Way. The conflict under these marriage and property laws is not technical. It is a conflict between mercy that orders the home and desire that wants no limit at all.",
  "27": "“And it is Allah’s Will to turn to you in grace, but those who follow their desires wish to see you deviate entirely ˹from Allah’s Way˺.” Two wills are now placed in the open. Allah’s will is to turn to the believers in grace: to forgive, to teach, to lighten, to keep their homes from becoming hunting grounds. The other will belongs to people who follow their desires. They do not only want one exception. They want to see the community “deviate entirely.” Entirely means not a small slip, but a full leaving of the way: no limits on who may be married, no dowry, no shares for women, no protection for orphans, no shame in taking back a firm commitment.\n\nDesire here is not only sexual. It is every wish that hates a limit: greed for an orphan’s field, hunger to inherit a widow, love of praise for a false piety, the wish to persist in sin and still be called safe. The last chapter spoke of people who wanted to make the way appear crooked. This verse says some want the believers themselves bent off the road.\n\nThe believer should therefore hear these family rulings as a rescue, not as a cage. The One who set them is turning in grace. The voices that say, “Drop all of this and be free,” are not offering freedom. They are offering a total deviation.\n\nThe next verse will add a third statement of Allah’s will: to lighten your burdens, for humankind was created weak. Clarity, grace, and lightness belong together. The concessions about patience, about one wife if justice is feared, and about not trapping people in the old ignorance are part of that lightness. Weakness is admitted. Desire is not given the throne.",
  "28": "“And it is Allah’s Will to lighten your burdens, for humankind was created weak.” The last two verses said Allah wills to make things clear and to turn to you in grace, while people of desire want a total deviation. This verse adds the tone of the law: lightness. “Lighten your burdens” means He does not enjoy trapping people. The concessions already given—one wife if justice is feared, patience called better than a hard marriage of necessity, repentance soon after a reckless sin, not unpicking every old union with chaos—are part of that will.\n\n“for humankind was created weak.” Weakness here is not an insult that cancels duty. It is a fact that shapes mercy. People get tired, afraid, hungry, and pulled by desire. A law that pretends they are angels will either break them or teach them to lie. A law that admits weakness can still forbid injustice while opening lighter paths. The next verses will forbid devouring wealth illegally and killing one another, then promise that if you avoid major sins, lesser misdeeds can be absolved. That is lightness without lawlessness.\n\nThis also answers anyone who hears the family rulings and says Allah only wants to burden. The chapter’s own voice is the opposite. He wants to lift what custom had made crushing: inherited women, swallowed orphans, unlimited marriages, stolen dowries. New limits on the strong are lightness for the weak. Limits on desire are lightness for the soul that would otherwise be owned by it.\n\nSo when a believer finds a ruling hard, he should not first accuse the Lawgiver of heaviness. He should look for the burden that was removed from someone weaker, and for the door of mercy that still remains. The One who created human beings weak did not then forget what He created.",
  "29": "“Do not devour one another’s wealth illegally, but rather trade by mutual consent.” After inheritance and marriage money, the chapter states the general rule of property. “Devour” makes illegal taking look like eating someone else’s life. Illegal wealth includes theft, cheating, false oaths, swallowing an orphan’s goods, taking back a dowry by harm, interest, bribery, and any transfer that the other side did not truly agree to. “Trade by mutual consent” is the lawful opposite: an open exchange in which both sides know and accept. Consent squeezed out by fear, fraud, or a hidden defect is not the consent meant here.\n\nThe first verse said people appeal to one another in Allah’s name. This verse says they may not use that same social closeness to eat each other.\n\n“And do not kill ˹each other or˺ yourselves.” The wording holds two crimes that often grow from the same despair or the same greed: killing another person, and destroying one’s own life. Both are forbidden. Life, like wealth, is a trust. Mutual consent does not make suicide lawful, and rage does not make murder a private right. “Surely Allah is ever Merciful to you.” The prohibitions themselves are mercy. A community that eats wealth and sheds blood is not a free community. It is a wounded one.\n\nThis verse should be read with the coming threat. Whoever does this sinfully and unjustly will be burned in the Fire. Mercy now is the warning before that burning. It is also the reminder that a person in pain is not abandoned: Allah is ever Merciful, so the answer to loss is not to devour a brother or to kill the self.\n\nIn ordinary life the two commands meet. People kill over money, and people ruin themselves over money. The Quran cuts both roads at once and leaves trade, consent, and mercy.",
  "30": "“And whoever does this sinfully and unjustly, We will burn them in the Fire.” “This” points back to the illegal devouring of wealth and the killing of others or of oneself. The verse does not threaten a person who made an honest mistake in a sale, or who caused a death by a true accident without sin. It names the one who does it “sinfully and unjustly”: knowing, choosing, and crossing the right of Allah and the right of people.\n\n“We will burn them in the Fire.” The Speaker is Allah. The punishment is not a village revenge. It is the Fire already prepared for those who treat lives and properties as prey. This continues the chapter’s hard honesty about orphans: unjust consumption is fire in the belly, then a blazing Hell. Now the same end is attached to the wider market and to blood.\n\n“That is easy for Allah.” People sometimes sin because they think the rich, the armed, or the hidden cannot be touched. Easy for Allah means no court is too corrupt, no grave is too deep, and no private room is too dark. He does not struggle to repay. The phrase also removes the idea that such burning would be difficult for a merciful Lord. Mercy has already been stated. Justice remains easy for Him.\n\nA believer should hear this before a tempting file, a bitter family fight, or a dark hour of self-harm. The act may look like a solution. The verse names it as a road into fire. The next verse will open a wide hope for ordinary sinners: if you avoid the major sins forbidden to you, We will absolve you of your lesser misdeeds and admit you into a place of honour. The religion is not a demand for angelic perfection. It is a demand to leave the great crimes.",
  "31": "“If you avoid the major sins forbidden to you, We will absolve you of your ˹lesser˺ misdeeds and admit you into a place of honour.” After naming fire for sinful, unjust devouring and killing, Allah speaks to the ordinary believer who fears he will never be clean. He does not say, “Be free of every speck.” He says, avoid the major sins forbidden to you. Major sins are the great forbidden acts this Book keeps naming: associating partners with Allah, devouring the orphan, killing without right, illegal intercourse, false accusation, dealing in interest, and other grave wrongs established by revelation. If a person guards himself from those, Allah promises to wipe the smaller faults that cling to a weak human life.\n\n“Absolve you of your lesser misdeeds” is a gift, not a wage that makes Allah a debtor. Weakness was admitted two verses ago. This is how weakness is treated when it does not become rebellion. The small daily slips—a sharp word, a missed extra good, a lapse that is not a major crime—are not turned into a life sentence.\n\n“and admit you into a place of honour.” The end is not only cleaning. It is honour: a noble place with Allah, the opposite of the humiliation threatened for those who exceed the limits. Honour here is the dignity of being received, not the pride of thinking one never needed pardon.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat this verse as permission to be careless about any sin that is not “major.” The verse is a comfort for those who are trying, not a discount for those who hunt for loopholes. Another misunderstanding is despair: “I can never be perfect, so why try?” This verse answers that despair. Avoid the great forbidden things. Seek forgiveness for the rest. Hope for a place of honour.\n\nThe next verse will treat a quieter disease that often leads back to major sin: craving what Allah has given some people over others, instead of asking Him for His bounties.",
  "32": "“And do not crave what Allah has given some of you over others.” After inheritance shares, marriage rules, and different provisions, hearts can sour. A woman may look at a man’s share or role and burn. A man may look at another man’s wealth, wife, or rank and burn. The verse forbids that craving. Envy is not only a feeling. It is a protest against Allah’s distribution and a seed of injustice. The last chapter warned that some People of the Book wished to turn believers back out of envy. This verse stops the same fire inside the home.\n\n“Men will be rewarded according to their deeds and women ˹equally˺ according to theirs.” The true scale is not the estate table and not a public role. It is deeds. Women and men are paid by what they do. This returns to the last chapter’s answer: I will never deny any of you—male or female—the reward of your deeds. Both are equal in reward. A person who measures honour only by a fraction of inheritance has chosen the weaker scale.\n\n“Rather, ask Allah for His bounties.” The lawful replacement for craving is asking. Ask for provision, for a good marriage, for a child, for knowledge, for a way out. Asking treats Allah as the Giver. Craving treats a brother or sister as a thief of what should have been yours. “Surely Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.” He knows why He gave what He gave, and He knows the hidden bitterness that people call ambition.\n\nThis verse does not forbid working, trading, or seeking lawful improvement. It forbids the eye that cannot rest except by taking what was given to another. The next verse will return to appointed heirs and to pledges. Give people the share Allah named, and give those you have pledged their share. He is a Witness over all things.",
  "33": "“And We have appointed heirs to what has been left by parents and next of kin.” The shares are not a human invention that a powerful relative may redraw. “We have appointed” means Allah assigned the heirs. Parents and next of kin leave property, and Allah names who receives it. This confirms the detailed portions already given and blocks the old habit of handing everything to one adult male.\n\n“As for those you have made a pledge to, give them their share.” Beside blood-heirs there may be people tied by a pledge: a contract of alliance, a commitment of support, or, in the early community, bonds that had been treated like kinship. The verse orders that such a pledged share be given and not “forgotten” once the inheritance table is recited. Scholars discussed how far this pledged share remained after the later settling of inheritance law. What the wording itself requires is clear: a pledge is not air. If you have made one, you owe the share.\n\n“Surely Allah is a Witness over all things.” He witnesses the will, the whispered plan to cut out a daughter, the pledge made in a room, and the later denial of that pledge. A witness this complete makes fraud a form of self-harm.\n\nRead with the previous verse, the teaching is balanced. Do not crave other people’s gifts. Do give people the gifts and shares Allah has assigned, and the shares you yourself promised. Envy looks at what is not yours. Faithfulness delivers what is theirs.\n\nThe next verse will describe caretaking in marriage: men as caretakers of women because of provision and the duty of financial support, the character of righteous women, and a graded response if ill-conduct is sensed—advice, then separation in bed, then gentle discipline—followed by a ban on injustice if they change.",
  "34": "“Men are the caretakers of women, as men have been provisioned by Allah over women and tasked with supporting them financially.” The verse begins with a job, not a throne. “Caretakers” means those charged to protect, provide, and take responsibility. The reason named is not that a woman has no mind or no reward. The reason named is provision and the financial duty already placed on men in this chapter: the dowry, the support of the home, the heavier worldly load that sat beside the inheritance shares. Caretaking that becomes control for the ego has left the verse.\n\n“And righteous women are devoutly obedient and, when alone, protective of what Allah has entrusted them with.” Righteous wives are described by two honours. They are devoutly obedient: first to Allah, and then faithful within the known good of the marriage. When alone, they protect what Allah entrusted: their chastity, the home, the husband’s honour, and the unseen trusts of the household. The best of this description is a woman who fears Allah in the empty house, not only in public.\n\n“And if you sense ill-conduct from your women, advise them ˹first˺, ˹if they persist,˺ do not share their beds, ˹but if they still persist,˺ then discipline them ˹gently˺.” The steps are ordered. First, words. Then, distance in the bed, which is a serious warning without a show for the neighbours. Then, if the ill-conduct still persists, discipline that the verse itself qualifies as gentle. It cannot be a license for cruelty. The Prophet, who explained this Book, was not a man who struck his wives, and he taught that the best men are the best to their wives. Any reading that produces terror in a home is fighting the word “gently” and fighting his example.\n\n“But if they change their ways, do not be unjust to them.” Once the problem ends, punishment must end. Bringing up the old fault as a permanent whip is injustice. “Surely Allah is Most High, All-Great.” The husband is not the Most High. He is a caretaker under a greater Watcher. The next verse will take a feared split out of one man’s hands and place it before two mediators, one from each family.",
  "35": "“If you anticipate a split between them, appoint a mediator from his family and another from hers.” When caretaking and the graded warnings of the previous verse are not enough, the Quran refuses a lonely, secret collapse of the marriage. “Anticipate a split” means the break is becoming likely, not only that a single angry night has occurred. Then each side is to have a mediator from its own family. Two, not one. Family, not a stranger who knows neither history. The aim named later is reconciliation, not a contest to humiliate the other house.\n\nThis is a mercy to the weaker party. A woman, or a man, who would be crushed in a private argument is given a voice through someone who cares for her or his honour. It is also a mercy to the children and to the wider kin. Marriage in this chapter is a public trust, not a hidden cage.\n\n“If they desire reconciliation, Allah will restore harmony between them.” “They” includes the couple and, in the spirit of the verse, the mediators who truly want peace. Desire for reconciliation is a condition of the heart. If the hidden wish is revenge, victory, or money, harmony is unlikely. If the wish is repair, Allah can put back what people cannot glue with speeches. He is the One who earlier united hearts that had been enemies.\n\n“Surely Allah is All-Knowing, All-Aware.” He knows which mediator is honest, which spouse is pretending, and which wound can heal. People see the outer sitting. He sees the desire.\n\nThe verse does not force a prison of a marriage. It forces a fair attempt, with two families present, before the split becomes a war. The next verse will widen kindness beyond the couple: worship Allah alone, and be kind to parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, neighbours near and far, friends, travellers, and those in your possession. Arrogance is named as the thing Allah does not like.",
  "36": "“Worship Allah ˹alone˺ and associate none with Him.” After homes, estates, and mediators, the chapter returns to the centre. Kindness without monotheism can become a social taste. Monotheism without kindness can become a hard slogan. This verse joins them. Worship Allah alone. Give none of His right to an idol, a desire, a saint, or a man.\n\n“And be kind to parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, near and distant neighbours, close friends, ˹needy˺ travellers, and those ˹bondspeople˺ in your possession.” The list is a map of the human circle, from the nearest to the easily forgotten. Parents come first after Allah’s right, as in many other verses. Relatives continue the family ties of the opening. Orphans and the poor return this chapter to its first concern. Neighbours are of two kinds, near and distant: those beside your wall and those farther in space or in relationship. Close friends are not left out. The traveller in need is named. Those in a person’s lawful possession are named last so that power will not become blindness. Kindness to the one who cannot leave is among the greatest tests.\n\n“Surely Allah does not like whoever is arrogant, boastful—” The sentence runs into the next verse, but the dislike is already clear. Arrogance and boasting cannot carry this list. A boastful man may worship in public and crush a parent, a neighbour, or a servant in private. Allah does not like that person. The next verse will finish the portrait: those who are stingy, promote stinginess, and withhold Allah’s bounties. A humiliating punishment is prepared for the disbelievers among them.\n\nSo the religion being taught is a house with an open door. The One God, then a chain of kindness that does not stop at the family wall. Anyone who loves slogans and hates neighbours has not yet entered this verse.",
  "37": "“those who are stingy, promote stinginess among people, and withhold Allah’s bounties.” The arrogant, boastful person of the previous verse is now seen in his money. Stinginess is refusing to give what should be given. Promoting stinginess is worse: teaching others to close their hands, mocking generosity, and making care of the poor look foolish. Withholding Allah’s bounties names the truth about the wealth. It is Allah’s bounty, not a private sun. The last chapter said people who withhold bounties should not think it is good for them; they will be leashed with what they withheld. This verse belongs to that same warning.\n\nThe three acts often travel together. A man first clings. Then he needs company in clinging, so he praises greed as wisdom. Then he treats every request—from an orphan, a neighbour, a relative—as an attack on what is “his.” The list of people to be kind to, just given, dies in that atmosphere.\n\n“We have prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment.” The sharpest end is named for disbelief joined to this greed. Humiliation fits the crime. They wanted to look high by keeping and by teaching others to keep. They are paid with being brought low. A believer who slips into stinginess should not comfort himself that the verse is only about someone else. The disease is being condemned. If it grows until it becomes a way of life against Allah’s bounties, it leads toward that humiliation.\n\nThe next verse will add another false use of wealth: spending to show off, without belief in Allah or the Last Day. Taking Satan as an associate is called an evil association. Stinginess and showy spending look like opposites. In this passage they are neighbours, because both treat wealth as a tool of the self, not as a trust from Allah.",
  "38": "“Likewise for those who spend their wealth to show off and do not believe in Allah or the Last Day.” The stingy and the show-off are placed under one threat. One locks the bounty. The other throws it in the street for a name. Neither believes in Allah and the Last Day in a way that governs the hand. If they did, the stingy would fear the leash of withheld wealth, and the show-off would fear a deed that has no weight because it was done for faces.\n\nSpending to show off can look like the kindness commanded two verses ago. It can feed a neighbour, free a captive, or fund a public good. The verse looks at the motive. Without faith in Allah and the Last Day, the spending is a purchase of reputation. The previous chapter compared such charity to a rock with a thin layer of soil, then rain, then bare stone. Nothing remains.\n\n“And whoever takes Satan as an associate—what an evil associate they have!” The show-off and the one who withholds are not acting alone in the moral sense. They have accepted Satan’s company: his pride, his love of being seen, his hatred of sincere giving. An associate shares the work and the end. What an evil associate, because he invites a person to spend or to withhold, then leaves him with the humiliating punishment.\n\nThe next verse will ask a plain question. What harm could have come to them if they had believed in Allah and the Last Day and donated from what Allah provided? The imagined harm is false. Belief and honest donation do not impoverish a person with Allah. They only threaten Satan’s partnership and the ego’s fame.",
  "39": "“What harm could have come to them if they had believed in Allah and the Last Day and donated from what Allah has provided for them?” The question unmasks the fear behind stinginess and behind show. People act as if faith and honest giving would injure them. The verse asks: what harm, exactly? Would belief reduce Allah’s kingdom? Would a donation from His provision empty a store that He cannot refill? Would the Last Day go worse for a person because he had believed in it and given? The implied answer is that no real harm would have come. The only things harmed by such faith and giving are pride and greed.\n\n“Donated from what Allah has provided” repeats the truth that the wealth is provision, not self-created glory. Giving from it is returning a portion of a gift, like the last chapter’s command to donate some of what you cherish. The people being questioned refused even that, or they gave only to be seen.\n\n“And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of them.” He knows the excuse they tell others—“I would give if I had more,” “I spend more than the misers,” “faith would ruin my position”—and He knows the real motive. Knowledge here is already a form of accountability. They do not need to write a defence. He has them.\n\nThe next verse will state the justice that makes this question so strong. Allah never wrongs anyone, even by an atom’s weight. If it is a good deed, He multiplies it many times and gives a great reward out of His grace. So the person who refused to believe and donate did not avoid a loss. He avoided a multiplication.",
  "40": "“Indeed, Allah never wrongs ˹anyone˺—even by an atom’s weight.” After asking what harm faith and donation could have done, the Quran states the scale of divine justice. An atom’s weight is a tiny measure. Even that is not stolen from a person. No one will meet Allah and find that a hidden good was deleted, or that a sin he did not commit was added. This continues the last chapter’s refrain that Allah desires no injustice to His creation.\n\nThe line comforts the weak. An orphan whose field was eaten, a wife whose dowry was snatched, a neighbour who was mocked for giving, a woman whose deeds were ignored by her people: none of that is too small for this scale. It also warns the strong. An atom’s weight of fraud is still on the scale.\n\n“And if it is a good deed, He will multiply it many times over and will give a great reward out of His grace.” Justice is the floor, not the ceiling. Good is not merely counted. It is multiplied. Then, beyond multiplication, there is a great reward out of grace. Grace means He gives more than the deed itself could buy. That is why refusing to donate is so foolish. The person is not avoiding a fair swap. He is refusing a multiplication and a gift.\n\nThis verse should make a believer both careful and hopeful. Careful, because nothing unjust is forgotten. Hopeful, because nothing good is wasted, and Allah’s grace is larger than the act. The next verse will show the public scene of that justice: a witness from every faith-community, and the Prophet brought as a witness against his own.",
  "41": "“So how will it be when We bring a witness from every faith-community and bring you ˹O Prophet˺ as a witness against yours?” The atom’s weight of the previous verse will not be weighed in private only. There will be witnesses. Every community will have a witness, the messenger sent to them, or the testimony of the truth that reached them. Then the Prophet Muhammad will be brought as a witness regarding his own community. “Against yours” means a testimony about them: who accepted, who delivered, who hid, who invented, who obeyed, who only used the name.\n\nThe question “how will it be” is meant to create awe. If a small lie is heavy now, what will the Day be like when messengers stand and the last Prophet testifies? A person who felt safe because his town praised him will see a different witness. A person who felt unknown will find that he was never unknown.\n\nThis also honours the Prophet’s role without making him a lord. He is a witness, as earlier scriptures made their prophets witnesses. He does not own the judgment. He testifies, and Allah judges. The last chapter said he had no say in whether Allah turns to wrongdoers or punishes them. This verse agrees. His honour is testimony, not a share in the throne.\n\nFor his community the verse is a responsibility. If he will be a witness, then the Book he left, the prayer he taught, and the limits he conveyed will be present. No one will say, “We never heard.” The next verse will describe the wish of those who denied Allah and disobeyed the Messenger: they will wish they were reduced to dust, and they will never be able to hide anything from Allah.",
  "42": "“On that Day, those who denied ˹Allah˺ and disobeyed the Messenger will wish they were reduced to dust.” The witness scene of the previous verse becomes unbearable for some people. Denial of Allah and disobedience of the Messenger, which looked like independence in the world, become a wish for non-existence. “Reduced to dust” means they will want to be nothing: not raised, not addressed, not seen. Dust has no shame and no Fire. They will envy the earth they once walked on proudly.\n\nThe pairing is important. Some deny Allah with the tongue. Some claim belief and still disobey the Messenger as if his teaching were optional. Both arrive at the same wish. The chapter has been teaching obedience to Allah and His Messenger in inheritance, in marriage, and in the limits. This verse shows the end of treating that obedience as a light thing.\n\n“And they will never be able to hide anything from Allah.” The wish to become dust is also a wish to hide. It fails. Faces, records, hands, and witnesses will not allow a cover. Earlier they hid rage, hid pledges, hid an orphan’s account, hid a sale of the Book. On that Day hiding is finished. The last chapter said they will never be able to hide anything from Allah in similar language of exposure. This verse applies it to the people of denial and disobedience.\n\nA listener should let this wish of the doomed become a teacher now, while hiding is still possible to abandon. The next verse will bring the believers back to a daily gate of honesty before Allah: do not approach prayer while intoxicated, nor in full impurity, and if water cannot be found, purify with clean earth. Prayer is training for a Day when nothing is hidden.",
  "43": "“Do not approach prayer while intoxicated until you are aware of what you say.” The believers are pulled from the terror of the Day to the honesty of the prayer they are about to enter. Intoxication and prayer cannot share one tongue. A person must know what he is saying to his Lord. This verse came when drink had not yet been totally forbidden, and it already closed the prayer to the drunk. Later revelation would forbid intoxicants altogether. The step here is still a teacher: if you cannot stand before Allah with a clear mind, you are not ready to speak to Him.\n\n“nor in a state of ˹full˺ impurity—unless you merely pass through ˹the mosque˺—until you have bathed.” Full impurity requires a bath before prayer. Passing through the mosque without sitting for prayer is excepted, so that the house of prayer is not made a trap. The aim is readiness, not the humiliation of people.\n\n“But if you are ill, on a journey, or have relieved yourselves, or been intimate with your wives and cannot find water, then purify yourselves with clean earth, wiping your faces and hands.” This is the lightness promised a few verses ago. Illness, travel, lack of water, and the ordinary needs of the body are not used to cancel prayer. Clean earth becomes a means of purification: a wipe of face and hands. The worshipper is not told to abandon the prayer or to harm himself hunting water.\n\n“And Allah is Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving.” He pardons the hardship and the times when people cannot do the full form. He forgives the slips of a weak creation. Prayer remains. The method is eased.\n\nThe next verses will turn again to people given a portion of the Scriptures who trade it for misguidance and wish to see the believers deviate. Allah knows the enemies. He is sufficient as Guardian and Helper.",
  "44": "“Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who were given a portion of the Scriptures yet trade it for misguidance and wish to see you deviate from the ˹Right˺ Path?” The Prophet is asked to look, not because he has not noticed, but because the pattern must be named. They were given “a portion of the Scriptures,” a real share of revelation, not emptiness. Then they traded it. The goods they bought were misguidance: a crooked reading, a hidden verse, a legal trick, a proud refusal of the confirming messenger. A trade requires that they judged misguidance more profitable than the portion they had.\n\n“and wish to see you deviate from the Right Path.” Their trade is not a private loss. They want company. This returns to the people of desire who wished to see the believers deviate entirely, and to the People of the Book who tried to make the way look crooked. Envy and fear of losing leadership turn a person who had Scripture into a recruiter for error.\n\n“A portion” is also a warning. Having some knowledge is not the same as being guided. A portion can be used against the rest of the Book. The last chapter said they mix truth with falsehood and hide the truth knowingly. This verse says the same thing as a trade.\n\nThe Prophet is not being told to panic. The next verse will say Allah knows best who the enemies are, and that Allah is sufficient as a Guardian and Helper. The right path does not become less right because people with old pages want it bent. The duty is to see the wish clearly and not walk into it, as the believers were told not to yield after Uhud.",
  "45": "“Allah knows best who your enemies are!” After naming people who trade Scripture for misguidance and wish the Prophet to deviate, Allah takes the work of identifying enemies out of panic and guesswork. He knows best. Some enemies smile, quote a Book, and offer advice. Some look like strangers. Human eyes can reverse the two. The Prophet, and the believers after him, are being told not to let appearances write the list.\n\nThis does not command hatred of every person of the Book. This chapter and the last have praised those who believe, humble themselves, and refuse to sell revelation. “Your enemies” are those who work for deviation, not every neighbour with an older Scripture.\n\n“And Allah is sufficient as a Guardian, and He is sufficient as a Helper.” The last chapter said, “But no! Allah is your Guardian, and He is the best Helper.” The wording returns because the fear returns. When enemies have knowledge, history, and a wish to make you deviate, a person may think he needs their approval or a clever counter-plot. Sufficient means you already have enough in Allah: enough protection, enough help, enough knowledge of who is who.\n\nGuardian and Helper cover the two needs of a threatened community. A guardian keeps you from being owned. A helper turns the day when you cannot. If He is sufficient in both roles, then yielding in order to be safer is a bad bargain.\n\nThe next verse will show one method of those enemies among some Jews: taking words out of context, twisting greetings, and playing with speech in order to discredit the faith. Better words were available. They chose the crooked ones, so most of them do not believe except for a few.",
  "46": "“Some Jews take words out of context and say, ‘We listen and we disobey,’ ‘Hear! May you never hear,’ and ‘Râ’ina!’ [Herd us!]—playing with words and discrediting the faith.” The enemies just mentioned are now heard. “Some” remains the Quran’s fair word. Not all. These take words out of context: they pull a phrase from its place so that it dies or turns ugly. Then they use speech that sounds, on the surface, like address to the Prophet, while hiding an insult. “We listen and we disobey” is open contempt. “Hear! May you never hear” is a prayer against him dressed as a call. “Râ’ina,” which could be twisted to a meaning of herding or mockery, is word-play meant to hurt the faith while leaving a polite excuse.\n\n“Had they said ˹courteously˺, ‘We hear and obey,’ ‘Listen to us,’ and ‘Unẓurna,’ [Tend to us!] it would have been better for them and more proper.” Better words existed in their own mouths. “We hear and obey” is the sentence of believers in the previous chapter. “Listen to us” and a respectful “tend to us” would have been proper requests. They did not lack language. They lacked a will to honour.\n\n“Allah has condemned them for their disbelief, so they do not believe except for a few.” The word-play is not treated as a small rudeness. It is disbelief expressing itself through the tongue. Condemnation follows. Yet “except for a few” keeps the door that this Book will not close. Some do believe. The last chapter ended by praising them.\n\nThe next verse will address the people of the Book directly. Believe in what We have revealed, confirming your own Scriptures, before faces are wiped out and turned backwards, or the defiant are condemned as the Sabbath-breakers were. Allah’s command is always executed.",
  "47": "“O you who were given the Book! Believe in what We have revealed—confirming your own Scriptures.” The address is honourable even in warning. They are people given the Book. The new revelation is not introduced as a stranger. It confirms their own Scriptures. This has been the Quran’s repeated claim: the last Book does not arrive to erase every truth they had, but to confirm it and to correct what was hidden or twisted. Belief, then, is loyalty to their own light, not treason against it.\n\n“before We wipe out ˹your˺ faces, turning them backwards, or We condemn the defiant as We did to the Sabbath-breakers.” Two images of judgment are given so that delay will not feel safe. Wiping out faces and turning them backwards is a picture of disgrace: identity reversed, honour removed, direction lost. The Sabbath-breakers are a known story in the previous chapter of the Cow: people who used tricks to hunt on a day they had been told to honour, then were condemned. Defiance dressed as cleverness is still defiance. “And Allah’s command is always executed!” He does not announce such things as empty thunder.\n\nThe verse should be heard beside the previous one. If some already play with words in order to discredit the faith, they are walking toward the fate of people who played with the Sabbath. Confirmation of their Scriptures is still being offered. The few who believe are still honoured. The command to believe is still an open door. What is not open is the idea that they can mock, delay, and remain untouched. Allah’s command is executed.\n\nThe next verse will state a foundation of this whole Book. Allah does not forgive associating others with Him in worship, but He forgives anything else of whoever He wills. Associating others with Him is a grave sin. The people of the Book, and the believers, are both being called back to that unshared right of Allah.",
  "48": "“Indeed, Allah does not forgive associating others with Him ˹in worship˺, but forgives anything else of whoever He wills.” After calling the people of the Book to believe in what confirms their Scriptures, the Quran states the line that no community may blur. Associating others with Allah in worship is the grave sin He has said He does not forgive if a person dies upon it. “Does not forgive” here means He has not promised pardon for that crime when it is carried to death without repentance. “But forgives anything else of whoever He wills” opens a huge door. Sins beneath that association—even great ones—are under His will to forgive. The last chapter said He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills, and that He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. This verse names the exception that remains a warning to every proud heart.\n\nAssociating others with Him is not only bowing to a statue. It is giving any created being a right that belongs to Allah alone: worship, final trust, or the claim that someone besides Him owns benefit and harm. The people who took rabbis, monks, or the Messiah as lords besides Allah were warned of this in other passages. The believers are warned too. A slogan of monotheism does not protect a person who then calls on other lords.\n\n“And whoever associates others with Allah has indeed committed a grave sin.” Grave means it is not a small stain. It reverses the purpose of creation, which is to worship the one Lord who made the single soul and its mate. The next verses will show smaller and related crimes: people who elevate themselves, fabricate lies against Allah, and even use a portion of Scripture to praise idols and to reassure disbelievers that they are better guided than the believers. All of that grows from not giving Allah His unshared right.",
  "49": "“Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who ˹falsely˺ elevate themselves?” The grave sin of associating partners often travels with a smaller, uglier sister: self-exaltation. Some people declare themselves pure, chosen, safe, or too noble to need the last messenger. The verse calls that elevation false. A human being does not climb into honour by announcing it. The last chapter condemned people who rejoice in misdeeds and love to take credit for what they have not done. This verse looks at the same face from another side.\n\n“It is Allah who elevates whoever He wills.” Real honour is a gift. He raised prophets, made some servants righteous, and brought a final messenger from a people others looked down on. Envy of that gift was already named in the last chapter. Self-elevation is the other error: treating honour as something you can award yourself with lineage, ritual, or a claim of purity.\n\n“And none will be wronged ˹even by the width of˺ the thread of a date stone.” The thread of a date stone is a tiny line. No one will lose even that much of what they deserve, and no one will be given a false honour that steals another’s right. The atom’s weight of a few verses earlier is now a date-thread. The meaning is one. Allah’s scale is finer than the boasts of people who elevate themselves.\n\nSo the Prophet is told to look at them, not to envy them and not to fear their claim of purity. The next verse will say, see how they fabricate lies against Allah—this alone is a blatant sin. Calling oneself elevated, or calling Allah what He did not say, is not a small error of wording. It is a lie about God.",
  "50": "“See how they fabricate lies against Allah—this alone is a blatant sin.” The self-exalting people of the previous verse do not only praise themselves. They speak about Allah what He did not say. They may claim He will not punish them, that they are His children, that He forbade what He did not forbid, or that He is pleased with their refusal of the confirming Book. Fabrication means the lie is made, not found. It is built and then hung on Allah’s name.\n\n“This alone is a blatant sin.” Even if no other crime were added, lying about Allah would be enough to name them sinners in the open. The last chapter said whoever still fabricates lies about Allah after being invited to evidence is among the true wrongdoers. This verse is shorter and sharper. Look. See. The sin is not hidden.\n\nThe command “see” trains the Prophet and the listener not to be impressed by religious speech. Not every sentence that contains Allah’s name is honour. Some sentences are crimes. A blatant sin can wear the robe of a sermon.\n\nThis also returns to the orphan, the wife, and the heir. People fabricate lies about Allah to swallow rights: “He allowed us to take this,” “He prefers us,” “He will not ask.” The chapter began by saying He is Watchful. A blatant lie does not change His watch.\n\nThe next verse will show one of the strangest of those lies in action. People given a portion of the Scriptures still believe in idols and false gods, and they reassure the disbelievers that they are better guided than the believers. Scripture in the hand, and praise for idols on the tongue: that is fabrication walking.",
  "51": "“Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who were given a portion of the Scriptures yet believe in idols and false gods and reassure the disbelievers that they are better guided than the believers?” The shock in the question is intended. A portion of the Scriptures should have killed idol-belief, not fed it. Yet some who had that portion still inclined to idols and false gods, then used their religious standing to comfort pagans: you are better guided than the Muslims. Early reports mentioned certain Medinan figures who, in their hatred of the Prophet, praised the religion of Mecca’s idols over the faith of those who said, “There is no god worthy of worship except Allah.” Whether one story or several, the wording is plain. Hatred can make a person of the Book a spokesman for idols.\n\n“Reassure the disbelievers” is a moral collapse. Instead of calling idol-worshippers away from false gods, they pat them on the back and rank them above people who worship Allah alone. The last chapter said some wished to make the way look crooked. This verse shows a deeper crookedness: calling the crooked straight.\n\n“A portion of the Scriptures” again warns that partial knowledge can be turned into a weapon. They had enough pages to know that idols are nothing. They used their status, not their pages.\n\nThe next verse will state the result. It is they who have been condemned by Allah. And whoever is condemned by Allah will have no helper. The reassurance they gave to disbelievers will not become a reassurance for themselves. The helpers they tried to win among the enemies of the Prophet will not stand when Allah’s condemnation arrives.",
  "52": "“It is they who have been condemned by Allah.” The people who had Scripture, then praised idols and ranked disbelievers above believers, are not described as having made a clever alliance. They are described as condemned. Condemnation here is expulsion from Allah’s mercy if they die on that path. It matches earlier sentences about people who hide the Book, kill prophets, and trade revelation for misguidance.\n\n“And whoever is condemned by Allah will have no helper.” The chapter has already used this emptiness of helpers for those who die in disbelief and for wrongdoers committed to the Fire. Now it is attached to this particular betrayal. They sought helpers among the disbelievers by flattering them. The verse says the only help that matters is gone. If Allah has condemned a person, no tribe, no idol, no ally in Mecca or Medina can reverse the sentence.\n\nThis should also caution the believers. Do not panic when a learned opponent joins your enemy and says the enemy is better guided. That speech is not a sign that you have lost Allah. It may be a sign that the speaker has.\n\nThe next verse will mock the idea that such people control the kingdom. Do they have control over shares of the kingdom? If so, they would not give anyone even the speck on a date stone. Their stinginess and their claims of superiority are being exposed together. They do not own the gifts they envy, and they would not share them if they did.",
  "53": "“Do they have control over shares of the kingdom? If so, they would not have given anyone so much as the speck on a date stone.” The question is sarcastic in the service of truth. These people speak as if honour, prophecy, and guidance were theirs to distribute. Do they control shares of Allah’s kingdom? If the keys were in their hands, their known stinginess would appear. They would not give another person even the tiniest speck on a date stone. The date stone’s speck, like the date-thread two verses earlier, is a picture of something almost nothing. They would withhold even that.\n\nThe verse unmasks envy dressed as religion. When they say the disbelievers are better guided, or that the last messenger cannot be true because he did not come from them, they are not defending Scripture. They are defending a monopoly they do not actually own. The kingdom belongs to Allah alone, as the last chapter repeated. Shares of prophecy, knowledge, and provision are His to give.\n\nThis also returns to the command not to crave what Allah has given some over others, and to ask Him for His bounties. The envious want the store to remain closed to everyone outside their circle. Allah opened it to the descendants of Abraham in a wider way than they wanted. The next verse will say that plainly. Do they envy the people for Allah’s bounties? We have given the descendants of Abraham the Book and wisdom, along with great authority.",
  "54": "“Or do they envy the people for Allah’s bounties?” The inner engine is named. Not scholarship. Not care for evidence. Envy. “The people” here are the Prophet and those given what he was given: revelation, honour, and a community. The last chapter said many among the People of the Book wished to turn the believers back because of envy after the truth had become clear. This verse asks the same question in a sharper form. Is the problem that they want Allah’s gifts not to fall where they fell?\n\n“Indeed, We have given the descendants of Abraham the Book and wisdom, along with great authority.” The gift they envy is not a theft from Abraham’s house. It is a continuation of Abraham’s house. Book, wisdom, and great authority were given to Abraham’s descendants again and again: to the line of Israel, and then to another branch of that same family through Ishmael in the person of Muhammad. “Great authority” includes prophethood, a law, and a standing among people. Envy of that is envy of Allah’s old habit of giving.\n\nThe verse should humble both sides. Those who envy are told they are fighting Abraham’s own blessing. Those who receive the blessing are told it is given, not self-made. The descendants of Abraham are not a racial club that owns God. They are a family through whom God sent Books.\n\nThe next verse will admit that the gift never forced every descendant to believe. Some believed in him while others turned away. Hell is sufficient as a torment. Lineage, even Abrahamic lineage, does not replace the choice to accept the messenger.",
  "55": "“Yet some believed in him while others turned away from him.” The bounty given to Abraham’s descendants did not produce one response. Some believed in the messenger—first in the messengers sent to them, and in this setting especially in Muhammad. Others turned away. The split is the same split this Book always records: the Book arrives, then people divide after knowledge, often by envy. Being from Abraham’s family is not the deciding fact. Belief is.\n\n“Him” in the flow of the passage is the one through whom the latest bounty came, the Prophet whose honour they envied. Some of the People of the Book believed, as the last chapter said near its end. Others turned away and even praised idols to hurt him.\n\n“Hell is sufficient as a torment!” The sentence is short because no extra threat is needed. For the one who turns away after Book, wisdom, and clear authority, Hell is enough. They do not need a larger warning invented by men. The sufficient torment matches the sufficient Guardian and Helper given to the Prophet. He has enough protection. They will have enough punishment if they persist.\n\nThe next verse will describe that torment in a way meant to end any light treatment of rejecting the signs. Those who reject Our signs will be cast into the Fire. Whenever their skin is burnt completely, it will be replaced so they will constantly taste the punishment. Allah is Almighty, All-Wise. The envy that seemed like a political stance is being shown its true end.",
  "56": "“Surely those who reject Our signs, We will cast them into the Fire.” The turning away of the previous verse is now named as rejection of signs. Signs include the verses of the Quran, the proofs in the messenger’s life, and the confirming marks in the older Books. Rejection is not a small difference of taste. It is a choice that leads to being cast into the Fire.\n\n“Whenever their skin is burnt completely, We will replace it so they will ˹constantly˺ taste the punishment.” The wording is severe because the crime is treating Allah’s signs as something to be thrown away. The point of replaced skin is not a love of gore. It is that the tasting does not end. A body that was destroyed once would stop feeling. The verse says the feeling is not allowed to stop for those who die upon this rejection. They will constantly taste. The last chapter said the punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be delayed from it. This verse explains that unlightened tasting in a physical picture.\n\n“Indeed, Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.” Almighty means no one can stop this repayment. All-Wise means it is not wild cruelty. It is the wise answer to a proud, knowing rejection after signs. The same Lord who multiplies a good deed by grace also repays a chosen denial in full.\n\nThe next verse will set the opposite home beside this Fire, as the Quran so often does. Those who believe and do good will be admitted into Gardens, forever, with pure spouses, under a vast shade. The two ends are being shown so that envy, self-elevation, and the praise of idols cannot pretend they are only “another opinion.”",
  "57": "“As for those who believe and do good, We will admit them into Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever.” After the constant tasting of people who reject the signs, the verse names the other admission. Belief and good deeds travel together. The Gardens, the rivers, and the lasting stay have been promised throughout these two chapters. “For ever and ever” stretches the stay so that no one thinks of Paradise as a long visit that might still end in fear.\n\n“There they will have pure spouses, and We will place them under a vast shade.” Purity in companionship is part of the reward. The marriages of this world can be stained by betrayal, harshness, or the old cruelties this chapter has been forbidding. The companionship of the garden is cleaned of that. “Vast shade” is rest after a life of heat: the heat of tests, of hurtful words, of Uhud, of envy, of carrying trusts. Shade in the Quran is a picture of ease that does not suffocate. It is wide.\n\nThis verse also answers the envious. They wanted the bounty to remain inside a small circle. Allah admits to the garden on the basis of faith and good, not on the basis of who resented whom. Some of Abraham’s descendants believed and will find this shade. Some turned away and will find the Fire sufficient.\n\nThe next verse will bring the listener back from the two homes to a command that decides, in daily life, which road a person is on: return trusts to their rightful owners, and when you judge between people, judge with fairness. That commandment is called noble. Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.",
  "58": "“Indeed, Allah commands you to return trusts to their rightful owners.” After Heaven and Hell, a very earthy command arrives, because the road to those homes runs through trusts. A trust is anything placed in your care: property, a secret, an office, a ruling, the orphan’s field, the keys of a public door, even a word you were told to deliver. “Return” means give it back, do not swallow it, do not delay it until it dies. “Rightful owners” means the ones it belongs to, not the ones you prefer.\n\nEarly reports mentioned this verse when the keys of the Kaaba were returned to their keeper after the opening of Mecca. The wording is larger than one key. The whole chapter has been about trusts: women’s dowries, orphans’ wealth, pledged shares, truthful witness. This sentence is the heading over them all.\n\n“and when you judge between people, judge with fairness.” The second command is public justice. Judging is itself a trust. Fairness means the orphan and the chief, the Muslim and the non-Muslim, the relative and the stranger, stand on one rule. The previous chapter said Allah loves those who are mindful. A mindful judge fears the Watcher more than he fears the powerful man in the room.\n\n“What a noble commandment from Allah to you! Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.” The commandment is called noble so that no one treats honesty and fair judgment as small ethics beneath “real religion.” Hearing and seeing mean a quiet bribe and a whispered threat are already in the record.\n\nThe next verse will tell the believers whom to obey in living out this nobility: Allah, the Messenger, and those in authority among you. If you disagree, refer the matter back to Allah and His Messenger. That is the best and fairest resolution.",
  "59": "“O believers! Obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you.” A community that returns trusts and judges fairly still needs a chain of obedience, or every person will become his own court. Obey Allah. Obey the Messenger. Then obey those in authority among you: leaders, judges, and people charged with public command in the known good. The wording “among you” matters. Authority here is not an idol from outside the faith, and it is not a tyrant who replaces revelation. It is leadership inside the believing community.\n\nObedience to authority is not a second religion. The Prophet taught that there is no obedience to a creature in disobedience to the Creator. This verse assumes that those in authority are being obeyed in what does not cancel Allah and His Messenger. The next sentences make that clear.\n\n“Should you disagree on anything, then refer it to Allah and His Messenger, if you truly believe in Allah and the Last Day.” Disagreement is expected. The method is not a fist and not a walk to a false judge. Refer it to Allah’s Book and to the Messenger’s teaching. After his death, that referral remains: the Quran and his established way. “If you truly believe in Allah and the Last Day” makes the method a test of faith. A person who only obeys when the ruling serves him is not doing this verse.\n\n“This is the best and fairest resolution.” Best in outcome, fairest in justice. The noble commandment of returning trusts and judging fairly is protected by this referral. The next verse will expose people who claim to believe in the new revelation and the old, then seek the judgment of false judges they were commanded to reject. Satan wants them farther away.",
  "60": "“Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who claim they believe in what has been revealed to you and what was revealed before you? They seek the judgment of false judges, which they were commanded to reject.” The claim is complete-looking: we believe in this Book and in the earlier Books. The action betrays it. When a dispute comes, they go to false judges: people or powers who judge by desire, custom, or a law they were told to leave. In the Prophet’s city this included hypocrites who preferred a soothsayer, a tribal arbiter, or an enemy of the Prophet when they thought he would not give them what they wanted.\n\n“Which they were commanded to reject.” They are not experimenting with a new idea. They are doing what they were told not to do. Belief that can be dropped at the courtroom door is a claim, not a faith. The previous verse said true belief refers disputes to Allah and His Messenger. This verse shows the opposite referral.\n\n“And Satan ˹only˺ desires to lead them farther away.” The false judge is not a neutral tool. Satan wants distance: farther from the Messenger, farther from the Book, farther from the Straight Path. Each time they choose a crooked court, the next choice becomes easier. The last chapter said Satan tries to make you fear his followers. Here he tries to make you prefer his courts.\n\nThe next verse will describe their bodies when they are called to the right court. When it is said, “Come to Allah’s revelations and to the Messenger,” you see the hypocrites turn away stubbornly. The claim of belief cannot walk the few steps to the Prophet.",
  "61": "“When it is said to them, ‘Come to Allah’s revelations and to the Messenger,’ you see the hypocrites turn away from you stubbornly.” The test is simple. They are invited, not hunted. “Come” is an opening. The destination is Allah’s revelations and the Messenger, the very referral the believers were just commanded to make. What is seen is a turning away. Not a thoughtful pause. A stubborn turning.\n\n“You see” makes the hypocrisy visible. For a time it lived in secret oaths and private courts. At the moment of invitation it shows in the neck, the feet, the face. The last chapter described people who said “We believe” when they met the believers, then bit their fingers in rage when alone. This verse is the public version of that rage: they cannot bear to walk toward the man they claim to believe in.\n\nStubbornly means the turning is a decision, not a confusion. They know where justice has been placed. They prefer a judge who will serve the desire. That is why the previous verse said Satan wants them farther away. Each stubborn turn is a step.\n\nThis also teaches the believers not to be shocked when a person of many religious sentences refuses a short walk toward the Book. The refusal is the lesson. A faith that cannot come when it is called is the faith of a claim.\n\nThe next verse will ask how horrible it will be if a disaster strikes them because of what their hands have done, and then they come swearing, “We intended nothing but goodwill and reconciliation.” The same mouths that would not come to the Messenger will come later with decorated excuses when fear arrives.",
  "62": "“How ˹horrible˺ will it be if a disaster strikes them because of what their hands have done, then they come to you swearing by Allah, ‘We intended nothing but goodwill and reconciliation.’” The stubborn turning of the previous verse does not last as bravery. When a disaster comes—exposure, defeat, a ruling that hurts, a fear of punishment—they hurry to the Prophet they had avoided. Their hands made the disaster: the false court, the betrayal, the plot. Then the tongue tries to clean the hands.\n\n“Swearing by Allah” shows how cheap an oath can become. They use the Name the first verse of this chapter told people to honour. “We intended nothing but goodwill and reconciliation.” The excuse is chosen to sound almost pious. Who can attack goodwill? Who can attack reconciliation? The Quran places the sentence in their mouths so the listener will recognise it later. Not every call for peace is honest. Some are a cover after a crime.\n\nThis is horrible because it joins three things: a sin, a disaster that reveals it, and a lie about motive. The hypocrite wants the fruit of betrayal and the reputation of a peacemaker. The last chapter said some people love to take credit for what they have not done. Here they take credit for a good intention they did not have.\n\nThe next verse will tell the Prophet what to do with such hearts. Only Allah knows what is in them. So turn away from them, caution them, and give them advice that will shake their very souls. He is not told to believe the oath. He is told to speak in a way that reaches the hidden place.",
  "63": "“˹Only˺ Allah knows what is in their hearts.” After the decorated oath of goodwill, the Prophet is forbidden to build his policy on their words. Hearts are Allah’s territory. The claim of reconciliation may be true in one person and false in another. The Prophet does not have to pretend he can open chests. He is told the limit of his knowledge and the sufficiency of Allah’s.\n\n“So turn away from them, caution them, and give them advice that will shake their very souls.” Three actions, carefully mixed. Turn away: do not sit in their schemes, do not make them intimates, do not let their false court become yours. Caution them: do not ignore them as if their hypocrisy were harmless. Give them advice that will shake their souls: speak the kind of reminder that reaches fear, hope, death, and the Day of witnesses. A light social rebuke will not do. Their souls are asleep inside oaths. They need words with weight.\n\nThis is neither softness that believes every swear, nor a cruelty that refuses to advise. The last chapter told the Prophet he had been made lenient by mercy, and that if he had been hard-hearted they would have left. That mercy still includes a shaking warning. Pardon and consultation were for believers who had slipped. This shaking advice is for people whose mouths and hearts do not match.\n\nThe next verse will open, even for them, a road back. Messengers are sent to be obeyed. If those hypocrites came to the Prophet after wronging themselves, sought Allah’s forgiveness, and the Messenger prayed for them, they would find Allah ever Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful. The shaking is meant to send them down that road.",
  "64": "“We only sent messengers to be obeyed by Allah’s Will.” The whole argument about false judges is gathered into one sentence. A messenger is not a decoration, a storyteller, or a mascot. He is sent to be obeyed. “By Allah’s Will” means that obedience to the messenger is part of Allah’s religion, not a later human extra. The previous chapter said Muhammad is no more than a messenger. This verse says that “no more” still includes a right to be obeyed. He is not a god. He is not optional either.\n\n“If only those ˹hypocrites˺ came to you ˹O Prophet˺—after wronging themselves—seeking Allah’s forgiveness and the Messenger prayed for their forgiveness, they would have certainly found Allah ever Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful.” The road is described step by step so no one can say it was unclear. They have wronged themselves. They come to the Prophet. They seek Allah’s forgiveness. The Messenger prays for them. The result is certain: they would find Allah Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful. The same names that closed the verse about the two who sinned are offered to hypocrites if they will actually come.\n\n“If only” is grief and invitation together. The door is real. Their stubborn turning is what keeps it unused. They prefer a false judge, then a false oath, to a walk of repentance.\n\nThe next verse will raise the definition of belief itself. By your Lord, they will never be true believers until they accept the Prophet as the judge in their disputes, find no resistance in themselves against his decision, and submit wholeheartedly. Coming for forgiveness and coming for judgment are two sides of one surrender.",
  "65": "“But no! By your Lord, they will never be ˹true˺ believers until they accept you ˹O Prophet˺ as the judge in their disputes.” Allah swears by Himself, the Lord of the Prophet, that a claim of faith is empty while the heart refuses the Prophet’s judgment. “Until” sets a condition, not a cruelty. They may become true believers. They are not so now if they run to other judges. Accepting him as judge means bringing the case to him and recognising his ruling as the ruling that binds.\n\n“and find no resistance within themselves against your decision and submit wholeheartedly.” The verse goes inside the chest. Outer obedience with inner rage is not the faith being defined. “No resistance” means the soul does not remain in argument after the decision. “Submit wholeheartedly” means the surrender is complete, not a polite pause until they can appeal to a false judge again. This is the same submission the last chapter named as Islam: to Him we fully submit.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat this as worship of the Prophet. The judge is a messenger judging by what Allah showed him. Refusing him is refusing the One who sent him. Another misunderstanding is to think any later ruler inherits this inner status by himself. The verse is about accepting the Messenger’s decision. Later authority is obeyed, as verse 59 said, when it refers back to Allah and His Messenger.\n\nThe next verses will expose how thin their claimed obedience is. If they had been commanded to sacrifice themselves or abandon their homes, only a few would have obeyed. Had they done what they were advised, it would have been better and more reassuring, and they would have been granted a great reward and the Straight Path.",
  "66": "“If We had commanded them to sacrifice themselves or abandon their homes, none would have obeyed except for a few.” The people who cannot accept the Prophet as a judge in a dispute are now measured against heavier commands. Even if Allah had ordered them to give their lives or to leave their houses—as some earlier communities were tested, and as the migrants in this community actually did—only a few of these claimants would have obeyed. The verse unmasks them. They will not even bring a quarrel to the Messenger. How then would they leave a home or a life?\n\n“Except for a few” honours the few. In every hypocritical crowd there may be people who would still rise if the command became that hard. The Quran does not erase them.\n\n“Had they done what they were advised to do, it would have certainly been far better for them and more reassuring.” What they were advised to do, in this passage, is the nearer thing: come to Allah’s revelations and the Messenger, accept his judgment, repent after wronging themselves. Doing that would have been far better than the false court and the false oath. It would also have been “more reassuring.” Their inner life is not calm. Hypocrisy is agitation: fear of exposure, fear of loss, fear of the next command. True referral to the Prophet would have settled them.\n\nThe sentence continues into the next verse. We would have granted them a great reward by Our grace, and guided them to the Straight Path. They think that coming to the Messenger is a loss of advantage. Allah says the unused path was better, calmer, rewarded, and guided. The bargain they chose is the worse one in every measure.",
  "67": "“and We would have granted them a great reward by Our grace” completes the “if” begun in the previous verse. Had those who turned from the Messenger done what they were advised to do, they would not only have found inner reassurance. They would have been given a great reward. “By Our grace” keeps the reward from looking like a tight wage. Grace means Allah would have given more than their small walk of obedience strictly earned. They refused a gift, not only a duty.\n\nThe great reward in this Book has already been named in more than one form: a place of honour, Gardens, multiplied good, forgiveness. Here it is attached even to people whose present state is hypocrisy, if they will do what they were told. That is how wide the unused mercy is.\n\nThis short verse should be read as the middle of one sentence. They would have been reassured, rewarded, and—as the next verse adds—guided to the Straight Path. Three losses follow their stubborn turning: no calm, no reward, no guidance. Three gifts sit waiting on the other side of a single decision: come to the Messenger.\n\nA listener who feels himself delaying a known duty should hear his own name in this grammar. The reward is described in the past-unreal: We would have granted. Meaning, the grant is ready in Allah’s grace, and the person is the one who has not stepped onto it. The next verse will name the third gift, guidance to the Straight Path, and then describe the company of those who do obey: prophets, people of truth, martyrs, and the righteous. What honourable company. That is the replacement for the false judges and the flattering disbelievers.",
  "68": "“and guided them to the Straight Path” finishes the gifts that those hypocrites would have received if they had done what they were advised. The previous verse promised a great reward by grace. This verse adds guidance. Reward without guidance could be imagined as a payment after a dark life. Guidance is the light for the road itself. The Straight Path is the same path asked for in the Opening chapter and described throughout this Book: worship of Allah alone, obedience to the Messenger, justice in trusts, and refusal of false judges.\n\nThe grammar is still part of the “if.” They do not have this guidance while they turn away. They would have had it. That makes their stubbornness heavier. They are not people who never received an offer. They are people who left a guided road unused.\n\nGuidance here is not a vague good mood. It is being put on the path and kept on it. The last chapter said whoever holds firmly to Allah is surely guided to the Straight Path. Coming to the Messenger is one form of that holding. Turning to a false judge is one form of letting go.\n\nThe next verse will show where that path ends for those who do obey. Whoever obeys Allah and the Messenger will be in the company of the prophets, the people of truth, the martyrs, and the righteous. What honourable company. The lonely hypocrite who feared losing a case would have gained the best companionship in existence. That is the trade he refused.",
  "69": "“And whoever obeys Allah and the Messenger will be in the company of those blessed by Allah: the prophets, the people of truth, the martyrs, and the righteous—what honourable company!” Obedience, which looked to the hypocrites like a loss of freedom, is now shown as an entrance into the finest society. “Whoever” is wide. Any person who obeys, man or woman, famous or unknown, is promised this company. The company is four kinds of blessed people.\n\n“The prophets” are those Allah sent. Obeying the last messenger does not remove a person from Moses, Jesus, and Abraham. It seats him in their company, because their religion was one submission. “The people of truth” are those who were honest with Allah and with people: they believed, they spoke straight, they did not mix the Book. “The martyrs” are those who gave their lives in Allah’s cause, the same people the last chapter said are alive with their Lord. “The righteous” are those whose faith became a good life. Together they are called honourable company. No false judge, no flattering idol-worshipper, no secret night-scheme can offer a gathering like this.\n\nThe verse also heals the fear of losing family or allies by obeying. A person may be abandoned by hypocrites for taking a case to the Messenger. He is not abandoned. He is being moved toward this company.\n\n“What honourable company!” is praise meant to be tasted. The next verse will say this is Allah’s favour, and that He fully knows who deserves it. The honour is not a club a person storms. It is a favour placed where He knows it belongs.",
  "70": "“This is Allah’s favour, and Allah fully knows ˹who deserves it˺.” The honourable company of prophets, people of truth, martyrs, and the righteous is not a wage a person can seize by lineage or by a loud claim of obedience. It is a favour. Favour means it comes from Allah’s generosity, even though obedience is required. The same word has been used for the sending of the Messenger himself. Being with the blessed is part of that same gift.\n\n“Allah fully knows who deserves it.” People misjudge deserts. They may think a polished hypocrite deserves honour, or that a quiet believer who only brings his dispute to the Book deserves nothing. Allah knows. He knows the one who came after wronging himself and sought forgiveness. He knows the one who said “We obey” and then spent the night undoing the sentence. The date-thread scale and the atom’s weight belong here too. No one is seated in that company by mistake, and no one is kept out by a human error.\n\nThis short verse protects the previous promise from becoming pride. If you obey and hope for that company, remember it is favour. If you see yourself as safer than others, remember He knows who deserves it. A person who treats the company of the prophets as a family inheritance has not heard this line.\n\nThe next verses will take the believers from this inner honour back to an outer caution. Take your precautions and go forth either in groups or together. Favour does not cancel prudence. The Straight Path includes tying the camel and then trusting.",
  "71": "“O believers! Take your precautions and go forth either in groups or together.” After promising honourable company to those who obey, the Quran speaks to the living community that still has to walk out of its doors. “Take your precautions” means do not treat trust in Allah as carelessness. Prepare, watch, choose the safer lawful way, and do not hand the enemy an easy opening. The last chapter taught that victory comes only from Allah, and also that the Prophet positioned the believers at dawn. This verse is that balance in one line.\n\n“go forth either in groups or together.” They may go out in smaller bands or as one body, according to the need. What is not praised is the lone, showy outing that ignores the community, or the scattered movement that lets people slip away. Groups and togetherness protect against the laggers about to be described.\n\nThe address “O believers” ties the military caution to faith. Precaution is not cowardice, and going forth is not a hunt for spoil. Both are acts of a people who obey Allah and the Messenger. The hypocrites will use every outing as material for two opposite speeches: if disaster comes, “Allah blessed us for not being there”; if bounty comes, “we wish we had been there for the gain.” The next verses will record both speeches so the believers can recognise them in any age.\n\nSo the community is told to move, but to move with its eyes open. The Straight Path is not a sleepwalk. It is a careful going forth under the Watcher who already knows who deserves the honourable company.",
  "72": "“There will be some among you who will lag behind so that if you face a disaster, they will say, ‘Allah has blessed us for not being there among them.’” The caution to go forth in groups is now explained. Inside the larger “you” there are people whose plan is to lag. They do not always announce unbelief. They delay, find an excuse, and wait to see the result. If the believers meet a disaster—a defeat, a loss of life, a hard day like Uhud—these laggers produce a pious sentence. “Allah has blessed us.” They hang Allah’s name on their absence.\n\nThe sentence is a lie about blessing. Blessing is not every escape from hardship. The last chapter said martyrdom and injury in Allah’s cause can be a choice and a mercy. To call staying behind a blessing, when the staying was from hypocrisy, is to use Allah’s name as a cover for relief at other people’s pain. This is the opposite of the believers who were distressed when good missed their brothers.\n\n“For not being there among them” shows the broken bond. They speak of the fighters as “them,” not as “us,” once blood has been spilled. The community was one when it was useful to hide. It becomes two when it is useful to survive.\n\nThe next verse will show the same people when the result is reversed. If you return with Allah’s bounties, they will speak as if there had been no bond, and they will wish they had been there to share the great gain. Disaster makes them grateful to have been absent. Profit makes them hungry to have been present. Their god is the outcome, not Allah.",
  "73": "“But if you return with Allah’s bounties, they will say—as if there had been no bond between you—‘We wish we had been there with them to share the great gain!’” The laggers’ second speech completes their portrait. When the outing ends in Allah’s bounties—safety, victory, or lawful gain—they do not say, “Allah blessed the believers.” They say, we wish we had been there to share. The wish is not for duty. It is for the gain.\n\n“As if there had been no bond between you” is a deep cut. There was a bond: faith claimed, neighbourliness, perhaps even family. In the moment of greed that bond disappears. They speak like strangers counting another tribe’s loot. The last chapter described people who grieve at your good and rejoice at your injury. These laggers do both, only in sequence, according to the news.\n\nThe two verses together are a mirror for later communities. After every public hardship and every public success, listen for the two sentences. “We were blessed not to be there.” “We wish we had been there for the share.” Both sentences can wear religion. Both are about the self.\n\nThe next verse will turn from their wish to a true reason for going out. Let those who would sacrifice this life for the Hereafter fight in the cause of Allah. Whether they meet martyrdom or victory, they will be honoured with a great reward. The laggers want only one of those two ends. The believer is told to walk toward both.",
  "74": "“Let those who would sacrifice this life for the Hereafter fight in the cause of Allah.” The people being invited are defined by a trade. They would give this life for the next. Fighting in Allah’s cause is not for the lagger who wants only the great gain, and not for the person who loves this world as his final home. It is for the one who has already compared the two lives and chosen the lasting one. “In the cause of Allah” keeps the fight from becoming a cause of pride, tribe, or spoil. The cause is His.\n\n“And whoever fights in Allah’s cause—whether they achieve martyrdom or victory—We will honour them with a great reward.” The two worldly ends of a battle are named so that neither will become the only measure. Martyrdom is not a failure. Victory is not the only success. Both are honoured with a great reward. This returns to the last chapter: whoever fights and is killed or dies in Allah’s cause finds forgiveness and mercy better than what others accumulate. It also answers the laggers. They divide days into blessed absence and desired gain. Allah divides the fighter’s end into two honoured gifts.\n\nThe great reward is from Him, as the reward by grace was from Him. A person does not have to calculate which end is more profitable. He has to be sure of the cause and of the trade he has made.\n\nThe next verse will add a human cry to this cause. Why do you not fight in the cause of Allah and for oppressed men, women, and children who ask their Lord to be delivered from a land of oppressors? The fight is not an abstract test of courage. It is also an answer to people who cannot save themselves.",
  "75": "“And what is it with you? You do not fight in the cause of Allah and for oppressed men, women, and children who cry out.” The question is a shake. What is wrong with you? The cause of Allah is now joined to a visible need: oppressed men, women, and children. The weak are named in three kinds so that no one can say the verse is only about warriors. Women and children are in the cry. The last verses of this chapter began with orphans and family ties. This verse says those weak people, when trapped in a land of oppressors, become a reason to stand.\n\n“Our Lord! Deliver us from this land of oppressors! Appoint for us a saviour; appoint for us a helper—all by Your grace.” The oppressed are heard praying. They ask Allah, not a proud chief. They ask for delivery, for a saviour, for a helper, and they attach it all to grace. That prayer makes the believers’ delay look heavier. People are calling Allah for help, and the community that says it obeys Allah is sitting.\n\nThe verse does not turn every later political quarrel into this cry. It describes people truly trapped under oppression, asking Allah for a way out. The first listeners would have heard the Muslims still stuck in Mecca, and others who had no strength to migrate. The wording remains a test whenever the weak cry and the able pretend not to hear.\n\nThe next verse will state the two banners. Believers fight for the cause of Allah. Disbelievers fight for the cause of the Devil. So fight Satan’s evil forces. His schemes are ever weak. The oppressed person’s prayer is answered, in part, by a community that knows which cause it serves.",
  "76": "“Believers fight for the cause of Allah, whereas disbelievers fight for the cause of the Devil.” The field is given its true names. Two groups may both carry weapons. They are not therefore the same. The believer’s fight, when it is truly his, is for Allah’s cause: to remove oppression, to defend the weak who cried out, to protect the worship of the one Lord. The disbeliever’s fight is for the Devil’s cause: pride, idols, domination, and the dragging of people back from faith. This verse is a measure, not a magic label a person may stick on every war he likes. If the cause is not Allah’s, the person has left the first half of the sentence.\n\n“So fight against Satan’s ˹evil˺ forces.” The enemy is described as Satan’s forces, which shrinks their myth. They may look like the main power of the earth. They are still a force serving a weak master. The last chapter said Satan tries to make you fear his followers. This verse says to fight those forces, not to live under that fear.\n\n“Indeed, Satan’s schemes are ever weak.” The schemes are real enough to mislead. They are weak before Allah. Horror can be cast into hearts. Promised help can arrive. A small group can stand. The weakness of Satan’s scheme is a reason for courage, not for sloppiness. Precautions were still commanded.\n\nThe next verse will recall a group who, when they were first told not to fight but to pray and give alms, were content, then, when fighting was later ordered, feared people as Allah should be feared, or even more. They asked why the order could not be delayed. The enjoyment of this world, they will be told, is little.",
  "77": "“Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who had been told, ‘Do not fight! Rather, establish prayer and pay alms-tax.’? Then once the order came to fight, a group of them feared those ˹hostile˺ people as Allah should be feared—or even more.” In Mecca the Muslims were told to restrain their hands and to build prayer and charity. Some were eager to fight then. When, after the migration, the order to fight arrived, a group reversed. They feared the hostile people with a fear that belongs to Allah, or with a fear even greater. That comparison is the scandal. People may be feared in the ordinary sense of caution. They must not be feared as if they, not Allah, held the final harm.\n\n“They said, ‘Our Lord! Why have You ordered us to fight? If only You had delayed the order for us for a little while!’” The request for a delay sounds polite. It is the old wish to enjoy a little more of the safe world. The last chapter recorded people who wished for martyrdom until they saw it. This verse records people who liked the religion when it was only prayer and alms, then asked for time when the cost rose.\n\n“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘The enjoyment of this world is so little, whereas the Hereafter is far better for those mindful ˹of Allah˺. And none of you will be wronged even by the width of the thread of a date stone.’” The answer does not mock their fear as if fear were unreal. It resizes the world. Enjoyment is little. The Hereafter is better for the mindful. And the date-thread of justice is still in place: no one will be cheated by being asked to fight. The next verse will add that death will overcome you wherever you are, even in fortified towers. Delay does not cancel death. It only cancels the honour of meeting it in the right cause.",
  "78": "“Wherever you may be, death will overcome you—even if you were in fortified towers.” The people who asked for a delay are answered with a fact they already know and still try to manage. Death is not waiting outside the fortress and then giving up. It overcomes. Towers, locks, and a quiet town do not cancel a written time. This returns to the last chapter: no soul dies without Allah’s will at the destined time, and even if you had remained in your homes, those destined to be killed would have met the same fate. The lagger’s “blessing” of absence is not a blessing if his day has come, and it is not a blessing if his day has not come and he has only missed the reward.\n\n“When something good befalls them, they say, ‘This is from Allah,’ but when something evil befalls them, they say, ‘This is from you.’” Their theology changes with their mood. Good: from Allah. Bad: from the Prophet. They used a similar split in Moses’ story in other passages. Here they use it against Muhammad. A hard march, a loss, a fear: they hang it on him. A gift: they hang it on Allah in a way that excludes the messenger’s truth.\n\n“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Both have been destined by Allah.’ So what is the matter with these people? They can hardly comprehend anything!” Both the good and the hard event are under Allah’s decree. The Prophet is not a second god who creates the evil days. Their lack of comprehension is not a lack of intelligence. It is a refusal to hold one Lord over both kinds of days.\n\nThe next verse will refine this for the believer. Whatever good befalls you is from Allah. Whatever evil befalls you is from yourself. The Prophet is sent as a messenger to all people, and Allah is sufficient as a Witness.",
  "79": "“Whatever good befalls you is from Allah and whatever evil befalls you is from yourself.” After saying both are destined by Allah, this verse teaches the servant how to speak about the two. Good is from Allah: gift, favour, guidance, a door opened. Evil that strikes you—meaning the bad fruit of your own sins, the wound that traces back to a choice—is “from yourself.” Destiny is not denied. Blame is placed where repentance can live. A person who says, “My sin is from myself,” can turn. A person who says, “My sin is from the Prophet, or from the stars, or from Allah’s injustice,” cannot.\n\nThis matches the last chapter: Allah never wronged them, but they wronged themselves. It also matches Uhud: the lost victory was tied to disobedience, then pardon was given. Decree and responsibility sit together. Allah destines. The servant earns.\n\n“We have sent you ˹O Prophet˺ as a messenger to ˹all˺ people. And Allah is sufficient as a Witness.” The Prophet is not the author of their evil days. He is a messenger to all people, not only to one town or race. Allah’s witness is enough to confirm that sending. Those who say “this evil is from you” are accusing a man whose only role is delivery, while the Witness of the heavens knows the truth.\n\nThe next verse will make the obedience crystal. Whoever obeys the Messenger has truly obeyed Allah. Whoever turns away should know that the Prophet was not sent as a keeper over them. He delivers. He does not drag souls by force.",
  "80": "“Whoever obeys the Messenger has truly obeyed Allah.” This sentence ties the last several verses in one knot. Seeking his judgment, coming when called, fighting in the cause he names, refusing the false court: all of that is obedience to Allah, not a second religion beside Allah. The Messenger does not compete with his Lord. He conveys Him. To split the two—Allah yes, the Messenger no—is the hypocrisy already exposed.\n\nThe wording “has truly obeyed Allah” is a comfort to people who feared that honouring the Prophet might look like taking a lord besides Allah. This chapter forbade that. It now says that obeying the Messenger, in what he brought, is exactly obedience to Allah. The two do not rival each other.\n\n“But whoever turns away, then know that We have not sent you ˹O Prophet˺ as a keeper over them.” The Prophet’s duty has a limit. He is not a jailer of souls, not a watcher who must force the turning heart, not a man who fails if someone walks off. The last chapter said his duty is to deliver. This verse says he is not sent as a keeper over those who turn away. That frees him from a grief that would crush a messenger: the grief of owning other people’s choices.\n\nIt also warns the one who turns away. You are not escaping a mere man. You are leaving the obedience of Allah, and no keeper will be blamed in your place. The next verse will show how some say “We obey” and then, at night, undo the sentence. Allah records the schemes. The Prophet is told to turn from them and trust Allah, who is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs.",
  "81": "“And they say, ‘We obey,’ but when they leave you, a group of them would spend the night contradicting what they said.” The mouth in the Prophet’s presence is easy. “We obey.” The night is honest. A group sits up and takes the sentence apart: how to avoid the outing, how to twist the ruling, how to keep a foot in both camps. Night here is secrecy. The last chapter spoke of people who bite their fingers when alone. This verse speaks of people who rewrite obedience when alone.\n\n“Allah records all their schemes.” The night is not dark to Him. Recording means the scheme is written, not lost in the air of a room. The Watcher of the first verse, the Reckoner of the orphan’s account, is the Recorder of midnight plans.\n\n“So turn away from them, and put your trust in Allah. And Allah is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs.” The Prophet is not told to become a spy in every house. He is told to turn away from their scheme-circle and to trust the One who records it. Sufficient as a Trustee means the affairs they are trying to manage in the dark are already in better hands. This matches “Allah is sufficient as a Guardian and Helper,” and “sufficient as a Witness.” The same sufficiency keeps being named because the same fear keeps returning.\n\nThe next verse will invite a different night activity: reflection on the Quran. Had it been from anyone other than Allah, they would have found many inconsistencies. The Book they leave in order to scheme is itself the proof they will not sit with.",
  "82": "“Do they not then reflect on the Quran?” The night-schemers and the stubborn turners are asked a question that is also an invitation. Reflect. Sit with the Book. The opposite of spending the night contradicting “We obey” is spending the night thinking about what was revealed. Reflection here is not hunting for a phrase to twist, as some took words out of context. It is honest thought about the whole.\n\n“Had it been from anyone other than Allah, they would have certainly found in it many inconsistencies.” This is a test the Book offers against itself. A long revelation, sent over years, in peace and war, in poverty and after victory, would break into clashing claims if it were a human patchwork. The Quran says that kind of breaking is not found. The teaching on Allah’s oneness, the stories of the prophets, the care for the weak, the warnings and the hopes, hold together. Small-minded people may imagine a clash between a verse of pardon and a verse of punishment, or between destiny and responsibility. Reflection shows they sit in one wise speech.\n\nThe verse does not forbid asking questions. It forbids the lazy conclusion that any hard place is proof of a human author. The people who will not refer a dispute to the Messenger also will not refer their doubts to patient reading.\n\nThe next verse will name another habit of a community without reflection: when they hear news of security or fear, they publicize it at once. Had they referred it to the Messenger or to those in authority, people of sound judgment would have known what to do with it. Allah’s grace is what has kept most of them from following Satan.",
  "83": "“And when they hear news of security or fear, they publicize it.” A weak community treats every rumour as a duty to announce. News of safety is spread in a way that relaxes people who should still be careful. News of fear is spread in a way that panics people who should stand. The laggers, the hypocrites, and the careless all find a use for a quick tongue. The last chapter warned that hurtful words would come. This verse warns that the believers’ own mouths can become a wound.\n\n“Had they referred it to the Messenger or their authorities, those with sound judgment among them would have validated it.” The same method given for disputes is given for news. Refer it. Do not publish first. The Messenger, or those in authority, and those among them with sound judgment, would test the report: is it true, is it to be announced, is it to be contained? Validation is a skill. Not every ear is a court.\n\n“Had it not been for Allah’s grace and mercy, you would have followed Satan—except for a few.” The verse admits how close the community lives to Satan’s wish. Panic and loose talk are his work. Allah’s grace, not their native cleverness, is why most of them have not already followed him. “Except for a few” honours people whom Allah kept even more firmly. The rest should not boast. They should refer news and thank Allah.\n\nThe next verse will tell the Prophet to fight in Allah’s cause even if he must stand as if accountable only for himself, and to motivate the believers. Perhaps Allah will curb the disbelievers’ might. Allah is far superior in might and in punishment.",
  "84": "“So fight in the cause of Allah ˹O Prophet˺. You are accountable for none but yourself.” After rumours, laggers, and people who ask for delay, the Prophet is told to stand even if he stood alone. “Accountable for none but yourself” does not cancel his duty to motivate others. It frees him from being owned by their hesitation. He does what he is commanded. Their sitting will not be written as his failure. This matches “We have not sent you as a keeper over them.”\n\n“And motivate the believers ˹to fight˺, so perhaps Allah will curb the disbelievers’ might.” His second duty is to stir those who do believe. Motivation is speech, example, and the reminder of the oppressed who cried out. “Perhaps” keeps the result in Allah’s hand. The aim named is not a thirst for ruin. It is the curbing of a might that has been used for oppression and for blocking the way of Allah.\n\n“And Allah is far superior in might and in punishment.” The disbelievers’ might, which some feared as Allah should be feared, is placed beside a greater might. Superior in punishment means no worldly force can out-punish Him, so fearing them more than Him is unreasonable as well as sinful.\n\nThe Prophet is therefore given a lonely courage and a social task at once. Stand as if you were only responsible for yourself. Still call the believers. The next verse will widen “calling” into everyday life. Whoever intercedes for a good cause shares the reward. Whoever intercedes for an evil cause shares the burden. Allah is Watchful over all things.",
  "85": "“Whoever intercedes for a good cause will have a share in the reward, and whoever intercedes for an evil cause will have a share in the burden.” Not everyone is on a battlefield. Everyone speaks for or against people. Intercession here is using your word, rank, or relationship to push a matter forward. If the cause is good—reconciling a couple as the mediators were told, defending an orphan, opening a lawful door, supporting a just peace—the speaker shares the reward. If the cause is evil—hiding a thief, crushing a weak person, helping a false judge, spreading a panic—the speaker shares the burden.\n\nThis is a merciful widening of responsibility. A person who never held a sword can still take a share of good or a share of sin with the tongue. The last verses condemned publicizing untested news. This verse praises a tongue that intercedes well.\n\n“And Allah is Watchful over all things.” The Watcher of the chapter’s first verse returns. A quiet word in a sitting, a letter, a plea to a ruler, a defence of a friend: all are watched. Share of reward and share of burden are not decided by whether the intercession succeeded in public. They are decided by the cause and by Allah’s watch.\n\nThe next verse will regulate an even smaller speech: the greeting. When you are greeted, respond with a better greeting or at least similarly. Allah is a vigilant Reckoner of all things. The religion that spoke of Hell, orphans, and war also cares how a greeting is returned. That is one of its proofs of consistency.",
  "86": "“And when you are greeted, respond with a better greeting or at least similarly.” After intercession and war, a door-greeting is given a ruling. When someone offers peace, honour, or a kind address, the answer should be better, or at least the same. Better means an increase in warmth and prayer, not an increase in pride. At least similarly forbids a colder return that humiliates the one who began well. In the Prophet’s teaching, the greeting of peace is a mark of faith. This verse protects it from becoming a tool of rank, where the strong grunt and the weak must pour honour.\n\nThe command also heals a community that has hypocrites, rumours, and fear in it. People still meet in streets. They still enter houses. If even the greeting becomes a battlefield of scorn, the whole social body sickens. A better greeting is a small intercession for a good cause.\n\n“Surely Allah is a ˹vigilant˺ Reckoner of all things.” The Reckoner counts what people think too small to count: a returned peace, a withheld peace, a greeting given to the rich and denied to the poor. All things includes this. The same Lord who records night-schemes records a smile at a door.\n\nThe next verse will close this passage with the foundation under every greeting, every fight, and every judgment. Allah, there is no god worthy of worship except Him. He will gather you on the Day of Judgment, about which there is no doubt. Whose word is more truthful than Allah’s? The Reckoner of greetings is the one God who will gather the worlds.",
  "87": "“Allah, there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him.” After trusts, wars, rumours, and greetings, the chapter plants the sentence that makes all of them serious. There is no god worthy of worship except Him. False judges, idols praised by people of Scripture, Satan’s forces, and the fear of hostile people that outruns the fear of Allah are all being broken on this line. Worship, final trust, and the right to command the unseen belong to Him alone.\n\n“He will certainly gather ˹all of˺ you together on the Day of Judgment—about which there is no doubt.” The gathering is certain. All of you: the lagger and the fighter, the oppressed child and the oppressor, the one who said “We obey” at night and the one who obeyed in the field, the one who returned a better greeting and the one who swallowed an orphan’s wealth. The last chapter said all will be gathered before Allah whether they die or are martyred. This verse says the Day itself has no doubt. Doubt is a luxury of this world. It will not survive the gathering.\n\n“And whose word is more truthful than Allah’s?” The question ends the argument. People have offered words: “Allah blessed us for not being there,” “this evil is from you,” “we intended goodwill,” “we obey.” Allah’s word has described their hearts, promised the honourable company, warned of the Fire, and sworn that He will gather them. No one’s word is truer. The earlier challenge to reflect on the Quran sits behind this question. If His word is the truest, then the Book that carries it is the place to return when news, fear, and disputes arrive.\n\nSo the believer lives between two certainties named here: no god except Him, and no doubt about the Day. Precautions, justice, and kind greetings are what those certainties look like before the gathering.",
  "88": "“Why are you ˹believers˺ divided into two groups regarding the hypocrites” asks a question that is also a rebuke. After the last verse swore that Allah will gather everyone on a Day without doubt, this verse looks at a split already happening in this world. Some believers wanted to treat a group of hypocrites as brothers who had only slipped. Others saw that those people had gone back to the enemy camp. The community was becoming two parties over people who were not two-sided by accident. They had chosen a side.\n\n“while Allah allowed them to regress ˹to disbelief˺ because of their misdeeds” names the cause. Their return to disbelief was not a mysterious fall. It was allowed because of what they themselves had done: the night schemes, the false courts, the lagging, the wish that the believers would fail. Allah left them to the road they insisted on walking. The last chapter said He does not guide the wrongdoing people while they persist. This verse is that rule applied to a known group.\n\n“Do you wish to guide those left by Allah to stray? And whoever Allah leaves to stray, you will never find for them a way.” The believers’ kindness is being guarded from becoming naivety. You may invite, advise, and hope. You cannot force a path on people Allah has left to their chosen stray. “You will never find for them a way” means no human plan can replace a guidance they have refused. It does not mean a living person is forbidden to repent. It means the community must stop tearing itself in half in order to defend those who want the believers to become like them.\n\nThe next verse will state that wish in the open. They wish you would disbelieve as they have disbelieved, so you may all be alike. That is why the split among the believers is so dangerous. One group is trying to keep a door of excuse open for people whose own wish is that the whole community leave faith.",
  "89": "“They wish you would disbelieve as they have disbelieved, so you may all be alike.” The hypocrites’ inner project is now said plainly. They do not only want safety. They want company in disbelief. If the believers fall, the hypocrites will feel less exposed. This is the same wish named in the last chapter: some People of the Book wanted the believers turned back so they would be alike. Envy and fear of standing alone produce one demand: come down to us.\n\n“So do not take them as allies unless they emigrate in the cause of Allah.” Alliance here is inner trust, military partnership, and the sharing of secrets. People who wish your disbelief cannot be taken as that kind of ally. The exception is real emigration in Allah’s cause: leaving the enemy camp, joining the believing community, and proving the wish has died. Words in a sitting are not emigration.\n\n“But if they turn away, then seize them and kill them wherever you find them, and do not take any of them as allies or helpers.” This is wartime law for people who have shown themselves as enemies: they leave the believers, join those who fight them, and still want access to the community’s trust. “Turn away” in this flow is not a private difference of opinion. It is a turn back to the hostile camp. Seizing and killing is the language of a battlefield against such people, not a license to hunt neighbours who stay peaceful.\n\nThe next verse will immediately fence this command. Except those bound by a treaty, and those wholeheartedly opposed to fighting either you or their own people. If they refrain and offer peace, Allah does not permit you to harm them. The severity of this verse cannot be read without that exception. Peace offered in truth stops the hand.",
  "90": "“except those who are allies of a people you are bound with in a treaty or those wholeheartedly opposed to fighting either you or their own people.” The hard command of the previous verse is now given its doors of safety. A treaty is a bond Allah has commanded the believers to honour. If a doubtful person is under the wing of a people you have a treaty with, you do not break the treaty in order to reach him. The second group is people who truly hate the war on both sides. They are not hunters. They are stuck, and their hearts oppose the fight.\n\n“If Allah had willed, He would have empowered them to fight you.” Their restraint is itself under Allah’s will. He could have made them another enemy force. He did not. That fact should make the believers careful not to create an enemy Allah has not unleashed.\n\n“So if they refrain from fighting you and offer you peace, then Allah does not permit you to harm them.” This is one of the clearest peace-verses in the passage. Refraining and offering peace closes the permission to harm. The community that was told to seize those who turn away to the enemy is now told that Allah does not permit harm against those who stop and offer peace. A believer who ignores this line is not being “tougher than the hypocrites.” He is crossing a limit Allah set.\n\nThe next verse will describe a third, more slippery group: people who want to be safe from you and from their own people, yet cannot resist the temptation of disbelief or hostility. If they will not keep away, offer peace, or refrain from attacking, permission is given against them. The Quran is sorting kinds of people, not painting every outsider one colour.",
  "91": "“You will find others who wish to be safe from you and their own people.” Another type now appears. They want two safeties at once: no harm from the Muslims, and no trouble from their own hostile people. That wish is understandable. What follows is the problem.\n\n“Yet they cannot resist the temptation ˹of disbelief or hostility˺.” When the chance comes, they slip back into the camp that fights you, or into the disbelief they claimed to have left. Safety was a cloak, not a new heart. They are not the treaty-allies of the last verse, and not the people wholeheartedly opposed to fighting. They are people who use peace as a pause between attacks.\n\n“If they do not keep away, offer you peace, or refrain from attacking you, then seize them and kill them wherever you find them. We have given you full permission over such people.” Three exits are named before permission is given: keep away, offer peace, or refrain from attack. The door is not hidden. Only if they refuse all three does the permission open. “Full permission” means the community is not sinning by treating such people as combatants. It does not mean the believers may drop justice, kill the helpless, or ignore a later offer of peace.\n\nRead with the two verses before it, the law is a scale. Wishers of your disbelief who join the enemy are not allies. Treaty-people and sincere avoiders of war are not to be harmed. People who bounce between camps and then attack are not protected by their last polite sentence. The next verse will turn from fighting the hostile to a different blood-rule inside the community: it is not lawful for a believer to kill another except by mistake, and then there is freeing of a slave, blood-money, or a fast of repentance.",
  "92": "“It is not lawful for a believer to kill another except by mistake.” After permission against hostile turncoats, the Quran builds a wall inside the community. A believer’s blood is not lawful. The only case opened here is a killing by mistake: an accident, a tragic error, a strike that was not meant to take a believer’s life. Intentional killing will be treated in the next verse with a far heavier sentence.\n\n“And whoever kills a believer unintentionally must free a believing slave and pay blood-money to the victim’s family—unless they waive it charitably.” Two rights are named. Freeing a believing slave is a right of Allah and a repair toward the community of faith: a life of bondage is opened because a life was closed. Blood-money is a right of the family, unless they choose to waive it as charity. Their waiver is honoured. The killer cannot waive it for them.\n\n“But if the victim is a believer from a hostile people, then a believing slave must be freed.” If the dead believer belonged to a people at war with the Muslims—perhaps he had believed and was still among them—the family-payment is not sent to strengthen a hostile camp. The freeing of a slave remains, because the dead person was a believer.\n\n“And if the victim is from a people bound with you in a treaty, then blood-money must be paid to the family along with freeing a believing slave.” A treaty-people’s family is paid, whether the victim was a believer or, as many teachers understood, a protected person of that treaty. Honouring the treaty includes honouring their blood.\n\n“Those who are unable, let them fast two consecutive months—as a means of repentance to Allah. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” Poverty does not cancel repentance. Two straight months of fasting take the place of freeing a slave for the one who cannot. Allah knows who is truly unable and who is hiding wealth. The next verse will leave the world of mistake and enter the crime of intent.",
  "93": "“And whoever kills a believer intentionally, their reward will be Hell—where they will stay indefinitely.” The wall around a believer’s life is now given its most severe warning. Intentional killing is not an accident with a payment attached. It is a crime whose reward is Hell, and a stay described as indefinite. The wording is meant to stop the hand before it rises against a person who says, “My Lord is Allah.”\n\n“Allah will be displeased with them, condemn them, and will prepare for them a tremendous punishment.” Four results are stacked: Hell, displeasure, condemnation, and a prepared tremendous punishment. No tribal excuse, no claim of a hasty anger, and no later gift of money is being offered here as a cheap wash. The last chapter said those who kill prophets unjustly have a recorded crime. This verse says the unjust killing of an ordinary believer is also a path into wrath.\n\nHonest teachers have discussed how this verse sits beside Allah’s words that He forgives, of whoever He wills, sins other than dying upon associating partners with Him, and beside later verses that open the door for a person who commits evil then seeks forgiveness. Some said the indefinite stay is for one who kills a believer while considering it lawful, which is disbelief. Others said the threat is real and terrible, yet a killer who truly repents, gives the family its right, and dies in faith is not locked out of mercy. The verse itself is a warning, not a permit for despair to kill a second soul. What no serious reading allows is treating a believer’s murder as a small town matter.\n\nThe next verse will guard the opposite error: being so eager to fight that you say to a person who offers peace, “You are no believer,” in order to take a fleeting gain. After forbidding the killing of a believer, the Quran forbids creating a fake unbeliever in order to make a kill look lawful.",
  "94": "“O believers! When you struggle in the cause of Allah, be sure of who you fight.” Caution is now turned toward the believer’s own sword. Struggle in Allah’s cause does not cancel the duty to know who is in front of you. Sure means investigate. Do not let heat, spoil, or an old grudge decide a person’s faith for them.\n\n“And do not say to those who offer you ˹greetings of˺ peace, ‘You are no believer!’—seeking a fleeting worldly gain.” A greeting of peace, or the word of faith that people used to make themselves safe, must not be thrown back in their faces so that their goods can be taken. “Fleeting worldly gain” names the motive the verse will not accept: a mount, a bag, a small profit. An authentic report tells of a man in a campaign who said the word of faith and was still killed, and the Prophet condemned that killing. This verse is that teaching in Allah’s own voice.\n\n“Instead, Allah has infinite bounties ˹in store˺. You were initially like them then Allah blessed you ˹with Islam˺.” You do not need that person’s bag. Allah’s bounties are infinite. And you were once as they are: new, afraid, reaching for a word that would make you safe. Allah blessed you. So do not become a door-closer against a person doing what you once did.\n\n“So be sure! Indeed, Allah is All-Aware of what you do.” The command to be sure is repeated. Awareness means the hidden motive—faith or spoil—is already seen. The next verses will compare those who strive with wealth and lives to those who stay at home. The one who is careful not to kill a greeting person, and the one who still goes forth, are both being shaped into a community that fights without becoming a pack of hunters.",
  "95": "“Those who stay at home—except those with valid excuses—are not equal to those who strive in the cause of Allah with their wealth and their lives.” After forbidding unjust killing, the Quran refuses another false equality: the equality of the sitter and the striver. They are not the same. “Except those with valid excuses” protects the blind, the ill, the truly unable. Their sitting is not the sitting of the lagger who said Allah blessed him for not being there. Excuse is honoured. Comfort dressed as excuse is not.\n\n“Allah has elevated in rank those who strive with their wealth and their lives above those who stay behind ˹with valid excuses˺.” Even above the excused sitters, the strivers are raised. Rank is Allah’s to give. Wealth and lives are both named, so that a person who can give money but not march, or who marches with little money, is not erased. Striving is whatever of the two a person can honestly spend.\n\n“Allah has promised each a fine reward, but those who strive will receive a far better reward than others—” The excused sitter is not unpaid. He has a fine reward if his faith is true. The striver has a far better reward. The sentence runs into the next verse: far superior ranks, forgiveness, and mercy. Equality of worth as human beings is not being denied. Equality of rank after different sacrifices is being denied. The last chapter said they each have varying degrees in the sight of Allah. This is one of those degrees made visible.\n\nA community that pretends the couch and the field are the same will stop producing people who answer the cry of the oppressed. A community that scorns the truly excused will become unjust in the other direction. This verse holds both lines.",
  "96": "“far superior ranks, forgiveness, and mercy from Him.” The better reward of the strivers is now unpacked. Ranks are far superior: not a thin extra, but a real elevation. Forgiveness is named because even a person who goes forth with his life still needs his sins covered. Mercy is named because the gift is larger than the march. The three together stop two errors. One error is to think striving buys Paradise without pardon. The other is to think sitting and striving end in the same place.\n\n“And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The names under the ranks matter. The striver is not a sinless angel. He is a weak human who spent himself, and he meets a Lord who forgives and shows mercy. The excused sitter who is faithful also meets those names. The hypocrite who sat and then claimed blessing is not being comforted here.\n\nThis short verse should be read as the end of one thought with the verse before it. Allah promised each a fine reward. Then He promised the strivers more: ranks, forgiveness, mercy. The previous chapter said those who strive and are patient will be distinguished. This is that distinction stated as a gift, not as a human medal.\n\nThe next passage will turn to people who had no valid excuse and still refused to leave a land of oppression. When the angels seize their souls, they will say they were oppressed. The angels will ask whether Allah’s earth was not spacious enough to emigrate. Hell will be their home—except for the truly helpless men, women, and children who cannot afford a way out.",
  "97": "“When the angels seize the souls of those who have wronged themselves—scolding them, ‘What do you think you were doing?’” The scene moves from ranks in this life to the first questioning after death. The people being seized had wronged themselves: they stayed in a place where they could not practise their faith, though they had a way to leave. The angels’ question is not for information. It is a scolding. What were you doing with a life that could have moved?\n\n“they will reply, ‘We were oppressed in the land.’” The excuse is the same word used by people who truly are oppressed. In their mouths it is a cover. They call a chosen staying oppression, because leaving would have cost home, money, or family pride. The last verses honoured oppressed men, women, and children who cry for a saviour. This verse unmasks people who borrow that cry after wasting the chance to walk.\n\n“The angels will respond, ‘Was Allah’s earth not spacious enough for you to emigrate?’” The earth is described as Allah’s, and as spacious. Emigration is not presented as a tiny hidden door. It is presented as a wide earth they refused. “It is they who will have Hell as their home—what an evil destination!” The home they would not leave for Allah is replaced by a home they cannot leave.\n\nThe next two verses will save the verse from cruelty. Except helpless men, women, and children who cannot afford a way out: it is right to hope that Allah will pardon them. He is Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving. The scolding is for those who could have moved. The hope is for those who could not.",
  "98": "“Except helpless men, women, and children who cannot afford a way out—” The hard scene of the previous verse is immediately limited. Hell as a home was named for people who wronged themselves by staying when they could leave. This exception is for people who are truly helpless. Men can be helpless: the old, the trapped, the one with no strength and no road. Women and children are named because they were often the least able to migrate through a hostile land. “Cannot afford a way out” means no money, no guide, no safe path, no power to walk.\n\nThe verse is unfinished without the next one, which says it is right to hope that Allah will pardon them. Even the grammar is mercy. The exception hangs, then pardon is hoped. The Quran will not let a listener use the angels’ scolding against a mother who could not cross the desert, or against a child who had no choice.\n\nThis also explains the earlier cry of the oppressed. Some who say “we are oppressed” are telling the truth and cannot move. Some are hiding. The Book distinguishes them. A community that emigrates must not despise those it had to leave behind if those left behind had no way. A community that stays must not call every staying helplessness if a way exists.\n\nThe first verse of this chapter honoured family ties and the weak. These two verses apply that honour at the border of a city. Helplessness is seen. False helplessness is not. The next verse will place the hope of pardon on Allah’s names: Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving.",
  "99": "“it is right to hope that Allah will pardon them.” The helpless who could not afford a way out are not handed a proud certificate of innocence, and they are not handed despair. “It is right to hope” is a gentle wording. Human beings do not issue their pardon. They are taught that hope in Allah for such people is a right hope, not a wild wish. He sees the blocked road, the frightened child, the woman with no protector, the man whose body will not serve him.\n\n“For Allah is Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving.” Ever-Pardoning means the pardon is not a rare mood. All-Forgiving means the sins that clung to people even in their helplessness—fear, complaints, a weak day—can be covered. These names were given earlier to those who repent soon after a reckless sin. Here they are given to people whose main “sin” in this discussion was remaining where they had no power to leave.\n\nThe pair of verses also teaches the living who can still move. Do not use the helpless as your excuse. Their hope of pardon is not your hope if your earth is spacious and your feet work. And do not use the angels’ scolding as a whip on people Allah has taught you to hope for.\n\nThe next verse will speak to the one who does emigrate. He will find many safe havens and bountiful resources. If he dies on the way to Allah and His Messenger, his reward is already secured. The helpless are hoped for. The traveller is promised. Both meet a Forgiving, Merciful Lord, but they meet Him on different roads.",
  "100": "“Whoever emigrates in the cause of Allah will find many safe havens and bountiful resources throughout the earth.” The spacious earth of the angels’ question is now described from the traveller’s side. Emigration in Allah’s cause is not presented only as loss of a house. It is presented as a road on which safe havens and resources exist. “Many” fights the fear that leaving one town means dying in an empty land. The provision may not look like the old shop. It is still called bountiful, because it comes with safety for the faith.\n\n“Those who leave their homes and die while emigrating to Allah and His Messenger—their reward has already been secured with Allah.” Some will not arrive. Death on the road does not cancel the journey. The reward is already secured: written, kept, not waiting on a successful arrival. Emigrating “to Allah and His Messenger” names the destination as a Lord and a messenger, not merely a new market. A person who dies between Mecca and Medina, or between any house of fear and a house of faith, has arrived in the only sense that finally matters.\n\n“And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The traveller may have delayed, feared, or left behind duties unfinished. Forgiveness and mercy go with him. The last chapter promised those who migrated and were expelled that their sins would be forgiven and gardens given. This verse adds the one who dies before the new city is seen.\n\nThe next verses will give the traveller a lightened worship. When you travel through the land, it is permissible to shorten the prayer, especially if you fear an attack. The same Lord who asked people to move also shortened the prayer for the moving.",
  "101": "“When you travel through the land, it is permissible for you to shorten the prayer—˹especially˺ if you fear an attack by the disbelievers.” The emigration and the campaigns of this chapter are now given a prayer-ruling. Shortening the prayer is called permissible. Later practice of the Prophet showed that shortening on a journey is a granted lightening, not a grudging exception. The verse mentions fear of attack as a special reason, because many of those journeys were through hostile land. The permission is wider than panic, and the fear-case is the one this passage has in view.\n\n“Indeed, the disbelievers are your sworn enemies.” The line explains why a travelling row of believers may need a shorter prayer and, in the next verse, an armed prayer. Sworn enemies are not a casual annoyance. They watch for a moment when weapons are set down and foreheads are on the ground. The last verses about taking precautions, going forth in groups, and not publicizing every fear sit behind this ruling.\n\nShortening is lightness for a weak creation, as this chapter said Allah wills to lighten burdens. A traveller already carrying distance, danger, and often grief is not asked to pray as if he were sitting in a safe mosque with no road behind him.\n\nThe next verse will describe the prayer of fear in detail. One group prays with the Prophet while armed. The other stands guard. Then they switch. The disbelievers would wish to see you neglect your weapons. Rain and illness allow laying weapons aside, but precaution remains. Prayer is not cancelled. Carelessness is.",
  "102": "“When you ˹O Prophet˺ are ˹campaigning˺ with them and you lead them in prayer, let one group of them pray with you—while armed.” The shortened journey-prayer now becomes a prayer that can survive an ambush. The Prophet leads. One group prays with him, carrying weapons. Worship and precaution are in the same body. Faith does not mean closing the eyes to a sworn enemy.\n\n“When they prostrate themselves, let the other group stand guard behind them. Then the group that has not yet prayed will then join you in prayer—and let them be vigilant and armed.” The community splits in order to remain one. Half pray, half watch. Then the watchers pray. No group is told that prayer is for later, and no group is told that watching is for the irreligious. Both halves do both duties.\n\n“The disbelievers would wish to see you neglect your weapons and belongings, so they could launch a sweeping assault on you.” Their wish teaches the ruling. A sweeping assault is the dream of an enemy who sees bows on the ground and heads in the dust. The Quran tells that wish in advance so the believers will not fulfil it.\n\n“But there is no blame if you lay aside your weapons when overcome by heavy rain or illness—but take precaution.” Lightness again. Rain and illness are real. Weapons may be set down. Precaution is not set down. “Indeed, Allah has prepared a humiliating punishment for the disbelievers.” The enemy who hopes to catch a praying row is himself headed toward humiliation. The believers take precaution; they do not take the enemy as a second god.\n\nThe next verse will say that when the prayers are over, remember Allah standing, sitting, or lying down. When you are secure, establish regular prayers. Prayer is a duty at appointed times. The special form does not cancel the ordinary form once safety returns.",
  "103": "“When the prayers are over, remember Allah—whether you are standing, sitting, or lying down.” The armed prayer ends, but remembrance does not. Standing, sitting, and lying down are the same three postures used in the last chapter for people of reason who remember Allah in every state. After a tense prayer with weapons, the heart could either go numb or stay loud with fear. The verse fills that space with remembrance.\n\n“But when you are secure, establish regular prayers.” The special form is for fear and travel. Security brings back the ordinary, complete prayer. A person is not allowed to make the fear-prayer his new habit in a safe town, and he is not allowed to drop prayer in a dangerous field. Each state has its form.\n\n“Indeed, performing prayers is a duty on the believers at the appointed times.” Duty and appointed times are the spine. Appointed times mean prayer is not left to mood, victory, or panic. The last chapter closed by telling the believers to patiently endure, persevere, stand on guard, and be mindful. This verse shows mindfulness as a prayer that has a clock even when the enemy has a plan.\n\nThe next verse will apply that same refusal to falter in the pursuit of the enemy. If you are suffering, they too are suffering. But you can hope to receive from Allah what they can never hope for. Prayer at appointed times and pursuit without collapse are two sides of a people who hope in Allah, not only in comfort.",
  "104": "“Do not falter in pursuit of the enemy—if you are suffering, they too are suffering.” After Uhud-like pain, a community can decide that the enemy is made of iron and they themselves are only flesh. This verse equalises the flesh. You suffer. They suffer. Tiredness, wounds, fear, and heat are not a Muslim speciality. The last chapter said if you have suffered injuries, they suffered similarly. This verse says it again in the language of pursuit. Faltering because you hurt, while forgetting that they hurt, is a trick of the self.\n\n“But you can hope to receive from Allah what they can never hope for.” Here the two sides become unequal in the only way that matters. The believer hopes for ranks, forgiveness, mercy, gardens, and the company of the prophets. The enemy, while he fights for the Devil’s cause, has no such hope. That difference should put strength back into a tired body. They run on pride, spoil, or rage. You run on a hope they do not own.\n\n“And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” He knows the suffering you hide and the faltering you almost chose. He is wise in asking you to continue anyway, and wise in the times He lightens the prayer, shortens the duty, and pardons the helpless.\n\nThe next verses will turn from the field to the court. The Book has been sent so the Prophet may judge between people by what Allah has shown him. He must not be an advocate for the deceitful. Even a messenger is warned not to let a clever liar use him. Seek forgiveness. Do not advocate for those who wrong themselves.",
  "105": "“Indeed, We have sent down the Book to you ˹O Prophet˺ in truth to judge between people by means of what Allah has shown you.” The Prophet’s judging, which hypocrites tried to escape, is now grounded again. The Book was sent in truth. He judges by what Allah has shown him, not by the tears of the more eloquent party and not by the standing of a family in town. Early reports connected these verses with a man who stole, hid the theft among his people, and tried to throw the blame on a Jewish man. The wording itself is enough. The Book is for judgment. The showing is from Allah.\n\n“So do not be an advocate for the deceitful.” An advocate here is a defender who uses skill to make the wrong look right. The Prophet is being told, and every later judge with him, that cleverness on behalf of a liar is not mercy. It is participation. The last verses said whoever intercedes for an evil cause shares the burden. This is that teaching aimed at the highest judge in the community.\n\nThe warning is an honour. Allah does not want His messenger’s court to become a tool of a deceitful clan. People who “wrong themselves” by lying, stealing, then seeking a prophetic stamp will appear in the next sentences. The Prophet must not become their shield.\n\nThe next verse will tell him to seek Allah’s forgiveness. Even a prophet is pointed to forgiveness when the air of a case is thick with other people’s deceit. Then: do not advocate for those who wrong themselves. Allah does not like the deceitful, sinful.",
  "106": "“And seek Allah’s forgiveness—indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” Right in the middle of a warning not to defend the deceitful, the Prophet is told to seek forgiveness. The command honours him and teaches his community. A judge can need pardon for a leaning of the heart, for almost being moved by a story, or simply as a servant who lives under a Forgiving Lord. If he is told to seek forgiveness, how much more the ordinary believer who has actually spoken for a liar or hidden a cousin’s theft.\n\n“All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” keeps the warning from becoming despair. The deceitful are about to be refused advocacy. The door of forgiveness is not being refused to people who turn. The next verses will say that whoever commits evil or wrongs themselves then seeks Allah’s forgiveness will find Him All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. This verse plants those names first on the Prophet’s tongue.\n\nThe placement also shows what a clean court looks like. It does not begin with rage at the accused. It begins with the judge asking Allah to cover him, then refusing to become a lawyer for deceit. Mercy and firmness sit in one person.\n\nThe next verse will make the refusal plain. Do not advocate for those who wrong themselves. Surely Allah does not like those who are deceitful, sinful. Liking is not a small word here. The one who enjoys defending a knowing liar is standing near a type of person Allah has said He does not like.",
  "107": "“Do not advocate for those who wrong themselves.” The command is repeated in harder focus. The deceitful are not described first as wronging the one they framed. They are described as wronging themselves. A thief who throws his crime on an innocent man has already injured his own soul before the judge opens the case. To advocate for him is to help a man harm himself further, as well as to harm the innocent.\n\n“Surely Allah does not like those who are deceitful, sinful.” Deceit and sin are paired. This is not a single slip followed by a red face. It is a way of handling life: hide, plot at night, look honest by day, and search for a respected mouth to speak on your behalf. Allah’s dislike of that type should end a believer’s eagerness to be their respected mouth. Family loyalty, tribal heat, and the wish to keep a group’s name clean are not higher than this dislike.\n\nThe next verse will show why advocacy for them is so useless. They try to hide their deception from people, but they can never hide it from Allah, in Whose presence they plot by night what is displeasing to Him. He is Fully Aware. A lawyer may fool a room. He cannot fool the One in Whose presence the night-plot was made.\n\nSo the court of this chapter is consistent with its first verse. Allah is Watchful. Trusts must be returned. Judgment must be fair. The orphan, the wife, the treaty-partner, and the framed neighbour are not to lose because the other side had better connections. The Prophet himself is told not to be that connection.",
  "108": "“They try to hide ˹their deception˺ from people, but they can never hide it from Allah.” The deceitful for whom advocacy was just forbidden are now seen in their favourite work: hiding. They fear faces. They do not fear the One who sees without a face being turned toward them. Hiding from people can succeed for a time. A stolen shield can be placed in another man’s house. A lie can be rehearsed. A clan can agree on a story. None of that is hiding from Allah.\n\n“in Whose presence they plot by night what is displeasing to Him.” The night-plot is not done in a private universe. It is done in His presence. The last verses said a group would spend the night contradicting “We obey,” and that Allah records their schemes. This verse says the same night more sharply. What they plot is displeasing to Him. The pleasure of the clan is not the measure. His displeasure is.\n\n“And Allah is Fully Aware of what they do.” Awareness here includes the act, the motive, the person they hope to frame, and the advocate they hope to use. A believer who was about to speak for them is being told: you are offering to hide what cannot be hidden. You may confuse a room. You will not confuse the Witness.\n\nThe next verse will ask the advocates a question they cannot answer well. You are advocating for them in this life, but who will dare to advocate for them before Allah on the Day of Judgment? Or who will come to their defence? The court of the world is not the last court.",
  "109": "“Here you are! You ˹believers˺ are advocating for them in this life, but who will ˹dare to˺ advocate for them before Allah on the Day of Judgment?” The tone is almost astonished. Here you are—alive, related to them, moved by their tears or their family name—speaking on their behalf. That speech has a time-limit. It works, if it works at all, only in this life. On the Day of Judgment, who will dare to stand up and defend a person whose night-plot is already known to Allah? The question expects the answer: no one.\n\n“Or who will come to their defence?” Defence in that court is not a clever cousin. The last chapter said the wrongdoers will have no helpers, and that those condemned by Allah will have no helper. This verse applies that emptiness to people you are tempted to shield now. If you cannot defend them there, why train your tongue to make their deceit look small here?\n\nThe verse also frees the innocent person they tried to blame. In this life he may have fewer advocates. On that Day the lack of a worldly lawyer will not matter. Allah is the Witness.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to hear this as a ban on defending the wrongly accused. The passage is about defending the deceitful after their wrong is becoming clear. The next verse will open the true door for the sinner himself. Whoever commits evil or wrongs themselves then seeks Allah’s forgiveness will certainly find Allah All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. They do not need you to paint them white. They need to ask the One who can actually make them clean.",
  "110": "“Whoever commits evil or wrongs themselves then seeks Allah’s forgiveness will certainly find Allah All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” After closing the door of false advocacy, the Quran opens the door that actually works. “Whoever” is wide. The thief, the liar, the one who almost framed an innocent man, the one who delayed emigration, the one who spoke for a deceitful relative: if they commit evil or wrong themselves and then seek forgiveness, they will find Him All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. Finding Him means the names are not locked away from them. They meet those names when they ask.\n\n“Then” is the hinge. Evil happens. Wronging the self happens. The verse does not praise the crime. It praises the turning that follows. This is the same path named earlier: those who commit evil ignorantly or recklessly then repent soon after. Seeking forgiveness is that repentance in one short act of the heart and tongue, joined to leaving the wrong.\n\nThe verse also stops despair, which is another trick of Satan. A person who has ruined a name or swallowed a right may think he is finished. He is not told to find a skilled advocate. He is told to find Allah. The names given are the same names given to the Prophet when he was told to seek forgiveness in the middle of the case.\n\nThe next verse will add a second truth beside this hope. Whoever commits a sin—it is only to their own loss. Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise. Forgiveness is available. The sin still harmed the sinner first. No one outsmarts the scale.",
  "111": "“And whoever commits a sin—it is only to their own loss.” People often imagine a successful sin: I gained the goods, I kept my name, someone else paid. This verse says the gain is a mask. The loss is the sinner’s. He loses purity, loses nearness, loses a piece of his own soul, and, if he does not turn, loses the Hereafter. The framed innocent man is hurt, and that hurt is real. Still the deepest loss is recorded against the one who did the sin.\n\nThis helps explain why the last verse called evil a wronging of themselves. The two verses are one teaching. You do not climb by climbing over another person. You fall, even if the town claps. A stolen object in the house is not a profit in the record.\n\n“Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” He knows the hidden profit and the hidden loss. He is wise in attaching the sin to the sinner’s own account, and wise in opening forgiveness to the one who asks. Knowledge without wisdom would only expose. Wisdom also heals those who return.\n\nSo the verse is both a warning and a mercy. A warning, because no one outsmarts the scale. A mercy, because the loss can still be repaired by the seeking of forgiveness just named. The next verse will name a sin that tries to move the loss onto someone else. Whoever commits an evil or sinful deed then blames it on an innocent person will bear the guilt of slander and blatant sin.",
  "112": "“And whoever commits an evil or sinful deed then blames it on an innocent person, they will definitely bear the guilt of slander and blatant sin.” The case behind this passage is now stated as a law for every age. First a person does the evil. Then he looks for a clean name to hang it on. The innocent person may be weaker, poorer, or from another community—as in the report of a theft being thrown onto a Jewish man. The verse does not care how useful the target looks. It names him innocent, and it names the extra crime.\n\n“Slander and blatant sin” are two loads. Slander is the false accusation that can follow a person through a town. Blatant sin is the original deed plus the lie, now open before Allah even if it is still dressed up before people. “Definitely bear” means the transfer failed. The guilt did not jump. It doubled.\n\nThis is why advocacy for such a person is so ugly. You would be helping the slander travel. The innocent would be crushed twice: once by the accusation, once by a respected voice repeating it. The Watcher named at the head of the chapter sees both crushings.\n\nThe next verse will tell the Prophet that only Allah’s grace stopped a group from deceiving him. They would have deceived none but themselves, and they cannot harm him in the least. The Book, the wisdom, and the teaching of what he never knew are a great favour. A messenger protected by revelation is safer than a clan’s night-plot.",
  "113": "“Had it not been for Allah’s grace and mercy, a group of them would have sought to deceive you ˹O Prophet˺.” The Prophet is being shown how close the plot came. A group—not everyone—almost used him. Grace and mercy stopped the deception from landing. The wording honours him without making him independently immune. He is protected because Allah protects him. The same grace was named when the community would have followed Satan except for a few. Messengers and communities both stand by mercy.\n\n“Yet they would deceive none but themselves, nor can they harm you in the least.” Even if the plot had confused a room for a day, the real deceived party is the plotter. He deceives himself about Allah, about the Prophet, and about the success of a lie. Harm to the Prophet is denied “in the least.” They cannot take his prophethood, cannot cancel the Book, and cannot make Allah’s judgment follow their clan.\n\n“Allah has revealed to you the Book and wisdom and taught you what you never knew. Great indeed is Allah’s favour upon you!” Book, wisdom, and new teaching are the same favour named in the last chapter when Allah sent a messenger reciting, purifying, and teaching. What he never knew includes hidden plots as well as laws. Favour is called great so that neither he nor his community will treat revelation as a small edge in an argument. It is the reason the deceitful cannot own the court.\n\nThe next verse will judge the secret talks themselves. There is no good in most of them, except talks that encourage charity, kindness, or reconciliation. Whoever does that seeking Allah’s pleasure will have a great reward. Night can be used for a plot or for a repair.",
  "114": "“There is no good in most of their secret talks—except those encouraging charity, kindness, or reconciliation between people.” Secret talk is not forbidden as such. Most of the secret talk in view here has no good in it: plots to hide a theft, to smear an innocent man, to undo a public “we obey.” The exceptions are named so that privacy is not made a sin. Charity planned without a drum, kindness arranged without a show, reconciliation between people that needs a quiet room: these are good uses of secrecy. The last chapter praised those who donate and those who pardon. This verse gives them a lawful whisper.\n\n“And whoever does this seeking Allah’s pleasure, We will grant them a great reward.” Motive is required. A public peacemaker may want a name. A secret giver may want Allah. Seeking His pleasure turns the quiet act into a rewarded act. The great reward has been promised in this chapter to strivers, to sincere intercessors, and now to people whose best work is not even seen.\n\nThis also redefines strength. The deceitful think a night-plot is power. The Quran says most of that talk is empty of good. The strong thing is a secret that feeds a poor person, or a secret that returns two families to peace.\n\nThe next verse will leave the room of whispers and state a public break. Whoever defies the Messenger after guidance has become clear, and follows a path other than that of the believers, will be left to what he chose, then burned in Hell. Secret talks that become a second path are not a small social habit.",
  "115": "“And whoever defies the Messenger after guidance has become clear to them and follows a path other than that of the believers, We will let them pursue what they have chosen, then burn them in Hell—what an evil end!” Defiance here is not a question asked in order to learn. It is a turning against the Messenger after the guidance has already become clear. The person saw, then chose another path. “A path other than that of the believers” means a road that leaves the community of those who obey Allah and His Messenger: their way of worship, their way of judging, their way of treating the weak.\n\n“We will let them pursue what they have chosen” is a fearful kind of freedom. Allah does not always block the wrong road with a wall. He may let a person have the path he insisted on. Then comes the end: burn them in Hell. The choice was theirs. The end is His justice.\n\nThis verse has been used by scholars as a warning against leaving the gathered way of the believers after the proof has arrived. It must not be turned into a whip against every honest disagreement among people who still obey the Messenger. The verse is about defiance after clarity, and about following a different path, not about two students reading a hard case in good faith.\n\nThe next verse will repeat a foundation already given. Allah does not forgive associating others with Him, but forgives anything else of whoever He wills. Whoever associates others with Him has gone far astray. The “other path” finds its worst form in taking gods besides Allah, or in taking Satan as a guardian.",
  "116": "“Surely Allah does not forgive associating ˹others˺ with Him ˹in worship˺, but forgives anything else of whoever He wills.” The sentence returns almost as it was said earlier in this chapter, because the “other path” has now been named. If a person dies upon associating others with Allah, he has died on the sin Allah has not promised to forgive. Anything else—theft, slander, a cowardly staying, a night-plot—remains under His will to forgive when He wills, especially when the servant seeks forgiveness as the last verses taught.\n\nRepeating the line after a passage about deceit is a mercy. A liar might think he is finished. He is not in the same case as a person who takes lords besides Allah and dies on that. He must still repent, return rights, and stop the slander. The door is not the same as the door of dying on idolatry. The two must not be mixed, either by despair or by carelessness.\n\n“Indeed, whoever associates ˹others˺ with Allah has clearly gone far astray.” Far astray means the person is not near the road and slightly off. He has left the purpose of his creation. The next verses will describe what that straying looks like in the old Arabian religion and in every later copy of it: invoking female gods, which is in fact invoking a rebellious Satan, cursed by Allah, who promised to take a share of the servants, mislead them, raise empty hopes, and make them alter Allah’s creation.\n\nA living person who still breathes can leave that far stray by returning to Allah alone. The warning is for dying on the association, not for a soul that wants to come back.",
  "117": "“Instead of Allah, they only invoke female gods and they ˹actually˺ invoke none but a rebellious Satan—” The far stray of the previous verse is given a picture from the religion around the first listeners. They called on goddesses by name, as if those names could hear, help, or draw them near to Allah. The Quran says the invocation does not stop at a statue or a title. It arrives at a rebellious Satan. The gods have no real power. The one who beautifies their worship and lives on it is Satan.\n\n“Female gods” names what those people actually served. The point is not an insult to women. The point is that they invented deities, often with female names, and then asked those inventions for what only the Creator gives. Other verses say they assigned to Allah daughters they themselves disliked to have. The whole structure is rebellion dressed as devotion.\n\nThe sentence runs into the next verse: a Satan cursed by Allah, who said he would take hold of a certain number of the servants. The human being who thinks he is honouring a goddess is, in this telling, answering a cursed rebel’s recruitment.\n\nThis also speaks beyond one century. Whenever people call on a created thing as if it shared Allah’s right, the call is not completed at that thing. It is completed at the rebel who first said, “Take other guardians.” The next verses will quote his plan: mislead, delude with empty hopes, order the slitting of cattle’s ears, and alter Allah’s creation. Whoever takes him as a guardian instead of Allah has suffered a tremendous loss.",
  "118": "“cursed by Allah—who said, ‘I will surely take hold of a certain number of Your servants.’” Satan is cursed: thrown away from mercy, named as an enemy, not a rival god. In that cursed state he still speaks a plan. He will take hold of a portion of the servants. He does not claim he can take all of them. He claims a number. That limit is itself a sign of his weakness, already mentioned when the chapter said his schemes are ever weak.\n\n“Take hold” means to recruit, to lead, to make people follow his orders as if he were a guardian. The servants are Allah’s. Satan’s whole project is to steal the loyalty of people who do not belong to him. The last verse said those who invoke false gods are actually invoking him. This verse shows that he asked for that role.\n\nThe next verse will list his methods. He will mislead them, delude them with empty hopes, order them to slit the ears of cattle, and order them to alter Allah’s creation. The methods mix the inner and the outer: lies in the heart, false hopes, then religious-looking mutilations of animals and of the human being. A tremendous loss waits for whoever takes him as a guardian instead of Allah.\n\nSo the cursed one is not feared as a second lord. He is known, quoted, and refused. The believer who heard that Allah is the only god worthy of worship now hears the voice that tries to collect a number against that truth.",
  "119": "“I will certainly mislead them and delude them with empty hopes.” Satan’s first two tools are inner. Misleading is taking people off the Straight Path. Empty hopes are promises that never arrive: you will be forgiven without turning, you will be safe in your sin, you will not be raised, the idols will speak for you, tomorrow is plenty. Hope is good when it is tied to Allah. Empty hope is a rope tied to nothing.\n\n“Also, I will order them and they will slit the ears of cattle and alter Allah’s creation.” The tools then become rituals. Slitting the ears of cattle was a pagan mark that certain animals were dedicated to false gods and taken out of ordinary lawful use. Altering Allah’s creation, in this setting, includes that kind of mutilation and the wider project of changing what Allah made from its created purpose: using the body, the animal, and the natural order in ways that serve rebellion rather than gratitude. Honest teachers have discussed the exact range of this phrase. What is clear is that Satan loves a change that says, “This is no longer as Allah made it for His service.”\n\n“And whoever takes Satan as a guardian instead of Allah has certainly suffered a tremendous loss.” Guardian is the word this chapter used for Allah. To give it to Satan is to switch protectors. The loss is tremendous because the person loses guidance now and the garden later, and gains only empty hopes and a home in Hell. The next verse will repeat that Satan’s promises are only delusion, so that no one treats the last advertisement as a small risk.",
  "120": "“Satan only makes them ˹false˺ promises and deludes them with ˹empty˺ hopes. Truly Satan promises them nothing but delusion.” The plan just quoted is now judged in one word: delusion. He promises. He does not pay. The promise may look like freedom, honour, pleasure, or a clever religion that asks less than Allah asks. What arrives is emptiness. The last chapter said the life of this world is the delusion of enjoyment. This verse says Satan’s entire speech is that same kind of delusion.\n\n“Only” and “nothing but” are repeated so the listener will stop bargaining. There is no corner of Satan’s promise that is solid. A person who says, “I will take this much of his hope and still keep my faith,” is already drinking what this verse called empty. The repetition is kindness. Some hearts need to hear the same warning twice in one breath.\n\nThis also explains the night-plots and the false advocacy. Those too are hopes: we will not be caught, the Prophet will defend us, the innocent man will carry the shame. Delusion.\n\nThe next verse will name the home at the end of the delusion. It is they who will have Hell as their home, and they will find no escape from it. A promise of escape was part of the empty hope. The home has no exit. Then, as the Quran loves to do, the opposite home will be named for those who believe and do good.\n\nSo the wise heart stops asking Satan for a better offer. There is no better offer. There is only a longer advertisement for the same emptiness.",
  "121": "“It is they who will have Hell as their home, and they will find no escape from it!” The people who took Satan as a guardian, invoked false gods, and lived on empty hopes are given a home. The word “home” has been used in these chapters for both the Fire and the Garden so that neither is imagined as a visit. “No escape” answers the last advertisement. Delusion said there would be a way out, a friend, an intercessor who could buy them, a second chance after death without faith. The verse says they will find no escape.\n\nThe shortness is part of the force. After several verses quoting Satan at length, Allah’s sentence is brief. The rebel spoke many words. The end is one home.\n\nThis should be read with the open door still standing a few verses back. Whoever seeks forgiveness will find Allah All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. The people of this home are those who die on Satan’s guardianship, not those who were deceived for a time and then came back. The Quran is not shutting the living. It is telling the truth about the end of a chosen delusion.\n\nThe next verse will set the true promise beside the false one. Those who believe and do good will be admitted into Gardens, to stay for ever and ever. Allah’s promise is always true. Whose word is more truthful than Allah’s? The two speakers—Satan and Allah—are now to be compared by their promises and by their truthfulness.",
  "122": "“And those who believe and do good, We will soon admit them into Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever.” The opposite home is described with the opposite time-word: soon. Hell was a home with no escape. The garden is a home that arrives, in Allah’s timing, for people of faith and good deeds, and then does not end. “Soon” comforts people who feel that good is slow to be paid. The payment is not as far as despair says.\n\n“Allah’s promise is ˹always˺ true. And whose word is more truthful than Allah’s?” The question was asked at the end of the last major section, and it returns after Satan’s speech on purpose. Satan promises delusion. Allah promises truth. A person is, in the end, choosing which speaker to trust. The last chapter closed a passage by asking whose word is more truthful than Allah’s. This verse uses the same question to crush empty hopes.\n\nBelief and good deeds are again paired. The garden is not sold for a slogan, and not sold for deeds without faith. The two together answer both the self-exalting person who thinks his group-name is enough, and the person who thinks work without faith is enough.\n\nThe next verse will cut another empty hope: the hope that grace is by wishes, yours or the People of the Book’s. Whoever commits evil will be rewarded accordingly, and will find no protector besides Allah. Wishes do not steer the Day. Deeds and faith do.",
  "123": "“˹Divine grace is˺ neither by your wishes nor those of the People of the Book!” Two audiences are stripped of a fantasy at once. Some Muslims might wish that their belonging to the final community is itself a ticket. Some of the People of the Book wished that Paradise was theirs alone because of lineage or an old name. The verse says grace is not by those wishes. The last chapter recorded each community saying none will enter Paradise except those of their own faith, and called those claims desires. This verse is that teaching restated after Satan’s empty hopes, because religious wishing can become another delusion.\n\n“Whoever commits evil will be rewarded accordingly, and they will find no protector or helper besides Allah.” Evil has a matching payment. The matching is justice, not a denial of repentance. A person who meets Allah with an evil he never left will find a payment that fits, and no protector besides Allah. If he wanted a tribe, a prophet, or a goddess as a shield independent of Allah, he will not find it.\n\nThis does not cancel the multiplication of good or the grace that forgives. It cancels the idea that wishing replaces turning. The next verse will state the other half. Those who do good—whether male or female—and have faith will enter Paradise and will never be wronged even as much as the speck on a date stone. Wishes fail. Faith and good do not fail, and they are not held back from a woman or a man.",
  "124": "“But those who do good—whether male or female—and have faith will enter Paradise and will never be wronged ˹even as much as˺ the speck on a date stone.” After denying that grace is by group wishes, the Quran names what actually enters a person into Paradise: doing good and having faith, whether the person is male or female. This returns to the last chapter’s answer, “I will never deny any of you—male or female—the reward of your deeds,” and to this chapter’s word that men and women are rewarded according to their deeds. The gate is not a male gate. It is faith and good.\n\n“Never be wronged even as much as the speck on a date stone” is the third tiny measure in this chapter: atom’s weight, date-thread, date-speck. The meaning is one. No good of a woman will be trimmed because she is a woman. No good of a quiet man will be trimmed because he had no tribe to wish on his behalf. The scale does not honour wishes. It honours what was done in faith.\n\nDoing good here is the wide good this chapter has been teaching: returning trusts, fair judgment, kind speech, a better greeting, protecting orphans, spending, reconciling people. Faith is the root that makes the good travel to the garden.\n\nThe next verse will ask who is better in faith than those who fully submit to Allah, do good, and follow the way of Abraham, the upright. Allah chose Abraham as a close friend. The standard is not a later slogan. It is the old, upright submission.",
  "125": "“And who is better in faith than those who ˹fully˺ submit themselves to Allah, do good, and follow the Way of Abraham, the upright?” The question expects the answer: no one. Better faith has three marks. Full submission to Allah: the body and the heart given over, which is the meaning of Islam in this Book. Doing good: the faith that does not remain a claim. Following Abraham’s way: the upright path that refuses partners. The last chapter said Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian; he submitted in all uprightness and was not a polytheist. This verse calls people back to that same man after a long discussion of false gods and empty hopes.\n\n“Allah chose Abraham as a close friend.” The honour is rare and warm. Close friend means a nearness of love and chosen companionship, not a sharing of divinity. Abraham is not a lord to be invoked beside Allah. He is a friend of Allah whose way is to be followed. The friendship was given to a man who submitted, did good, and smashed the claim of idols. Those who invoke female gods are not on his road, even if they claim his house.\n\nSo the better faith is not a new invention of Medina and not a property of a later label. It is Abraham’s submission brought to life again. The next verse will place that friendship inside Allah’s ownership. To Him alone belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. He is Fully Aware of everything. The Friend of Abraham is the Owner of the worlds, not a local god of one family.",
  "126": "“To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.” After naming Abraham as a close friend, the Quran refuses any thought that friendship split the kingdom. The heavens and the earth are still His alone. Abraham did not become a partner. The false gods do not own a speck. Satan does not own the servants he bragged he would take. Ownership explains why submission to Allah is the better faith. You submit to the One who already owns you.\n\nThis line has been the drum under both chapters. It sat under inheritance, under fear of enemies, under stolen praise, and now under the choice between Satan’s guardianship and Allah’s. If He owns everything, then taking another guardian is not independence. It is a misunderstanding of where you already live.\n\n“And Allah is Fully Aware of everything.” Awareness closes the verse as it closed the night-plot verse. He is aware of the secret talk that has no good in it, of the secret charity that has good in it, of the woman whose good would be ignored by people, of the man who follows Abraham in a quiet house. Everything is a large word. It is meant to be large.\n\nThe next verse will return from this height to a question the people were actually asking the Prophet: regarding women. The answer will point back to instruction already revealed about orphan women deprived of rights, helpless children, and standing up for orphans. The Owner of the heavens is still watching the orphan file.",
  "127": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ regarding women. Say, ‘It is Allah Who instructs you regarding them.’” The community has questions about women: marriage, orphans, rights, fear of injustice. The Prophet is not told to invent an answer from custom. He is told to say that Allah is the one who instructs. The teaching is revelation, not male convenience and not a reaction of anger.\n\n“Instruction has ˹already˺ been revealed in the Book concerning the orphan women you deprive of their due rights but still wish to marry, also helpless children, as well as standing up for orphans’ rights.” The questions are sent back to the opening of this chapter. Orphan women were being married for their property and denied a fair dowry or a fair standing. Helpless children were being erased from estates. Standing up for orphans was already commanded: give them their wealth, do not swap the good for the bad, take witnesses, fear for them as you would fear for your own. The people want a new fatwa. The Book says: you already have the instruction. The problem is not lack of verses. It is lack of standing up.\n\n“And whatever good you do is certainly well known to Allah.” A person who gives the orphan woman her right, who refuses a useful but unjust marriage, who speaks for a helpless child, is seen. The date-speck scale is still working. Good in this file is not wasted even if the town prefers the old habit.\n\nThe next verses will continue the women’s questions into a marriage that has grown cold. If a woman fears indifference or neglect, a fair settlement is allowed and called best. Humans incline to selfishness. If they separate, Allah will enrich both from His bounties. The instruction regarding women is not only about beginnings. It is about the middle and the end of a bond as well.",
  "128": "“If a woman fears indifference or neglect from her husband, there is no blame on either of them if they seek ˹fair˺ settlement, which is best.” The instruction regarding women now enters a cold marriage. Indifference is a turning of the face. Neglect is a turning of care, time, or support. The woman is not told she must smile and call the coldness a blessing. She may fear it, name it, and seek a fair settlement. “No blame on either of them” protects both from shame. Settlement is not a dirty bargain. It is called best.\n\nA fair settlement may mean she accepts a lighter share of his time or provision in order to keep a peaceful home, or they agree on terms that stop the harm. Early reports mentioned older wives who feared being set aside when a husband inclined to a newer marriage, and they agreed on arrangements rather than a bitter split. The verse does not force her to surrender rights under threat. “Fair” remains the fence.\n\n“Humans are ever inclined to selfishness.” The diagnosis is honest. A husband may neglect because the self wants a smoother companion. A wife may cling in a way that also has self in it. The verse names the disease so that settlement will not be written as if angels were negotiating.\n\n“But if you are gracious and mindful ˹of Allah˺, surely Allah is All-Aware of what you do.” Grace and mindfulness can save a house that law alone cannot warm. He is aware of the quiet giving, the quiet pressure, and the quiet fear. The next verse will admit that full emotional justice between wives is beyond human reach, then forbid total inclination that leaves one woman in suspense.",
  "129": "“You will never be able to maintain ˹emotional˺ justice between your wives—no matter how keen you are.” The permission of more than one wife, given at the start of the chapter, is now given its human limit. Emotional justice—the equal pull of the heart—is beyond people, even the keen and the sincere. Allah states the inability so that no man will claim an inner fairness he does not own. The heart leans. That leaning is not made into a god, and it is not denied.\n\n“So do not totally incline towards one leaving the other in suspense.” The command is about what he can control. He cannot make two loves identical. He can refuse to pour himself wholly into one wife while leaving the other hanging: neither truly married nor truly released, waiting at a door that does not open. Suspense is a cruelty this verse names. Time, nights, spending, and public honour must not be thrown to one side until the other becomes a ghost in the house.\n\n“And if you do what is right and are mindful ˹of Allah˺, surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” Doing right is the possible justice: fairness in what can be measured, kindness in speech, no suspense. Mindfulness watches the lean of the heart so it does not become injustice of the hand. Forgiving and Merciful means the remaining imbalance of feeling is not treated as a crime equal to neglect, if the man is trying and not abandoning.\n\nThe next verse will open the other honest door. If they choose to separate, Allah will enrich both from His bounties. He is Ever-Bountiful, All-Wise. A forced prison is not the only alternative to a cold house.",
  "130": "“But if they choose to separate, Allah will enrich both of them from His bounties.” Settlement was called best. It is not the only lawful end. If the couple choose to separate, the verse does not describe them as ruined. It describes Allah as able to enrich both. “Both” is the mercy. The man is not the only one who will find a future. The woman is not being thrown into a desert with no Lord. His bounties include a calmer life, a later lawful marriage, provision, and dignity after a bond that could not be repaired.\n\n“And Allah is Ever-Bountiful, All-Wise.” Ever-Bountiful means the store does not shrink when a marriage ends. All-Wise means He may enrich them in ways they would not have chosen, and the separation itself may be part of that wisdom. People stay in cruel houses sometimes because they fear poverty more than they fear Allah. This verse speaks to that fear.\n\nThe chapter that forbade inheriting women and snatching dowries is now forbidding another trap: using hunger to keep a woman in neglect. If they separate, He can enrich. The next verses will lift the eye from one household to the Owner of all households. To Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. He commanded those given Scripture before you, and you, to be mindful of Him. If you disobey, the kingdom is still His. He is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.\n\nThe next verses will lift the eye from one household to the Owner of all households. If He can enrich two people after a divorce, He can be trusted with every smaller fear that made them cling to a dead bond.",
  "131": "“To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.” The marriage rulings are placed again under ownership. He does not need a particular couple to remain together in order to remain King. He does not lose when they separate, and He does not gain when they stay. The heavens and the earth are already His.\n\n“Indeed, We have commanded those given the Scripture before you, as well as you, to be mindful of Allah.” Mindfulness is not a new slogan for Muslims. It is the old command to every community that received a Book. The People of the Book and this community stand under one demand: be mindful of the Owner. Family law, inheritance, and prayer are branches of that mindfulness.\n\n“But if you disobey, then ˹know that˺ to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. And Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.” Disobedience does not rob Him. It robs the disobedient. Self-Sufficient means He has no need of your mindfulness, your marriage, or your worship. Praiseworthy means He is worthy of praise whether you praise Him or not. The repetition of ownership in one verse is meant to empty pride. You do not do Allah a favour by remaining mindful. You do yourself a favour.\n\nThe next verse will say the ownership a third time in this cluster and add that Allah is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs. The one who fears separation, poverty, or the collapse of a house is being handed a Trustee larger than the house.",
  "132": "“To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.” Said again, so close to the last saying, the line is not filler. Some hearts need ownership repeated until fear loosens. The neglected wife, the man who cannot split his heart evenly, the couple about to separate, the community afraid of enemies: all of them live inside a kingdom that already has an Owner.\n\n“And Allah is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs.” Trustee of Affairs means the management of things can be placed with Him. The hypocrites were told, in meaning, that they take false trustees. The Prophet was told Allah is sufficient as a Trustee when people scheme at night. Now the ordinary believer is told the same in the middle of household fear. Sufficient means you may do what is right—settle, be fair, or separate—and then leave the outcome with Him.\n\nA trustee who owns the heavens does not misplace a woman’s provision or a man’s future. People fail as trustees of one orphan file. He does not fail as Trustee of all affairs.\n\nThe next verse will make the ownership felt as power. If He wills, He can remove you altogether, O humanity, and replace you with others. He is Most Capable to do so. The Self-Sufficient King is not trapped with a disobedient species. Mindfulness is honour for us, not a need in Him.\n\nA believer who has just been told that separation need not mean ruin should hear this line as the reason. The Trustee owns the earth into which both will walk. No relative and no former spouse owns their provision the way He does.",
  "133": "“If it is His Will, He can remove you altogether, O humanity, and replace you with others.” The address widens from one marriage to the whole human race. Removal altogether is not a small loss of one town. It is the taking away of a people and the bringing of another. The last chapter said similar words about replacing people. This verse says them after commanding mindfulness. If you will not be mindful, you are not the last possible servants. Others can be brought.\n\n“And Allah is Most Capable to do so.” Capability has been named after many threats in these chapters. Here it sits under a cosmic replacement. He is capable of ending a nation that inherited women, ate orphans, and then claimed to be necessary to God. He is capable of raising a people who follow Abraham’s way more honestly.\n\nThe verse should humble a community that has begun to feel permanent. Revelation, a prophet, and a new city can produce a subtle pride: we are the replacement. This verse says you too can be replaced. Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy, Capable: He will not be left without worshippers.\n\nThe next verse will speak to the person who heard all this and still wants only the world. Whoever desires the reward of this world should know that with Allah are the rewards of this world and the Hereafter. He is All-Hearing, All-Seeing. Why ask the Owner of both homes for only one?\n\nSo the command to be mindful is an honour offered to a people who could be swapped. A wise community answers with gratitude, not with the feeling that God has no other servants.",
  "134": "“Whoever desires the reward of this world, then ˹let them know that˺ with Allah are the rewards of this world and the Hereafter.” Some people, after hearing of separation, replacement, and the kingdom, still aim their religion at a worldly prize: safety, wealth, a better marriage, victory. The verse does not say the world has no reward. It says the One they are asking holds both rewards. To ask Him only for this world is to treat the Owner of two homes as if He owned a stall.\n\nThis returns to the strivers who sacrifice this life for the Hereafter, and to the laggers who only wanted the great gain. It also speaks to a person staying in an unjust marriage, or leaving one, only for money. With Allah are both. Ask for the better, and the lesser may come in a cleaner way.\n\n“And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.” He hears the desire under the prayer. He sees whether a person uses His name to seek only a house in this world. Hearing and seeing make the verse a check on hidden aims, not only a piece of information about two treasuries.\n\nThe next verse will name one of the highest ways to seek the lasting reward. Stand firm for justice as witnesses for Allah, even against yourselves, your parents, or close relatives. Do not let desire pull the testimony. If you distort it or refuse it, He is All-Aware.\n\nThe Prophet and the poor migrants had left a world-reward in Mecca. This verse tells them they did not leave Allah’s store. They left one room of it and were standing nearer the better room.",
  "135": "“O believers! Stand firm for justice as witnesses for Allah even if it is against yourselves, your parents, or close relatives.” This is one of the peak commands of the chapter. Justice is not a mood. It is a standing. “Witnesses for Allah” means the testimony is offered to Him, not to a family, a class, or a party. The hardest list comes at once: yourselves, parents, close relatives. The orphan file, the theft thrown on an innocent man, the widow’s share, the neglected wife: all of these become tests of this verse when the guilty person is you or yours.\n\n“Be they rich or poor, Allah is best to ensure their interests.” People twist justice toward the rich from fear and toward the poor from a pity that can also lie. The verse forbids both twists. Allah is better than you at looking after the rich person’s honour and the poor person’s need. Your job is the straight word.\n\n“So do not let your desires cause you to deviate ˹from justice˺. If you distort the testimony or refuse to give it, then ˹know that˺ Allah is certainly All-Aware of what you do.” Desire may be love of a parent, hatred of an enemy, or hunger for rest. Distortion is changing the testimony. Refusal is swallowing it. Both are seen. The Watcher of the first verse is still the Watcher of the court.\n\nThe next verse will ground this justice in a fuller faith. Believe in Allah, His Messenger, the Book revealed to the Messenger, and the Scriptures revealed before. Whoever denies Allah, His angels, His Books, His messengers, and the Last Day has gone far astray. A witness who does not believe in the Last Day will not stand long against his own father.",
  "136": "“O believers! Have faith in Allah, His Messenger, the Book He has revealed to His Messenger, and the Scriptures He revealed before.” People already called believers are told to believe. The command means: make the faith complete, keep it, and do not treat it as a half-list. Allah, His Messenger, this Quran, and the earlier Scriptures: the chain is one. Denying the last Book while claiming the earlier ones was the fault of some People of the Book. Denying the earlier Scriptures while claiming the last would be another kind of split. The believers’ creed in the last chapter already said they make no distinction between the messengers.\n\n“Indeed, whoever denies Allah, His angels, His Books, His messengers, and the Last Day has clearly gone far astray.” Five denials are named as one far stray. Angels are included because rejecting the unseen servants is rejecting part of the truth of revelation. Books and messengers are plural. The Last Day is the day that makes standing for justice against yourself reasonable. Without it, the witness-verse becomes heroic talk.\n\nThis verse prepares the next warning about people who keep flipping: they believed, then disbelieved, then believed, then disbelieved, only increasing in disbelief. A faith that is a complete list can still be betrayed by a heart that treats it like a coat, on and off. Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to the Right Way while they live in that increase.\n\nStanding firm for justice against your own father becomes possible when the Last Day is part of the creed. Without that Day, family heat will almost always win the testimony.",
  "137": "“Indeed, those who believed then disbelieved, then believed and again disbelieved—˹only˺ increasing in disbelief—Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to the ˹Right˺ Way.” The last chapter spoke of people who disbelieved after belief then increased in disbelief, and said their repentance would not be accepted. This verse adds the ugly pattern of going back and forth. They enter, leave, enter, leave, and the movement is not a sincere struggle. It is an increase in disbelief. Each return is lighter, each leaving is easier, until faith becomes a game.\n\n“Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to the Right Way” is the same kind of severe sentence met before. It describes people who use the door of faith as a revolving door and die on that increase, or who remain in it so long that their turning is no longer a turning. It is not a lock on a person who fell once, wept, and came back in truth. The last chapter still said those who repent afterwards and mend their ways find Allah All-Forgiving. The two teachings stay together: a real return is loved; a habit of leaving is a path He does not bless with guidance.\n\nThe next verses will name the people in view more clearly. Give good news of a painful punishment to hypocrites, who choose disbelievers as allies instead of the believers. They seek honour through that company, while all honour belongs to Allah. The flip of belief is often tied to a flip of alliance.",
  "138": "“Give good news of a painful punishment to hypocrites,” is a bitter use of a sweet word. Good news is normally for gardens and forgiveness. Here the Prophet is told to announce a painful punishment as if it were news they had been waiting for. The irony is intended. They wanted good news of safety no matter what they did with their alliances and their prayers. This is the news that fits them.\n\nThe verse is short because the next one defines them. Hypocrites, in this cluster, are those who choose disbelievers as allies instead of the believers, seeking honour in that company. The painful punishment is not hung on a weak moment of fear. It is hung on a chosen loyalty.\n\nThis also continues the revolving-door verse. A person who believes and disbelieves again often does it because another company looks more honourable, more safe, more useful. The “good news” is that such a calculation has a price.\n\nThe next verse will ask whether they seek honour and power through that company. Surely all honour and power belongs to Allah. Taking disbelievers as allies instead of believers is therefore not only a political error. It is a misreading of where honour lives.\n\nThe shortness of the sentence is part of its blow. No extra story is given yet. The next verse will define the people and the honour they were hunting. The news has already been named: painful punishment. The hypocrite who waited for good news of safety is handed news of fire instead, so that the waiting itself is judged.",
  "139": "“who choose disbelievers as allies instead of the believers.” The hypocrites of the previous verse are defined by a choice of company. “Instead of” is the wound. Kindness, justice, and treaties with peaceful people have already been allowed and even commanded in this Book. What is condemned is taking those who reject Allah as the inner allies—the people of secrets, honour, and help—while pushing the believers aside.\n\n“Do they seek honour and power through that company? Surely all honour and power belongs to Allah.” The motive is diagnosed. They think the stronger-looking camp can give them honour. The last chapter said Allah is the Guardian and the best Helper. This verse says honour and power are His. A company that does not have them cannot hand them over. At most it can hand over a brief protection and a long humiliation.\n\nThe question form is meant to wake them. Is that what you are hunting—honour from people who have no honour to give? The believers who feel small beside a richer, older, or more armed group are being told the same thing. Do not sell the alliance of faith for a borrowed pride.\n\nThe next verse will give a practical rule for sitting. When you hear Allah’s revelations denied or ridiculed, do not sit in that company unless they change the topic, or you will be like them. Allah will gather the hypocrites and disbelievers together in Hell. Alliance begins, sometimes, with a chair.\n\nTreaties and fair dealing remain standing. What falls is the inner leaning that says, “These, not the believers, are my honour.” That leaning is what this verse calls choosing allies instead of the believers.",
  "140": "“He has already revealed to you in the Book that when you hear Allah’s revelations being denied or ridiculed, then do not sit in that company unless they engage in a different topic, or else you will be like them.” A rule already given—in the chapter of the Cattle and in the spirit of this Book—is recalled. If revelation is being denied or made a joke, the believer’s chair becomes a witness. Remaining, laughing, or being silent in a way that helps the ridicule makes a person “like them.” The likeness is in sharing the sitting of mockery, not in every hidden grade of the heart. Still the warning is heavy. Leave, or wait until the topic changes.\n\n“Surely Allah will gather the hypocrites and disbelievers all together in Hell.” The sitting that looked mixed in this world becomes one gathering there. Hypocrites wanted to be with whoever was winning. They end up with the people whose ridicule they tolerated. The last chapter said Allah will gather all on a Day without doubt. This verse names one of those gatherings: mockers and those who made themselves at home with mockery.\n\nThe next verse will show the hypocrites waiting to see what happens. If you win, they say they were with you. If the disbelievers get a share, they say they protected them from the believers. Allah will judge between all of you. He will never grant the disbelievers a way over the believers. The chair and the alliance are one story.",
  "141": "“˹The hypocrites are˺ those who wait to see what happens to you.” Their religion is a weather report. They do not commit. They wait. The believers’ next victory or wound will decide the sentence they speak.\n\n“So if Allah grants you victory, they say ˹to you˺, ‘Were we not on your side?’ But if the disbelievers have a share ˹of victory˺, they say ˹to them˺, ‘Did we not have the advantage over you, yet we protected you from the believers?’” Two speeches, two audiences, one soul. To the believers they claim companionship in the win. To the disbelievers they claim they held the Muslims back and were a shield. This is the same double tongue as the laggers who blessed themselves in a disaster and wished for a share in a gain. Here the tongue is even busier. It writes two histories of the same day.\n\n“Allah will judge between ˹all of˺ you on the Day of Judgment.” The two speeches will not both survive. Judgment will pick the truth. “And Allah will never grant the disbelievers a way over the believers.” The final way, the lasting domination that would cancel the faith, will not be given to them. Worldly wounds can happen, as Uhud happened. A granted, lasting way over the believers is denied. That promise steadies a community that hears the hypocrites offering themselves as a protection to the enemy.\n\nThe next verse will take us from their politics to their prayer. They seek to deceive Allah, but He outwits them. They stand for prayer half-heartedly, only to be seen, hardly remembering Allah at all.",
  "142": "“Surely the hypocrites seek to deceive Allah, but He outwits them.” They think the double speech, the waiting, and the show of prayer can fool the Lord of the worlds. The verse says the opposite happens. He outwits them. Their plot becomes a rope for themselves. The last chapter said they deceive none but themselves. This verse says Allah’s counter is already in motion while they still think they are clever.\n\n“When they stand up for prayer, they do it half-heartedly only to be seen by people—hardly remembering Allah at all.” The prayer exposes them more than the battlefield speeches. They stand, so they cannot be called open abandoners. They stand half-heartedly: lazy, late, lifeless. They stand to be seen: the eyes they want are human eyes. They hardly remember Allah, so the prayer has no inside. The people of reason in the last chapter remembered Allah standing, sitting, and lying down. These people stand and still do not remember.\n\nBeing seen by people is the same disease as spending to show off. The audience has replaced the One who is All-Seeing.\n\nThe next verse will describe their position between camps. Torn between belief and disbelief, belonging neither to these nor those. Whoever Allah leaves to stray, you will never find for them a way. The half-hearted prayer is the worship of a person who does not belong anywhere.\n\nA person who hates his own show and asks Allah to give his prayer a heart is not the target of this portrait. The target is the one who wants the show and does not want Allah. The half-hearted rising is his tool, not his sorrow.",
  "143": "“Torn between belief and disbelief—belonging neither to these ˹believers˺ nor those ˹disbelievers˺.” The hypocrite’s inner life is a tearing. He is not settled with the believers, because their path costs him. He is not openly with the disbelievers, because he still wants the safety or the name of faith. “Neither these nor those” is a homeless sentence. He thought double alliance would give him two homes. It gave him none.\n\nThis is the spiritual meaning of waiting to see what happens. A person who always waits never arrives. The believers at least know where they stand. The open disbelievers at least know what they have refused. The torn person spends his life adjusting his face.\n\n“And whoever Allah leaves to stray, you will never find for them a way.” The line returns from the start of this hypocrite-section. When a person insists on the tear, Allah may leave him in it. No friend, no prophet, no clever plan will invent a third path that is still the Straight Path. The way is one. Torn is not a way.\n\nThe next verse will tell the believers not to copy the alliance. Do not take disbelievers as allies instead of the believers. Would you like to give Allah solid proof against yourselves? The torn people’s chair is not an offer the believers should accept.\n\nThe believers should not try to build a third camp to keep such people comfortable. The way is one. Belonging will hurt before it heals. Torn is only a waiting room for stray.",
  "144": "“O believers! Do not take disbelievers as allies instead of the believers.” The command given as a description of hypocrites is now given as a law to people of faith. Do not make their choice your choice. “Instead of the believers” remains the key. Justice toward peaceful non-Muslims, honoured treaties, and kind neighbourliness are not this forbidden alliance. The forbidden thing is handing inner loyalty, honour, and help to those who reject Allah while leaving the believers without that loyalty.\n\n“Would you like to give Allah solid proof against yourselves?” The question is meant to sting. Choosing that alliance is not a silent private taste. It becomes proof against you: a witness on the Day when hypocrites and disbelievers are gathered together. Solid proof means the case will not be foggy. You will have acted your preference.\n\nThe believers who felt weak after losses are being protected from a bargain that looks like safety and is actually evidence. Honour belongs to Allah. He is sufficient as Guardian and Helper. Taking the other camp as your honour is writing a proof that you did not believe that.\n\nThe next verse will state the end of the unrepentant hypocrites. They will be in the lowest depths of the Fire, and you will never find for them any helper—except those who repent, mend, hold fast to Allah, and are sincere. The lowest depths are not the last word for a person who still turns.\n\nThe last verses showed how hypocrites use alliance as a weather-vane. This command stops the believers from becoming weather-vanes with them. Proof against yourselves is a high price for a little borrowed honour.",
  "145": "“Surely the hypocrites will be in the lowest depths of the Fire—and you will never find for them any helper—” The torn people who mocked, waited, and prayed to be seen are given the lowest depths, not a middle punishment. Hypocrisy is treated as worse than open disbelief in this ranking because it joins rejection to a lie about faith. They used the believers as cover, then offered cover to the disbelievers. The lowest place matches a double betrayal.\n\n“You will never find for them any helper” continues a refrain of these two chapters. No ally they chose, no honour they borrowed, no company they sat with in ridicule will climb down to lift them. The sentence is left hanging into the next verse on purpose. The listener is meant to feel the drop, then hear the exception.\n\nA common misuse is to call every sinful Muslim a hypocrite of the lowest depths. The Quran’s hypocrites in this passage are people of a chosen double life, increasing in disbelief, taking the enemy as allies instead of the believers. Ordinary weakness, a lazy prayer a person hates in himself, a fear he fights, is not this verse’s first target.\n\nThe next verse is the rope thrown into the depths before they close. Except those who repent, mend their ways, hold fast to Allah, and are sincere in their devotion. They will be with the believers. Allah will grant the believers a great reward. The lowest depths are for those who refuse that rope.",
  "146": "“except those who repent, mend their ways, hold fast to Allah, and are sincere in their devotion to Allah; they will be with the believers.” Four acts pull a person out of the hypocrite’s end. Repent: turn from the double life. Mend their ways: change the alliances, the mockery-sittings, the show-prayer. Hold fast to Allah: take Him as the Guardian they used to seek in other company. Be sincere in devotion: do the prayer and the loyalty for Him, not to be seen. Then comes the honour: they will be with the believers. The torn person can belong somewhere after all.\n\n“And Allah will grant the believers a great reward.” The returning person shares the believers’ reward. He is not kept at the door as a permanent suspect if the four acts are real. This matches the last chapter’s pardon of those who fled and then were pardoned, and the promise that whoever seeks forgiveness will find Allah All-Forgiving.\n\nSincerity is named because hypocrisy was, at root, a disease of the audience. They lived for eyes. Healing is to live for the One who is All-Aware.\n\nThe next verse will ask a question that makes punishment look strange for people who have become grateful and faithful. Why should Allah punish you if you are grateful and faithful? He is ever Appreciative, All-Knowing. The door is not only escape from the lowest depths. It is entry into a relationship with a Lord who appreciates.\n\nThe four acts also protect the community. A returning person is not asked to wear a hypocrite-sign for life. If he holds fast and is sincere, he is with the believers, and their great reward is not locked against him.",
  "147": "“Why should Allah punish you if you are grateful and faithful?” The question is a kindness after the lowest depths. Punishment is not something Allah hunts for. If the servant is grateful and faithful, what reason remains for punishment? Gratitude is the opposite of the hypocrite’s use of Allah’s name for honour. Faithfulness is the opposite of the revolving door. Together they describe a person who has taken the exception of the last verse and made it a life.\n\n“Allah is ever Appreciative, All-Knowing.” Appreciative means He values the small sincere good, multiplies it, and does not forget a hidden holding-fast. People failed to appreciate the orphan, the neglected wife, the honest witness. He does not fail to appreciate. All-Knowing means He knows whether the gratitude is real or another show. The names protect both hope and honesty.\n\nThis verse should quiet two fears. The fear that God enjoys punishing is answered by the question. The fear that a returning hypocrite has no future is answered by the names. Be grateful. Be faithful. He knows, and He appreciates.\n\nThe next verses will regulate the tongue that still wants to complain. Allah does not like negative thoughts to be voiced, except by those who have been wronged. Whether you reveal or conceal a good, or pardon an evil, He is Ever-Pardoning, Most Capable. After hypocrisy’s double tongue, the believers are taught a single, clean use of speech.\n\nGrateful and faithful: the two words are a whole programme after a long passage on double tongues. Thank Him for the rope. Be true in the hand that holds it. He already knows, and He appreciates.",
  "148": "“Allah does not like negative thoughts to be voiced—except by those who have been wronged.” After teaching a clean tongue to people leaving hypocrisy, the Quran regulates complaint. Negative thoughts voiced are the public spreading of evil: insults, exposés of faults, and talk that dirties a name. Allah does not like that habit. The exception is just. A person who has been wronged may speak the wrong: in a court, to a judge, to someone who can stop the harm, or in a way that names the injury without becoming a new injustice. Silence forced on a victim is not the piety this verse wants.\n\nThe exception must stay an exception. Being slightly annoyed is not being wronged. The chapter has already refused slander of the innocent and advocacy for the deceitful. This verse does not reopen those doors under the name of hurt feelings.\n\n“Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.” He hears the victim who has no audience, and He knows the person who uses “I was wronged” as a cover for a campaign of dirt. Hearing and knowing make both the silence of the oppressed and the noise of the malicious visible to Him.\n\nThe next verse will praise a better path when it is possible. Whether you reveal or conceal a good, or pardon an evil, Allah is Ever-Pardoning, Most Capable. Speaking a wrong may be allowed. Pardoning it, when safety and rights allow, is loved by a Lord who Himself pardons.\n\nA community trained by this verse becomes safer for the weak and quieter in its gatherings. The injured can speak. The rest do not live on fault-finding. Allah hears both the needed word and the extra one.",
  "149": "“Whether you reveal or conceal a good or pardon an evil—surely Allah is Ever-Pardoning, Most Capable.” Good may be shown or hidden. Both can be sincere. A revealed good may teach others. A concealed good may be freer of show. The verse does not force one style. It says both are in His sight. The earlier warning about spending to be seen still stands. Concealment is often safer for the heart. Revelation is sometimes needed for a community.\n\n“Or pardon an evil” is the high option after the last verse’s permission to speak. The wronged person may talk. He may also pardon. Pardon here is not a denial of the crime and not a cancel of a public right that others still need. It is the personal dropping of revenge when one can. Ever-Pardoning means Allah loves this quality because it is one of His own. Most Capable means He can still repay the wrong even if you let it go. You are not leaving justice undone in the universe. You are leaving it with the One able to handle it.\n\nThis pair of verses trains a community that has suffered thefts, mockery, and neglect. Do not become a people of constant negative speech. Do not gag the injured. Do good, hidden or seen. Pardon when you can. The next verses will leave the ethics of the tongue and return to a creed-crime: those who deny Allah and His messengers and want a distinction between them, believing in some and not others, seeking a compromise. They are the true disbelievers.",
  "150": "“Surely those who deny Allah and His messengers and wish to make a distinction between Allah and His messengers, saying, ‘We believe in some and disbelieve in others,’ desiring to forge a compromise,” The sentence is unfinished until the next verse, but the crime is already clear. Some deny Allah and the messengers together. Some want a more polite disbelief: we accept Allah, we accept our favourite messengers, we reject the rest. That split is called a distinction between Allah and His messengers, because refusing the messenger He sent is refusing His sending.\n\n“We believe in some and disbelieve in others” was the slogan of people who took Moses and dropped Jesus, or took Jesus and dropped Muhammad, or took the idea of God and dropped the actual law. “Desiring to forge a compromise” means they want a middle religion that keeps their pride and still looks like faith. The last chapter called those who believe in some and disbelieve in others the true disbelievers. This verse begins that same judgment again.\n\nThe believers’ creed has been the opposite: we make no distinction, we submit. A compromise that edits the list of messengers is not humility. It is a new religion built to avoid the messenger who costs them something.\n\nThe next verse will finish the grammar. They are indeed the true disbelievers. A humiliating punishment is prepared. Then the opposite people will be named: those who believe in Allah and His messengers, accepting all, rejecting none. He will give them their rewards. He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  "151": "“they are indeed the true disbelievers.” The people who split the messengers, believing in some and refusing others, are not called near-believers or people of a small mistake. They are called the true disbelievers. “True” here means this is disbelief in its real form, even when it wears a religious sentence. A person who says “we believe” and then rejects the messenger sent to him is not in a safer category than a person who rejects openly. He has refused Allah’s choice.\n\n“And We have prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment.” Prepared means the end is not improvised. Humiliating fits the crime. They wanted a compromise that would save their honour among their group. They are paid with humiliation. The last chapter used the same pairing for people who used longer life to increase in sin.\n\nThis judgment must sit beside the many verses that still praise those among the People of the Book who believe in what was revealed to them and to the believers. Those people are not in this sentence. They accepted all. They rejected none. The next verse will honour them by name of action. As for those who believe in Allah and His messengers—accepting all, rejecting none—He will surely give them their rewards. Forgiveness and mercy remain the names over that reward.\n\nThe next verse will honour the opposite people at once, so that this hard name is not the only name left in the air. Accepting all messengers is still open. The humiliating punishment is for those who refuse that openness and die on the split.",
  "152": "“As for those who believe in Allah and His messengers—accepting all; rejecting none—He will surely give them their rewards.” The opposite of the compromise is stated in four words of meaning: believe, messengers, all, none rejected. This is the faith of the people of reason who heard the caller and believed, and of the upright among the People of the Book who did not trade revelation. “Surely give them their rewards” answers the fear that leaving a group-slogan will leave a person unpaid. The payment is sure.\n\n“And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” Even this complete faith is not a claim of personal perfection. They still need forgiveness and mercy. Accepting all messengers does not mean they never sinned. It means they did not edit the list of Allah’s envoys. Mercy covers the rest when they seek it.\n\nThe next passage will show how demanding some people were even after messengers came. The People of the Book demand that you bring down a revelation in writing from heaven. They demanded something greater from Moses: make Allah visible. A thunderbolt struck them. Then they took the calf. Still He forgave them after they repented and gave Moses proof. The history of demands is being recalled so that a new demand for a written sheet from the sky will not look like sincere scholarship.\n\nThis is the same wide faith the last chapter put on the believers’ tongues. It is not a Muslim invention against older communities. It is the old honesty: Allah sent many, and we do not drop the ones we dislike.",
  "153": "“The People of the Book demand that you ˹O Prophet˺ bring down for them a revelation in writing from heaven.” The demand sounds like a wish for stronger evidence. In the flow of this chapter it is another stall. They have Scripture already. The Quran confirms it. Still they ask for a sheet to descend in writing, as if ink from the clouds would heal a heart that refused ink already in their hands.\n\n“They demanded what is even greater than this from Moses, saying, ‘Make Allah visible to us!’ So a thunderbolt struck them for their wrongdoing.” The present demand is placed beside an older, larger one. Seeing Allah with their eyes in this world was asked in arrogance, not in love. The thunderbolt was a punishment for that wrongdoing. The story is told in the chapter of the Cow. This verse uses it to say: your habit of raising the price after the goods have arrived is not new.\n\n“Then they took the calf for worship after receiving clear signs. Still We forgave them for that ˹after their repentance˺ and gave Moses compelling proof.” After a demand to see God, some worshipped a calf. The contrast is meant to stun. Clear signs did not prevent the calf. Forgiveness still came after repentance. Proof was still given to Moses. So neither their history of demands nor their history of falls is being hidden. Mercy after repentance is also not being hidden.\n\nThe next verse will recall more of that covenant history: the Mount raised over them, the command to enter the gate in humility, the Sabbath warning, and a firm covenant. The people who now demand a new writing already stood under a mountain of warning and still broke what they promised.",
  "154": "“We raised the Mount over them ˹as a warning˺ for ˹breaking˺ their covenant and said, ‘Enter the gate ˹of Jerusalem˺ with humility.’” The covenant was not taken in a casual meeting. The Mount raised over them is a picture of how serious the pledge was. Entering the gate with humility was a command of body and soul: do not walk into a blessing as if you owned it. The chapter of the Cow told that some changed the word they were told to say and entered in a manner other than humility. This verse recalls the original order.\n\n“We also warned them, ‘Do not break the Sabbath,’ and took from them a firm covenant.” The Sabbath was a sign for them, a day set apart. Breaking it with tricks—as other verses describe with the fishing town—was breaking a firm covenant, not a small custom. “Firm” means they had no excuse of vagueness. Allah took the pledge. They knew.\n\nThe verse is part of a charge-sheet that the next verses will read out: they were condemned for breaking the covenant, rejecting signs, killing prophets, saying their hearts were unreceptive, denying Mary, and boasting that they killed the Messiah. A people who ask the last Prophet for a new writing from heaven are being reminded of how they treated the old, firm writing.\n\nA listener from any community should also hear a warning. Covenants under mountains of evidence can still be broken by pride. The cure is the humility they were told to walk with through the gate.",
  "155": "“˹They were condemned˺ for breaking their covenant, rejecting Allah’s signs, killing the prophets unjustly, and for saying, ‘Our hearts are unreceptive!’” Four crimes are listed as causes, not as slurs on every later Jew or Christian. Breaking the covenant: the firm pledge just mentioned. Rejecting signs: clear proofs treated as nothing. Killing prophets unjustly: a crime this Book will not soften with the word “history.” Saying “our hearts are unreceptive”: a boast of being closed, as if hardness were a defence. In the Cow they also said their hearts were wrapped. Here the claim is turned back on them.\n\n“it is Allah Who has sealed their hearts for their disbelief, so they do not believe except for a few—” The seal is not an injustice that came before their choice. It is a seal for their disbelief. They closed, then they were closed. “Except for a few” is the door this chapter will not nail shut. The few who believe are real. The last verses of the previous chapter praised them. A coming verse will praise those well-grounded in knowledge who believe in what was revealed to you and what was revealed before.\n\nThe sentence continues into the next verses with more causes: their denial and outrageous accusation against Mary, and their boast that they killed the Messiah. The charge-sheet is long because the refusal of the last messenger did not begin on a blank page. It grew from an old habit of meeting Allah’s gifts with breach, blood, and a claim of closed hearts.",
  "156": "“and for their denial and outrageous accusation against Mary,” The list of causes continues. Denial here is denial of the truth Allah stated about Mary and her son: that she was chaste, that the child was a word and a mercy, that the birth was a sign, not a stain. The “outrageous accusation” is the filthy charge some aimed at her, treating the miracle as a crime. The Quran has already honoured Mary in the last chapter as a woman who guarded her chastity and believed the words of her Lord. This verse names the insult against her as one of the reasons for condemnation.\n\nThe wording is brief and severe. It does not repeat the accusation. It calls it outrageous and moves on. A believer should copy that restraint. The point is to defend her honour, not to drag the slander through another telling.\n\nMary’s honour also prepares the next, larger correction. They boasted, “We killed the Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, the messenger of Allah.” The Quran will deny the killing and the crucifixion, say it was made to appear so, and say they certainly did not kill him. Rather, Allah raised him. The people who slandered the mother also claimed victory over the son. Both claims are being broken in sequence.\n\nHonouring Mary is part of honouring Allah’s signs. A religion that stains her in order to deny the miracle is not defending purity. It is attacking a woman Allah chose to defend. The next verse will break a second claim about her son. The mother is cleared. The boast about killing the son will not stand either.",
  "157": "“and for boasting, ‘We killed the Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, the messenger of Allah.’” The boast is itself a crime in this list. They name him Messiah, son of Mary, messenger of Allah—titles of honour—and then claim to have killed him. The sentence is proud. It treats a messenger’s death, as they imagined it, as a trophy. The Quran first records the boast, then dismantles it.\n\n“But they neither killed nor crucified him—it was only made to appear so.” The denial is double: not killed, not crucified. “Made to appear so” means the event they thought they had secured was an appearance, not the reality of taking his life. How that appearance was arranged is not spelled out here. Honest scholars have discussed details. What the verse itself requires is firm: they did not achieve the killing they advertised.\n\n“Even those who argue for this ˹crucifixion˺ are in doubt. They have no knowledge whatsoever—only making assumptions. They certainly did not kill him.” Argument around the event is described as doubt and assumption, not knowledge. The closing line repeats the core fact. They certainly did not kill him. A community that built later theology on that killing is being told the foundation is not knowledge.\n\nThe next verse will say what did happen, in the Quran’s terms. Rather, Allah raised him up to Himself. Allah is Almighty, All-Wise. The messenger they tried to turn into a trophy was taken by the One who sent him.\n\nA believer should not add what the verse did not say, nor drop what it did say. They did not kill him. They did not crucify him. Appearance is not the same as the deed they claimed.",
  "158": "“Rather, Allah raised him up to Himself.” After denying the killing and the crucifixion, the Quran states the alternative in a short, high sentence. Jesus was not left in their hands. Allah raised him to Himself. The last chapter said, “O Jesus! I will take you and raise you up to Myself.” This verse agrees. Raised to Himself is honour, protection, and a nearness that their plot could not touch. It is not a statement that Jesus became a partner in the throne. The same passage will soon say he would never be too proud to be a servant, and that Allah is far above having a son.\n\n“And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.” Almighty means their soldiers, wood, and boasts had no power to finish a messenger Allah willed to raise. All-Wise means the appearance they were left with, the later arguments, and the raising itself belong to a wisdom that will be completed, including Jesus’ witness on the Day of Judgment and, as other texts of this religion teach, his return before the end. This verse does not tell that whole later story. It tells the fact needed here: they did not kill him. His Lord raised him.\n\nThe next verse will add that every one of the People of the Book will definitely believe in him before his death, and that on the Day of Judgment Jesus will be a witness against them. The man they claimed to have finished will be a witness they cannot cross-examine away.",
  "159": "“Every one of the People of the Book will definitely believe in him before his death.” The wording has been read in more than one honest way. Many teachers said “his death” is the death of Jesus after Allah returns him, and that the People of the Book will then believe in him as he truly is: a messenger, not a god, and not a man they killed. Others said each person among them will see the truth about Jesus at the point of his own death, when guessing ends. Both readings share one point: the truth about Jesus will not remain a permanent open argument. Belief in him, as he actually is, will come.\n\n“And on the Day of Judgment Jesus will be a witness against them.” Witness against them means he will testify to what he was commanded to say and to what they did with that message. In the last chapter he is imagined answering Allah that he told people only to worship Allah, his Lord and their Lord. This verse fits that scene. The people who slandered his mother, or called him God, or boasted of killing him, will not find him silent.\n\nThe believer should hold what is clear and be careful with what is not spelled out. Clear: they did not kill him; Allah raised him; he will be a witness. Details of the timetable belong to other texts and to Allah’s knowledge. The next verses will turn from Jesus to a different part of the charge-sheet: certain foods were forbidden to the Jews for wrongdoing, for hindering from Allah’s way, for taking interest, and for consuming wealth unjustly.",
  "160": "“We forbade the Jews certain foods that had been lawful to them for their wrongdoing, and for hindering many from the Way of Allah,” The last chapter had already said that all food was lawful for the children of Israel except what Israel made unlawful for himself before the Torah, and that later forbidding was a repayment for violations. This verse states that repayment again. Foods that had been lawful were made unlawful because of wrongdoing and because they hindered many from Allah’s way. The restriction was not a mark of being more holy. It was a mark of a crime.\n\n“Hindering many from the Way of Allah” is a public harm. A people of knowledge who stand in the road and turn others away—by hiding, mixing, or mocking the confirming messenger—are not only spoiling themselves. They are spoiling others. The food-law is tied to that social crime so that no one will treat it as a random diet.\n\nThe sentence continues into the next verse with more causes: taking interest despite its prohibition, and consuming people’s wealth unjustly. A painful punishment is prepared for the disbelievers among them. “Among them” matters. The well-grounded in knowledge and the faithful who pray and pay the alms-tax will be excepted and rewarded. The charge is not a racial curse. It is a moral account.\n\nSo a later listener should not read those food-rules as a badge of nearness and miss the warning. Wrongdoing and blocking the way can turn even a table into a testimony against a people.",
  "161": "“taking interest despite its prohibition, and consuming people’s wealth unjustly.” The list of causes for the later food-restrictions and for punishment continues with money-crimes this chapter has already forbidden to the believers. Interest was prohibited to them, and they took it anyway. Consuming wealth unjustly includes the orphan’s pile, the false case, the bribe, and the devouring of one another without consent. The last chapter banned multiplied interest. This chapter banned illegal devouring. The People of the Book are not being measured by a stranger’s law. They are being measured by a law they already had and by crimes this Book names for everyone.\n\n“We have prepared for the disbelievers among them a painful punishment.” “Among them” is the justice-word. Disbelievers among them, not every person of Jewish lineage, not the well-grounded who will be praised next. Prepared, painful: the same seriousness given to hypocrites and to those who kill a believer intentionally. Money-crimes and hindering from the way are not treated as a side file.\n\nThe next verse will show the other face immediately, as this Book loves to do. Those of them well-grounded in knowledge, who believe in what was revealed to you and what was revealed before, who establish prayer, pay the alms-tax, and believe in Allah and the Last Day, will be granted a great reward. The charge-sheet was never meant to erase these people.\n\nThe believers who now hear the ban on interest and unjust consumption are being shown that they are not the first to be tested with money. An older community was tested, and some failed. The painful punishment is not a relic. It is a warning still live.",
  "162": "“But those of them well-grounded in knowledge, the faithful ˹who˺ believe in what has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ and what was revealed before you—” After a long hard list, the verse turns with “but.” Well-grounded in knowledge are not people of a few memorised lines used as weapons. They are people whose knowledge has roots. That rootedness shows in what they believe: this revelation and the earlier one. They do not make the distinction that made others true disbelievers.\n\n“˹especially˺ those who establish prayer—and those who pay alms-tax and believe in Allah and the Last Day, to these ˹people˺ We will grant a great reward.” Prayer, alms, Allah, the Last Day: the same marks given to the upright in the last chapter who recited at night and raced in good. Establish prayer is highlighted, perhaps because a rooted scholar who does not pray has a knowledge that has not yet reached his limbs. The great reward is the same phrase used for strivers, secret reconcilers, and sincere repenters. It is not a smaller prize for people who came from another community. It is the great reward.\n\nThis verse should govern the believer’s mouth when he reads the hard verses before it. If Allah paused to honour these people, a Muslim may not wipe them away with a general insult. The next verses will place the Prophet himself in the line of those they already accept: revelation as it was sent to Noah, Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, Jesus, Job, Jonah, Aaron, Solomon, and David who was given the Psalms.",
  "163": "“Indeed, We have sent revelation to you ˹O Prophet˺ as We sent revelation to Noah and the prophets after him.” The last messenger is not a new species of human. Revelation came to him as it came to Noah and to those after him. “As We sent” is the word that breaks both arrogance and rejection. He is not inventing. They are not being asked to leave the family of prophets. They are being asked to recognise the same sending.\n\n“We also sent revelation to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants, ˹as well as˺ Jesus, Job, Jonah, Aaron, and Solomon. And to David We gave the Psalms.” The list is a reunion. Abraham’s house is named on both sides, Ishmael and Isaac together. Jesus is in the list as a recipient of revelation, not as a god and not as a man they killed. Job, Jonah, Aaron, Solomon, and David with the Psalms: gifts they already honour are placed beside the gift they are refusing. The Psalms are named as given, so that a Book in their hands becomes a witness for the idea of giving Books.\n\nThe next verse will add that some messengers were told about, others not, and that Allah spoke to Moses directly. The line is longer than any community’s official shortlist. Humanity has no excuse after messengers. The sending is wider than the stories we happen to know.\n\nAnyone who loves Abraham and still refuses Ishmael’s son as a prophet is being asked to look at the list again. The house is one house. The sending is one sending. The names will not sit still for a partial loyalty.",
  "164": "“There are messengers whose stories We have told you already and others We have not.” The list in the last verse was not a complete census. Some stories have been told. Others have not. This stops two errors. One is to deny a prophet because his story is not in the pages you happen to hold. The other is to invent names and tales and then demand they be treated like revelation. The Quran admits untold messengers and does not fill the silence with folklore.\n\n“And to Moses Allah spoke directly.” Among the told stories, Moses is given a special honour they already know: direct speech. The last chapter used Moses as a measure of later demands. This verse uses him as a measure of honour. Direct speech did not make him a god. It made him a messenger with a weighty proof. If they accept that Allah spoke to Moses, they cannot call the idea of revelation to a man impossible when it reaches Muhammad.\n\nThe next verse will state the purpose of all of them. They were messengers of good news and warnings so that humanity should have no excuse after they came. Allah is Almighty, All-Wise. The long line is not entertainment. It is the closing of a courtroom door: you were not left without a voice from your Lord.\n\nMoses’ honour, then, becomes a bridge, not a wall. The God who spoke to him is the God who sent the later Book with His knowledge. Direct speech and a recited Quran are two forms of one care for humanity.",
  "165": "“˹All were˺ messengers delivering good news and warnings so humanity should have no excuse before Allah after ˹the coming of˺ the messengers.” Every name in the last verses, told or untold, had one job in two parts: good news and warning. Good news of mercy, gardens, and a Lord who forgives. Warning of fire, injustice, and a Day when faces tell the truth. The aim is that humanity should have no excuse. After messengers, a person cannot say, “No one told us.” After this last messenger, a person cannot say, “The line had stopped.”\n\n“And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.” Almighty means He could have forced obedience. He sent news and warnings instead. All-Wise means this method—message, choice, then judgment—is the method He chose. The excuse He wanted closed is the excuse of not being told. The excuse of not liking the messenger remains, and it will not hold.\n\nThe next verse will comfort the Prophet if he is still denied after all this. Allah bears witness to what He sent down. He sent it with His knowledge. The angels bear witness. Allah alone is sufficient as a Witness. A denied prophet is not an unattested prophet. The highest witnesses have already signed.\n\nGood news without warning would leave people playing. Warning without good news would leave people hopeless. The messengers brought both, so that the excuse left to a person is only the excuse of not wanting the truth. After this, a person may still refuse. What he may not honestly say is that no warner and no bringer of good news ever reached the world.",
  "166": "“Yet ˹if you are denied, O Prophet,˺ Allah bears witness to what He has sent down to you—He has sent it with His knowledge.” Denial by people is painful. It is not a lack of witnesses. Allah Himself witnesses the sending. “With His knowledge” means the Book is not a guess thrown into the world. It is sent in the knowledge of the One who knows the plots, the unseen of Mary, the truth about Jesus, the hidden theft, and the Last Day. A book sent with that knowledge cannot be reduced to a man’s opinion.\n\n“The angels too bear witness. And Allah ˹alone˺ is sufficient as a Witness.” Angels are added, then immediately shown to be extra, not needed. Sufficient as a Witness is a phrase this chapter has used for Guardian, Helper, and Trustee. The Prophet who was told he is not a keeper over those who turn away is also told he is not standing in a court without testimony. The highest Witness is enough.\n\nThis should steady him and his community when demands for a writing from heaven continue. The writing they have is already witnessed. The next verse will describe those who still refuse and also block the road. Those who disbelieve and hinder others from the Way of Allah have certainly strayed far away. Denial plus hindering is a farther stray than a private refusal.\n\nWhen a small town denies a prophet, the scene can look like failure. This verse changes the camera. The town is not the jury. Allah and the angels have already witnessed. That is the court that counts.",
  "167": "“Those who disbelieve and hinder ˹others˺ from the Way of Allah have certainly strayed far away.” Disbelief can be a personal closing. This verse adds the public crime already named in the food-passage: hindering others. The person who will not walk the way and also stands in the way of others has gone far, not near. Far away matches the earlier “far astray” of those who associate partners with Allah. Blocking a road to God is not a small social stance.\n\nHindering can be done with weapons, with mockery, with hiding a verse, with a night-plot that frames an innocent man, or with a demand for miracles designed to waste time. All of it is standing in the path.\n\nThe next two verses will complete the threat. Those who disbelieve and wrong themselves—Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to any path except that of Hell, to stay there for ever and ever. That is easy for Allah. Then the address will widen to humanity: the Messenger has come with the truth, so believe for your own good. If you disbelieve, the heavens and the earth are still His. The far stray of the hinderers is not a hole in His kingdom. It is a hole in their own ending.\n\nA private disbeliever may ruin himself. A hinderer tries to ruin a road. The far stray is measured by how many feet he tried to stop, not only by the fact that his own feet halted. The next verses will say there is no path left for such people except Hell if they die on that wronging of themselves. Easy for Allah does not mean light for them.",
  "168": "“Those who disbelieve and wrong themselves—surely Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to any path” continues the last verse about people who disbelieve and hinder others. Now the same kind of person is described as wronging themselves. The two crimes meet in one soul: rejection, and the harm that rejection does to the person who carries it. “Neither forgive them nor guide them to any path” is the severe wording already used for those who increase in disbelief and for those who die on associating partners with Allah. It is the language of an end, not of a living person who still wants to return.\n\n“Any path” is then limited by the next verse: except the path of Hell. So the meaning is not that Allah cannot do something. The meaning is that the only road left for people who die on this disbelief and self-wronging is the road they chose to walk toward.\n\nThe living listener should not steal this verse to despair of himself after a sin. The same chapter still says whoever seeks forgiveness will find Allah All-Forgiving. The people here are those who disbelieve, hinder, wrong themselves, and do not turn. The next verse will make the remaining path explicit: Hell, for ever and ever. That is easy for Allah.\n\nA hinderer who still lives can drop the hindrance, believe, and seek the forgiveness this chapter promised to whoever asks. The closed path is the path of dying on the two crimes, not the path of a soul that wants a third way back to Allah.",
  "169": "“except that of Hell, to stay there for ever and ever.” The “any path” of the previous verse is now named. For those who disbelieve, wrong themselves, and die on that, the remaining path is Hell. “For ever and ever” matches other endings in these chapters for people who meet Allah in chosen rejection. It is not the language used for a believer who sinned and then returned. It is the language of a home that does not become a visit.\n\n“And that is easy for Allah.” Easy does not mean the punishment is a small matter for the people entering it. It means it is not hard for the Almighty to carry out. No ally, no boast of having killed a messenger, no night-plot, and no borrowed honour can make the sentence difficult for Him. The last chapter said a world of gold would not be accepted from those who die as disbelievers. This verse says the path that remains is easy for the One who owns the heavens.\n\nTogether the two verses close the long argument with people who hinder, split the messengers, and refuse the witnessed Book. The next address will leave their closed path and speak to humanity at large. The Messenger has come with the truth, so believe for your own good. If you disbelieve, the kingdom is still His. He is All-Knowing, All-Wise. The easy punishment is not His need. Belief is your good.\n\nEasy for Allah also comforts the oppressed. The people who blocked the way and wronged themselves are not too large for judgment. Their fortress is not a problem for the Owner of the heavens. The next call to humanity will show that His first wish in speech is still, believe for your own good.",
  "170": "“O humanity! The Messenger has certainly come to you with the truth from your Lord, so believe for your own good.” The chapter that began with “O humanity” about a single soul now ends its wide argument with the same address. The Messenger has come. The truth is from your Lord, not from a town’s ambition. “For your own good” is the motive given. Allah does not need the belief. You need it. The last verses said He is Self-Sufficient. This verse says belief is a benefit to the believer.\n\n“But if you disbelieve, then ˹know that˺ to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” Disbelief does not rob the kingdom. It does not leave a hole in the sky. It leaves a hole in the person. All-Knowing means the refusal is seen. All-Wise means the sending of the Messenger, the leaving of choice, and the later gathering are all wise, not weak.\n\nSo the invitation is kind and unsentimental at once. Come for your own good. If you will not, the Owner remains Owner. The next verse will turn from humanity in general to the People of the Book in particular, and will forbid extremes about Jesus: he is a messenger, a word fulfilled through Mary, a spirit created by a command. Do not say “Trinity.” Stop—for your own good. Allah is only One God.\n\nThe single soul at the start of the chapter and the single kingdom at the end are one teaching. You were created from one origin. You are invited by one messenger. You will be gathered by one Lord. Belief is the good that fits that origin.",
  "171": "“O People of the Book! Do not go to extremes regarding your faith; say nothing about Allah except the truth.” Extremes here are the two ditches around Jesus: calling him a liar or a mere target of a boast, and calling him God. The verse demands truth about Allah, not a party line. Truth is what He said, not what later heat added.\n\n“The Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger of Allah and the fulfilment of His Word through Mary and a spirit ˹created by a command˺ from Him.” “No more than a messenger” is honour and a limit. He is Messiah, son of Mary—his mother’s name defending his humanity. He is the fulfilment of Allah’s Word through Mary: the word “Be” that made him, as the last chapter said of Adam. He is a spirit created by a command from Him, not an independent divine person. Every title is a gift. None of them is a throne.\n\n“So believe in Allah and His messengers and do not say, ‘Trinity.’ Stop!—for your own good.” Belief in Allah and His messengers already includes Jesus. Trinity is named in order to be stopped. “For your own good” repeats the last verse’s motive. The stop is not Allah’s need. It is their safety.\n\n“Allah is only One God. Glory be to Him! He is far above having a son! To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And Allah is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs.” Oneness, glory, denial of a son, ownership, and a sufficient Trustee: the same pillars this chapter has used all along. The next verse will say the Messiah would never be too proud to be a servant, nor would the nearest angels. Pride in refusing servanthood is the real fall, not Mary’s son’s humility.",
  "172": "“The Messiah would never be too proud to be a servant of Allah, nor would the angels nearest to Allah.” Jesus is cleared of the honour people tried to force on him. Servanthood is not a demotion he would refuse. It is his glory. The nearest angels are placed beside him so that no one says, “Then the high spirits are gods.” They too are servants. Pride is the thing they do not have. The people who refuse to worship Allah from pride are the ones in danger, not the Messiah who worshipped Him.\n\n“Those who are too proud and arrogant to worship Him will be brought before Him all together.” The gathering returns. Proud people who thought servanthood was beneath them will be brought, not invited. All together means the proud among humans and the proud among jinn, the theologians of excess and the worshippers of their own minds. The Messiah they raised will not stand with their pride. He will stand as a servant and a witness.\n\nThe next verse will split that gathering. Those who believe and do good will be rewarded in full and increased out of grace. Those too proud will be subjected to a painful punishment, and they will find no protector or helper besides Allah. The servant is safe. The one who refused to serve is not.\n\nIf Jesus is not too proud to serve, a scholar, a priest, or a proud pagan has no excuse to treat worship as a lowering. The nearest angels serve. The Messiah serves. Pride is the orphaned choice that belongs to no blessed company.",
  "173": "“As for those who believe and do good, He will reward them in full and increase them out of His grace.” Full reward means nothing is trimmed. Increase out of grace means the payment is larger than the deed. This is the same generosity named after the atom’s weight: He never wrongs, and He multiplies good. The people who accepted Jesus as a messenger, Allah as one, and the last Book as confirmation are not paid with a thin wage. They are increased.\n\n“But those who are too proud and arrogant, He will subject them to a painful punishment. And besides Allah they will find no protector or helper.” Pride was the disease of refusing servanthood. Its end is a punishment they cannot command, and a search for protectors that fails. The Trinity they named will not protect. The angels they may have raised will not protect. Helpers fail when the only Helper is the One they were too proud to serve.\n\nThe contrast is the whole religion in two sentences. Belief and good, then full pay and extra grace. Pride, then pain and no helper. The next verse will call humanity again. Conclusive evidence has come from your Lord, and a brilliant light has been sent down. The proud are not being asked to bow to a darkness. They are refusing a light.\n\nIncrease out of grace also answers those who feared that leaving Trinity would leave them with a smaller religion. They are not being offered less. They are being offered full pay and extra, under a Lord who has no partner and no need.",
  "174": "“O humanity! There has come to you conclusive evidence from your Lord. And We have sent down to you a brilliant light.” The chapter’s last wide call names what has arrived. Conclusive evidence is proof that settles: the Messenger, the Book, the confirmed stories, the exposed plots, the honoured Mary, the raised Messiah, the justice for orphans and women. “From your Lord” keeps the evidence from looking like a man’s project. “A brilliant light” is the Quran itself, and the guidance it carries. Light is not only information. It is what lets a person walk.\n\nHumanity is addressed, not one sect. The evidence is for the idol-worshipper, the Person of the Book, and the hypocrite who prayed to be seen. No one can say the world was left without a lamp.\n\nThe next verse will describe what the light does for people who use it. Those who believe in Allah and hold fast to Him will be admitted into His mercy and grace and guided to Himself through the Straight Path. Holding fast was the hypocrite’s missing act. Here it is the act that turns evidence into a path.\n\nConclusive evidence does not mean every stubborn heart will admit it. It means the proof needed for the gathering has arrived. A person may close his eyes. He may not say the sun was never sent. The brilliant light is already down.\n\nThe next verse will show what to do with the light: believe, hold fast, and be walked to Allah on the Straight Path. Evidence that is not held becomes a proof against the one who saw it and walked away.",
  "175": "“As for those who believe in Allah and hold fast to Him, He will admit them into His mercy and grace and guide them to Himself through the Straight Path.” Belief is joined to holding fast. Holding fast is the opposite of the torn life, the revolving door, and the alliance that hunts honour elsewhere. It is taking Allah as Guardian, Trustee, and Helper in the way this chapter has taught. Admission into mercy and grace is the inner home before the outer garden. Guidance to Himself through the Straight Path means the road does not end at a rule-book. It ends at Him.\n\nThe last chapter closed by telling the believers to endure, persevere, stand guard, and be mindful so they may be successful. This verse names the success: mercy, grace, and a path to Allah. The two endings agree.\n\nThe chapter will not close on that height without one more practical ruling. They ask you for a ruling about those who die without children or parents. Sisters and brothers have shares. Allah makes this clear so you do not go astray. The light that guides to Him also divides an estate fairly when a person dies with no child and no parent. Mercy and inheritance sit in one Book on purpose.\n\nHolding fast is a hand-word. It pictures a person gripping, not glancing. The hypocrites glanced at Allah and gripped other company. These people grip Him, and He admits them, guides them, and walks them to Himself on a straight road.",
  "176": "“They ask you ˹for a ruling, O Prophet˺. Say, ‘Allah gives you a ruling regarding those who die without children or parents.’” The chapter opened with shares for men and women and with a special case of maternal siblings. It closes by answering another question about a person who dies with no child and no parent. “Allah gives you a ruling” keeps the answer in revelation, not in the strongest male in the room.\n\n“If a man dies childless and leaves behind a sister, she will inherit one-half of his estate, whereas her brother will inherit all of her estate if she dies childless. If this person leaves behind two sisters, they together will inherit two-thirds of the estate. But if the deceased leaves male and female siblings, a male’s share will be equal to that of two females.” The sister is not erased. One sister takes half. Two sisters take two-thirds. When brothers and sisters inherit together, the 2:1 share already explained returns, with the same background of financial duty. A brother inheriting all of a childless sister’s estate is stated as the rule of this case, while her right in his estate is a defined half. The point the chapter has made from the beginning remains: women have obligatory shares. They are not guests at the leftover.\n\n“Allah makes ˹this˺ clear to you so you do not go astray. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.” Clarity is to prevent stray in a room where money and grief meet. Perfect knowledge means the shares fit a wisdom we do not fully own, as the earlier verse said you do not fully know who is more beneficial to you. The Book that spoke of Trinity, pride, and a brilliant light also cares who takes half a house. That is part of its proof that it was sent with His knowledge."
};
